10.1371/journal.pone.0294325,dd0a356e6efa9ef9,0,,Effects of group pressure upon the modification and distortion of judgments.,"Asch, Solomon E.",1951,full_text,,correct,argon,"{""target_index"": 0, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""verdict"": ""shipped_right"", ""corrected_value"": null, ""quote"": ""In this study we first replicated the original experiment of Asch [ 1 – 3 ] with 5 confederates and ten line tasks."", ""location"": ""Section 5. Conclusion and discussion (line 169)"", ""reason"": ""Duplicate target: the paper cites Asch (1951, 1955, 1956) [1–3] collectively for the single original Asch line-judgment experiment, which is already captured in shipped row 6 (Asch 1956, matched to target_index 2).""}","{""authors"": ""Asch, Solomon E."", ""year"": ""1951"", ""title"": ""Effects of group pressure upon the modification and distortion of judgments."", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""In this study we first replicated the original experiment of Asch [ 1 – 3 ] with 5 confederates and ten line tasks."", ""outcome"": ""successful"", ""outcome_quote"": ""We find an average error rate of 33% which replicates the original findings of Asch very closely and which is in line with other replications that were conducted predominately with American students [ 12 ]."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": """"}"
10.1371/journal.pone.0294325,dd0a356e6efa9ef9,1,,Opinions and social pressure,"Asch, Solomon E.",1955,full_text,,correct,argon,"{""target_index"": 1, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""verdict"": ""shipped_right"", ""corrected_value"": null, ""quote"": ""In this study we first replicated the original experiment of Asch [ 1 – 3 ] with 5 confederates and ten line tasks."", ""location"": ""Section 5. Conclusion and discussion (line 169)"", ""reason"": ""Duplicate target: the paper cites Asch (1951, 1955, 1956) [1–3] collectively for the single original Asch line-judgment experiment, which is already captured in shipped row 6 (Asch 1956, matched to target_index 2).""}","{""authors"": ""Asch, Solomon E."", ""year"": ""1955"", ""title"": ""Opinions and social pressure"", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""In this study we first replicated the original experiment of Asch [ 1 – 3 ] with 5 confederates and ten line tasks."", ""outcome"": ""successful"", ""outcome_quote"": ""We find an average error rate of 33% which replicates the original findings of Asch very closely and which is in line with other replications that were conducted predominately with American students [ 12 ]."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": """"}"
10.1371/journal.pone.0294325,dd0a356e6efa9ef9,3,,The forgotten variable in conformity research: Impact of task importance on social influence,"Baron, Robert S.; Vandello, Joseph A.; Brunsman, Bethany",1996,full_text,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": ""Baron, Robert S.; Vandello, Joseph A.; Brunsman, Bethany"", ""year"": ""1996"", ""title"": ""The forgotten variable in conformity research: Impact of task importance on social influence"", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""Hence, the studies using incentives yield inconclusive and contradicting results: Particularly, Baron et al. [ 5 ] found both an accuracy-increasing and accuracy-decreasing effect of monetary incentives depending on task difficulty. Bhanot and Williamson [ 6 ] found an increased conformity rate, and Fujita and Mori [ 7 ] found that the conformity bias disappears in the individually incentivized condition. Overall, we follow the economic notion that monetary incentives matter and expect that rewards for nonconformity decrease group pressure (H 2 )."", ""outcome"": ""mixed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""Furthermore, we incentivized the decisions and find a drop of the error rate by 8%-points to 25%. Hence, monetary incentives do not eliminate the effect of group pressure. This finding sheds doubt on former results which predominately show the opposite effect, namely that incentives increase compliance [ 5 ,  6 ]."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": false, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The paper states agreement with the original easy-task reduction but contrasts its result with the original increase in compliance for difficult tasks.""}"
10.1371/journal.pone.0294325,dd0a356e6efa9ef9,4,,Financial incentives and herding: Evidence from two online experiments,"Bhanot, Syon P.; Williamson, Charles",2020,full_text,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": ""Bhanot, Syon P.; Williamson, Charles"", ""year"": ""2020"", ""title"": ""Financial incentives and herding: Evidence from two online experiments"", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""Hence, the studies using incentives yield inconclusive and contradicting results: Particularly, Baron et al. [ 5 ] found both an accuracy-increasing and accuracy-decreasing effect of monetary incentives depending on task difficulty. Bhanot and Williamson [ 6 ] found an increased conformity rate, and Fujita and Mori [ 7 ] found that the conformity bias disappears in the individually incentivized condition. Overall, we follow the economic notion that monetary incentives matter and expect that rewards for nonconformity decrease group pressure (H 2 )."", ""outcome"": ""failed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""Furthermore, we incentivized the decisions and find a drop of the error rate by 8%-points to 25%. Hence, monetary incentives do not eliminate the effect of group pressure. This finding sheds doubt on former results which predominately show the opposite effect, namely that incentives increase compliance [ 5 ,  6 ]."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Incentives decrease conformity, explicitly opposite to this target.""}"
10.1371/journal.pone.0294325,dd0a356e6efa9ef9,5,,Group versus individual reward in the Asch experiment without confederates,"Fujita, Yuki; Mori, Kazuo",2017,full_text,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": ""Fujita, Yuki; Mori, Kazuo"", ""year"": ""2017"", ""title"": ""Group versus individual reward in the Asch experiment without confederates"", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""Hence, the studies using incentives yield inconclusive and contradicting results: Particularly, Baron et al. [ 5 ] found both an accuracy-increasing and accuracy-decreasing effect of monetary incentives depending on task difficulty. Bhanot and Williamson [ 6 ] found an increased conformity rate, and Fujita and Mori [ 7 ] found that the conformity bias disappears in the individually incentivized condition. Overall, we follow the economic notion that monetary incentives matter and expect that rewards for nonconformity decrease group pressure (H 2 )."", ""outcome"": ""failed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""Furthermore, we incentivized the decisions and find a drop of the error rate by 8%-points to 25%. Hence, monetary incentives do not eliminate the effect of group pressure. This finding sheds doubt on former results which predominately show the opposite effect, namely that incentives increase compliance [ 5 ,  6 ]."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Original conformity disappeared under individual incentives; here it remains at 25%.""}"
10.1371/journal.pone.0294325,dd0a356e6efa9ef9,6,,Conformity and Character,"Crutchfield, Richard S.",1955,full_text,,error,argon,"{""target_index"": 6, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""verdict"": ""blind_right"", ""corrected_value"": {""record_type"": ""replication"", ""authors"": ""Crutchfield, Richard S."", ""year"": ""1955"", ""title"": ""Conformity and Character"", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""outcome"": ""mixed""}, ""quote"": ""We randomized the groups’ response to five different political statements and find an average conformity rate of 38%. Hence, these results suggest that the original finding of Asch can also be generalized to matters of opinion. This result is in line with former evidence by Crutchfield [ 8 ], and Mallinson and Hatemi [ 9 ]. However, both of these studies had only small sample sizes of 50 and 58 subjects respectively, which called for further replication studies. Finally, we measured the Big Five, intelligence, self-esteem and social approval. With the exception of openness, our study finds no support that these personality traits are statistically significantly related to the susceptibility of group pressure."", ""location"": ""Section 5. Conclusion and discussion (line 170)"", ""reason"": ""Although framed as an extension relative to Asch's line task, the paper directly re-tests Crutchfield's (1955) published findings on political-opinion conformity (H3, derived in Section 2.2 from Crutchfield's 36-40 percentage-point effects with n=50) and individual-difference correlates of conformity (intellectual competence, self-esteem/ego strength, and need for social approval in Section 2.3, H4-H6) in a larger sample (N=202). The Conclusion explicitly states that Crutchfield's small sample size called for further replication studies and confirms the political-opinion conformity effect (38%), while finding no overall support for the intelligence, self-esteem, and social-approval associations, yielding a mixed outcome.""}","{""authors"": ""Crutchfield, Richard S."", ""year"": ""1955"", ""title"": ""Conformity and Character"", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""However, both of these studies had only small sample sizes of 50 and 58 subjects respectively, which called for further replication studies."", ""outcome"": ""mixed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""Moreover, our study suggests that group pressure is not only influential in the simple line task but also when it comes to political opinions. We randomized the groups’ response to five different political statements and find an average conformity rate of 38%. Hence, these results suggest that the original finding of Asch can also be generalized to matters of opinion. This result is in line with former evidence by Crutchfield [ 8 ], and Mallinson and Hatemi [ 9 ]."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": false, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Political conformity agrees, but this paper also re-tests the intellectual competence, self-esteem and approval associations attributed to Crutchfield and reports no support for those traits overall.""}"
10.1371/journal.pone.0294325,dd0a356e6efa9ef9,7,10.1371/journal.pone.0196600,The effects of information and social conformity on opinion change,"Mallinson, Daniel J.; Hatemi, Peter K.",2018,full_text,,error,argon,"{""target_index"": 7, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""verdict"": ""blind_right"", ""corrected_value"": {""record_type"": ""replication"", ""authors"": ""Mallinson, Daniel J.; Hatemi, Peter K."", ""year"": ""2018"", ""title"": ""The effects of information and social conformity on opinion change"", ""doi"": ""10.1371/journal.pone.0196600"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""outcome"": ""successful""}, ""quote"": ""We randomized the groups’ response to five different political statements and find an average conformity rate of 38%. Hence, these results suggest that the original finding of Asch can also be generalized to matters of opinion. This result is in line with former evidence by Crutchfield [ 8 ], and Mallinson and Hatemi [ 9 ]. However, both of these studies had only small sample sizes of 50 and 58 subjects respectively, which called for further replication studies."", ""location"": ""Section 5. Conclusion and discussion (line 170)"", ""reason"": ""The paper does not merely cite Mallinson and Hatemi (2018) as background motivation: Section 2.2 reviews their specific experimental finding (38% vs. 8% political opinion change under social pressure, n=58) to derive H3, Part 2 of the experiment tests political-opinion conformity under randomized unanimous confederate responses in a larger sample (N=202), and the Conclusion explicitly compares the resulting 38% conformity rate as confirming Mallinson and Hatemi (2018) while noting that their small sample size (n=58) called for further replication studies.""}","{""authors"": ""Mallinson, Daniel J.; Hatemi, Peter K."", ""year"": ""2018"", ""title"": ""The effects of information and social conformity on opinion change"", ""doi"": ""10.1371/journal.pone.0196600"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""However, both of these studies had only small sample sizes of 50 and 58 subjects respectively, which called for further replication studies."", ""outcome"": ""successful"", ""outcome_quote"": ""Moreover, our study suggests that group pressure is not only influential in the simple line task but also when it comes to political opinions. We randomized the groups’ response to five different political statements and find an average conformity rate of 38%. Hence, these results suggest that the original finding of Asch can also be generalized to matters of opinion. This result is in line with former evidence by Crutchfield [ 8 ], and Mallinson and Hatemi [ 9 ]."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": """"}"
10.1371/journal.pone.0294325,dd0a356e6efa9ef9,8,,"Age, intelligence, and sex as variables in the conformity behaviour of Negro and white children","Iscoe, Ira; Williams, Martha; Harvey, Jerry",1964,full_text,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": ""Iscoe, Ira; Williams, Martha; Harvey, Jerry"", ""year"": ""1964"", ""title"": ""Age, intelligence, and sex as variables in the conformity behaviour of Negro and white children"", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""Summarizing, we expect to find a positive association between social approval and conformity (H 4 ), and negative associations for intelligence (H 5 ) and self-esteem (H 6 )."", ""outcome"": ""successful"", ""outcome_quote"": ""Finally, we measured the Big Five, intelligence, self-esteem and social approval. With the exception of openness, our study finds no support that these personality traits are statistically significantly related to the susceptibility of group pressure."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": false, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Original null association of intelligence proxies with conformity agrees with this paper’s null.""}"
