10.1037/edu0000146,701e7e90a9964039,1,10.1037/a0019506,Enhancing interest and performance with a utility value intervention.,"Hulleman, C. S., Godes, O., Hendricks, B. L., & Harackiewicz, J. M.",2010,full_text,,error,argon,"{""target_index"": 1, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""verdict"": ""blind_right"", ""corrected_value"": {""doi"": ""10.1037/a0019506"", ""title"": ""Enhancing interest and performance with a utility value intervention."", ""authors"": ""Hulleman, C. S., Godes, O., Hendricks, B. L., & Harackiewicz, J. M."", ""year"": ""2010"", ""type"": ""replication"", ""outcome"": ""mixed""}, ""quote"": ""The utility value interventions utilized in the current studies were based on the self-generated utility value interventions used by Hulleman and colleagues (e.g., Hulleman et al., 2010; Hulleman & Harackiewicz, 2009). In addition to replicating this prior research, we extend it in five ways."", ""location"": ""introduction (Current Studies, lines 517-524)"", ""reason"": ""Omitted target: Hulleman et al. (2010) (DOI: 10.1037/a0019506) is an independent original paper explicitly cited throughout the introduction, methods, and discussion alongside Hulleman & Harackiewicz (2009) as prior utility value intervention research replicated in Study 2, and shares the same 'mixed' partial-replication verdict in the Study 2 Discussion.""}","{""authors"": ""Hulleman, C. S., Godes, O., Hendricks, B. L., & Harackiewicz, J. M."", ""year"": ""2010"", ""title"": ""Enhancing interest and performance with a utility value intervention."", ""doi"": ""10.1037/a0019506"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": ""2"", ""replication_study_numbers"": ""2"", ""evidence_quote"": ""Although the research generated by the expectancy-value framework has been largely correlational (Wigfield & Cambria, 2010), recent classroom studies reveal that interventions designed to enhance perceptions of value can increase both interest and course perfor- mance (e.g., Hulleman & Harackiewicz, 2009; Hulleman, Godes, Hendricks, & Harackiewicz, 2010). The research presented herein replicates and extends this prior work by further investigating a theory-based intervention designed to enhance student motivation and performance."", ""outcome"": ""mixed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""The results of Study 2 partially replicated prior research dem- onstrating that a theoretically guided intervention based on the expectancy-value framework could enhance student learning out- comes (Hulleman & Harackiewicz, 2009; Hulleman et al., 2010)."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Authors explicitly give a partial-replication verdict over both cited prior intervention papers; interest/performance benefits coexist with an unexpected mechanism.""}"
10.1037/edu0000095,75424acd1af13f1d,0,,,,,abstract,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": """", ""year"": """", ""title"": """", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": """", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""This study reports 2 years of findings from a randomized controlled trial designed to replicate and demonstrate the efficacy of an existing, successful professional development program, the Pathway Project, that uses a cognitive strategies approach to text-based analytical writing. Building on an earlier randomized field trial in a large, urban, low socioeconomic status (SES) district in which 98% of the students were Latino and 88% were mainstreamed English learners (ELs) at the intermediate level of fluency, the project aimed to help secondary school students, specifically Latinos and mainstreamed ELs, in another large, urban, low-SES district to develop the academic writing skills called for in the rigorous Common Core State Standards for English Language Arts."", ""outcome"": ""successful"", ""outcome_quote"": ""Difference-in-differences and regression analyses revealed significant effects on student writing outcomes in both years of the intervention (Year 1, d = 0.48; Year 2, d = 0.60). Additionally, Pathway students had higher odds than control students of passing the California High School Exit Exam in both years. (PsycINFO Database Record (c) 2017 APA, all rights reserved)"", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""abstract"", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Abstract identifies an existing successful Pathway intervention, then reports positive effects on the writing outcome it was designed to replicate. Original citation not available in abstract.""}"
10.1002/dev.21515,53850ba7cc0a14c2,0,,,,,abstract,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": """", ""year"": """", ""title"": """", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": """", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": ""1"", ""evidence_quote"": ""In Study 1 ( n = 32), we replicated previous findings showing an asymmetrical pattern of categorization of happy and fearful faces in 6.5‐month‐old infants, and extended these findings by demonstrating that infants’ categorization did not differ when emotions were expressed by familiar (i.e., caregiver) faces."", ""outcome"": ""successful"", ""outcome_quote"": ""In Study 1 ( n = 32), we replicated previous findings showing an asymmetrical pattern of categorization of happy and fearful faces in 6.5‐month‐old infants, and extended these findings by demonstrating that infants’ categorization did not differ when emotions were expressed by familiar (i.e., caregiver) faces."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""abstract"", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The abstract expressly states that this study replicated the prior finding; the original citation is not named in the available evidence.""}"
