10.1017/s0003055422000946,a0c4032c0bd9530a,0,,Self-Censorship of Regime Support in Authoritarian States: Evidence from List Experiments in China.,"Robinson, Darrel, and Tannenberg, Marcus",2019,abstract,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": ""Robinson, Darrel, and Tannenberg, Marcus"", ""year"": ""2019"", ""title"": ""Self-Censorship of Regime Support in Authoritarian States: Evidence from List Experiments in China."", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""description"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""We replicated previous list experiment results confirming that a majority trusts the central government despite overreports."", ""outcome"": ""successful"", ""outcome_quote"": ""We replicated previous list experiment results confirming that a majority trusts the central government despite overreports."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""abstract"", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The abstract explicitly says previous list-experiment findings were replicated. This supports the described target; novel local-government and term-limit studies are not additional targets.""}"
10.3758/s13428-023-02329-5,49c906aa1fa610be,1,10.1037/xge0000037,When goals conflict with values: Counterproductive attentional and oculomotor capture by reward-related stimuli.,"Le Pelley, M. E., Pearson, D., Griffiths, O., & Beesley, T.",2015,full_text,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": ""Le Pelley, M. E., Pearson, D., Griffiths, O., & Beesley, T."", ""year"": ""2015"", ""title"": ""When goals conflict with values: Counterproductive attentional and oculomotor capture by reward-related stimuli."", ""doi"": ""10.1037/xge0000037"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""The first aim of the present study was to replicate and extend the results of Watson et al. (\n2019a\n), which demonstrated that the VMAC effect could be observed in conditions where a high reward predicting singleton had always been response irrelevant and held no informational value. In our study, we showed that the VMAC effect increased over time in the rewarded phase, that is, we observed a progressive increase in RTs when the high valued singleton acted as singleton distractor compared to the low valued singleton. Furthermore, when reward feedback was omitted in the unrewarded phase, the VMAC effect remained constant, without any evidence of reduction over trials. These two results replicate previous findings with Le Pelley’s paradigm (Le Pelley et al.,\n2015\n), where the VMAC effect emerged over trials, even when the reward-signals are always task irrelevant, and also replicate Watson et al. (\n2019a\n), who showed that once established, the VMAC effect remains present regardless of the current informational value of the reward-related stimuli."", ""outcome"": ""successful"", ""outcome_quote"": ""The first aim of the present study was to replicate and extend the results of Watson et al. (\n2019a\n), which demonstrated that the VMAC effect could be observed in conditions where a high reward predicting singleton had always been response irrelevant and held no informational value. In our study, we showed that the VMAC effect increased over time in the rewarded phase, that is, we observed a progressive increase in RTs when the high valued singleton acted as singleton distractor compared to the low valued singleton. Furthermore, when reward feedback was omitted in the unrewarded phase, the VMAC effect remained constant, without any evidence of reduction over trials. These two results replicate previous findings with Le Pelley’s paradigm (Le Pelley et al.,\n2015\n), where the VMAC effect emerged over trials, even when the reward-signals are always task irrelevant, and also replicate Watson et al. (\n2019a\n), who showed that once established, the VMAC effect remains present regardless of the current informational value of the reward-related stimuli."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The authors explicitly state that their new-sample results replicate this finding.""}"
10.1027/1864-9335/a000188,fe0e494e6758464b,1,,,Gong and Medin,2012,abstract,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": ""Gong and Medin"", ""year"": ""2012"", ""title"": """", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""Eyal, Liberman, and Trope (2008) established that people judged moral transgressions more harshly and virtuous acts more positively when the acts were psychologically distant than close. In a series of conceptual and direct replications, Gong and Medin (2012) came to the opposite conclusion. Attempting to resolve these inconsistencies, we conducted four high-powered replication studies in which we varied temporal distance (Studies 1 and 3), social distance (Study 2) or construal level (Study 4), and registered their impact on moral judgment."", ""outcome"": ""mixed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""Eyal, Liberman, and Trope (2008) established that people judged moral transgressions more harshly and virtuous acts more positively when the acts were psychologically distant than close. In a series of conceptual and direct replications, Gong and Medin (2012) came to the opposite conclusion. Attempting to resolve these inconsistencies, we conducted four high-powered replication studies in which we varied temporal distance (Studies 1 and 3), social distance (Study 2) or construal level (Study 4), and registered their impact on moral judgment. We found no systematic effect of temporal distance, the effect of social distance consistent with Eyal et al., and the reversed effect of direct construal level manipulation, consistent with Gong and Medin. Possible explanations for the incompatible results are discussed."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""abstract"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The construal effect agrees with Gong and Medin, while the social distance result agrees with their stated opposite Eyal finding. The abstract does not map original study numbers.""}"
10.1097/j.pain.0000000000000657,1f0d03bc2043e5b8,1,,Pharmacological characterization of intraplantar Complete Freund's Adjuvant-induced burrowing deficits.,"Gould SA, Doods H, Lamla T, Pekcec A.",2016,full_text,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": ""Gould SA, Doods H, Lamla T, Pekcec A."", ""year"": ""2016"", ""title"": ""Pharmacological characterization of intraplantar Complete Freund's Adjuvant-induced burrowing deficits."", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""Although burrowing is a relatively novel pain outcome measure, a few studies have reported decreased burrowing in rat models of inflammatory and neuropathic pain; importantly, burrowing deficits were reversed by known analgesics, suggesting a degree of predictive validity for this outcome measure.2,9,20,35,61,62"", ""outcome"": ""mixed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""Overall, reduced burrowing was partially replicated at 6 of the 8 participating centres with an element of variability between and within centres."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The authors give an overall partial replication verdict for CFA-associated suppressed burrowing across centres.""}"
10.1177/20531680231205157,6d33998f7d1c5c95,1,10.1257/aer.20191435,The welfare effects of social media,"Allcott H, Braghieri L, Eichmeyer S, et al.",2020,full_text,,correct,argon,"{""target_index"": 1, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""verdict"": ""shipped_right"", ""corrected_value"": null, ""quote"": ""In addition to following the same process as in the original study (Asimovic et al., 2021), we are encouraged by the fact that we were able to replicate secondary findings from the previous study in terms of deactivation reducing users’ anxiety."", ""location"": ""discussion (lines 465-469); introduction (lines 88-96, 135-139)"", ""reason"": ""Wrong original / not an independent target under the replication-chain rule. The paper explicitly identifies Asimovic et al. (2021) in the singular as 'the original study' and describes reduced anxiety and reduced news knowledge as 'secondary outcomes from the original study' (lines 135-136) and 'secondary findings from the previous study' (line 468) that Asimovic et al. (2021) had already tested following Allcott et al. (2020) (lines 88-93, 389-392). Under the codebook rule on replication chains, the target is the study closest to this paper in the chain that it actually re-tests (Asimovic et al., 2021), not the earlier U.S. deactivation study (Allcott et al., 2020).""}","{""authors"": ""Allcott H, Braghieri L, Eichmeyer S, et al."", ""year"": ""2020"", ""title"": ""The welfare effects of social media"", ""doi"": ""10.1257/aer.20191435"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""We also find support for recent findings (Allcott et al., 2020; Asimovic et al., 2021) that Facebook deactivation leads to a reduction in anxiety levels and suggestive evidence of a reduction in knowledge of current news, though the latter is again limited to our 2021 subsample."", ""outcome"": ""mixed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""We also find support for recent findings (Allcott et al., 2020; Asimovic et al., 2021) that Facebook deactivation leads to a reduction in anxiety levels and suggestive evidence of a reduction in knowledge of current news, though the latter is again limited to our 2021 subsample."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""abstract"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Anxiety agrees, but news knowledge agrees only in 2021; the pooled and 2020 news effects are absent. These secondary findings are explicitly preregistered replication targets.""}"
10.3233/jad-2010-1357,5b3bc0521d2f9aa1,1,,No association between CALHM1 and Alzheimer’s disease risk.,"Bertram L, Schjeide BM, Hooli B, Mullin K, Hiltunen M, Soininen H, Ingelsson M, Lannfelt L, Blacker D, Tanzi RE",2008,full_text,competing_supported_matches,correct,argon,"{""target_index"": 1, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""verdict"": ""shipped_right"", ""corrected_value"": null, ""quote"": ""Unfortunately, original ﬁndings, obtained in ﬁve different case-control series, were not replicated in a large-scale study of familial AD [2] and in six additional studies conducted in AD patients and controls from different populations (Sweden [2], Finland [2], Belgium [3], Caucasian Americans [4,5], and Asians [6])."", ""location"": ""introduction"", ""reason"": ""Wrong original / non-target prior replication study. Reference [2] (Bertram et al., 2008, including the original authors' reply in Cell 135: 993-996) is explicitly introduced as an earlier replication study of the original findings [1] and included in the meta-analysis; its mention alongside [1, 4, 11] in Results serves to motivate testing [1]'s P86L variant under a recessive model for age at onset rather than establishing an independent replication target omitted by the shipped data.""}","{""authors"": ""Bertram L, Schjeide BM, Hooli B, Mullin K, Hiltunen M, Soininen H, Ingelsson M, Lannfelt L, Blacker D, Tanzi RE"", ""year"": ""2008"", ""title"": ""No association between CALHM1 and Alzheimer’s disease risk."", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""Several independent research groups have observed\nthat P86L and other CALMH1 markers modulate the\n\nAAO only under a recessive model [1,2,4,11]. These\n\nobservations reinforce a hypothetical recessive model\n\nfor the P86L variant in AD. So, we decided to evalu-\n\nate this model in AAO in Spanish AD patients."", ""outcome"": ""successful"", ""outcome_quote"": ""We have provided new evidence for the role of the\n\nCALHM1P86L marker in ADrisk. Infact,wehaveob-\n\nserved a trend toward association in case-control stud-\n\nies and a full independent replication of the P86L ef-\n\nfect in AAO. Intriguingly, both results are evidentonly\n\nwhen EOAD patients are eliminated from the pool and\n\nwhen a recessive model is applied to analyses."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Outcome follows the explicit comparisons; the disease-risk nulls of prior replication papers are not themselves the age-at-onset claims re-tested here.""}"
10.3233/jad-2010-1357,5b3bc0521d2f9aa1,2,,No association between CALHM1 variation and risk of Alzheimer disease.,"Minster RL, Demirci FY, DeKosky ST, Kamboh MI",2009,full_text,,correct,argon,"{""target_index"": 2, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""verdict"": ""shipped_right"", ""corrected_value"": null, ""quote"": ""Unfortunately, original ﬁndings, obtained in ﬁve different case-control series, were not replicated in a large-scale study of familial AD [2] and in six additional studies conducted in AD patients and controls from different populations (Sweden [2], Finland [2], Belgium [3], Caucasian Americans [4,5], and Asians [6])."", ""location"": ""introduction"", ""reason"": ""Wrong original / non-target prior replication study. Reference [4] (Minster et al., 2009) is explicitly introduced as one of the prior negative replication studies of the original findings [1] and is cited alongside [1, 2, 11] only as supporting the hypothetical recessive model for P86L in age at onset; per the codebook, an earlier replication attempt cited as background/context for model specification is not a separate target.""}","{""authors"": ""Minster RL, Demirci FY, DeKosky ST, Kamboh MI"", ""year"": ""2009"", ""title"": ""No association between CALHM1 variation and risk of Alzheimer disease."", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""Several independent research groups have observed\nthat P86L and other CALMH1 markers modulate the\n\nAAO only under a recessive model [1,2,4,11]. These\n\nobservations reinforce a hypothetical recessive model\n\nfor the P86L variant in AD. So, we decided to evalu-\n\nate this model in AAO in Spanish AD patients."", ""outcome"": ""successful"", ""outcome_quote"": ""We have provided new evidence for the role of the\n\nCALHM1P86L marker in ADrisk. Infact,wehaveob-\n\nserved a trend toward association in case-control stud-\n\nies and a full independent replication of the P86L ef-\n\nfect in AAO. Intriguingly, both results are evidentonly\n\nwhen EOAD patients are eliminated from the pool and\n\nwhen a recessive model is applied to analyses."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Outcome follows the explicit comparisons; the disease-risk nulls of prior replication papers are not themselves the age-at-onset claims re-tested here.""}"
10.3233/jad-2010-1357,5b3bc0521d2f9aa1,3,,Candidate single-nucleotide polymorphisms from a genomewide association study of Alzheimer disease.,"Li H, Wetten S, Li L, St Jean PL, Upmanyu R, Surh L",2008,full_text,,correct,argon,"{""target_index"": 3, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""verdict"": ""shipped_right"", ""corrected_value"": null, ""quote"": ""Several independent research groups have observed that P86L and other CALMH1 markers modulate the AAO only under a recessive model [1,2,4,11]. These observations reinforce a hypothetical recessive model for the P86L variant in AD. So, we decided to evaluate this model in AAO in Spanish AD patients."", ""location"": ""results"", ""reason"": ""Wrong original / background citation. Reference [11] (Li et al., 2008) is cited only once in passing as part of the background observation that 'P86L and other CALMH1 markers modulate the AAO only under a recessive model [1,2,4,11]' to motivate evaluating the recessive model for P86L in the authors' replication of [1]; the present paper only genotyped P86L and P85P and did not re-test Li et al.'s GWAS candidate SNPs.""}","{""authors"": ""Li H, Wetten S, Li L, St Jean PL, Upmanyu R, Surh L"", ""year"": ""2008"", ""title"": ""Candidate single-nucleotide polymorphisms from a genomewide association study of Alzheimer disease."", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""Several independent research groups have observed\nthat P86L and other CALMH1 markers modulate the\n\nAAO only under a recessive model [1,2,4,11]. These\n\nobservations reinforce a hypothetical recessive model\n\nfor the P86L variant in AD. So, we decided to evalu-\n\nate this model in AAO in Spanish AD patients."", ""outcome"": ""successful"", ""outcome_quote"": ""We have provided new evidence for the role of the\n\nCALHM1P86L marker in ADrisk. Infact,wehaveob-\n\nserved a trend toward association in case-control stud-\n\nies and a full independent replication of the P86L ef-\n\nfect in AAO. Intriguingly, both results are evidentonly\n\nwhen EOAD patients are eliminated from the pool and\n\nwhen a recessive model is applied to analyses."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": false, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""New Spanish sample tests the published CALHM1 association and age-at-onset findings. The dominant versus recessive contrast makes the first target mixed; age-at-onset replication supports the remaining targets. Li includes other CALHM1 markers, leaving some target specificity uncertainty.""}"
10.1002/pits.22106,a9fa5691229969ab,0,,,,,abstract,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": """", ""year"": """", ""title"": """", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": """", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""In this study, we replicated a targeted professional development approach that included a brief one‐on‐one training session and emailed visual performance feedback to increase novice teachers’ use of behavior‐specific praise, an evidence‐based classroom management skill."", ""outcome"": ""cannot_be_determined"", ""outcome_quote"": """", ""outcome_quote_section"": """", ""outcome_confident"": false, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Abstract reports the new intervention results but neither names the earlier paper nor states its finding sufficiently to compare outcomes. Do not infer mixed from small effects or variable teacher response.""}"
10.3233/jad-2011-101932,c0b98991411cfedb,1,10.1001/archneurol.2010.201,"Meta-analysis Confirms CR1, CLU, and PICALM as Alzheimer Disease Risk Loci and Reveals Interactions With APOE Genotypes","Jun G, Naj AC, Beecham GW, Wang LS, Buros J, Gallins PJ, Buxbaum JD, Ertekin-Taner N, Fallin MD, Friedland R, Inzelberg R, Kramer P, Rogaeva E, St George-Hyslop P, Arnold SE, Baldwin CT, Barber R, Beach T, Bigio EH, Bird TD, Boxer A, Burke JR, Cairns N, Carroll SL, Chui HC, Clark DG, Cotman CW, Cummings JL, Decarli C, Diaz-Arrastia R, Dick M, Dickson DW, Ellis WG, Fallon KB, Farlow MR, Ferris S, Frosch MP, Galasko DR, Gearing M, Geschwind DH, Ghetti B, Gilman S, Giordani B, Glass J, Graff-Radford NR, Green RC, Growdon JH, Hamilton RL, Harrell LE, Head E, Honig LS, Hulette CM, Hyman BT, Jicha GA, Jin LW, Johnson N, Karlawish J, Karydas A, Kaye JA, Kim R, Koo EH, Kowall NW, Lah JJ, Levey AI, Lieberman A, Lopez OL, Mack WJ, Markesbery W, Marson DC, Martiniuk F, Masliah E, McKee AC, Mesulam M, Miller JW, Miller BL, Miller CA, Parisi JE, Perl DP, Peskind E, Petersen RC, Poon W, Quinn JF, Raskind M, Reisberg B, Ringman JM, Roberson ED, Rosenberg RN, Sano M, Schneider JA, Schneider LS, Seeley W, Shelanski ML, Smith CD, Spina S, Stern RA, Tanzi RE, Trojanowski JQ, Troncoso JC, Van Deerlin VM, Vinters HV, Vonsattel JP, Weintraub S, Welsh-Bohmer KA, Woltjer RL, Younkin SG, Cantwell LB, Dombroski BA, Saykin AJ, Reiman EM, Bennett DA, Morris JC, Lunetta KL, Martin ER, Montine TJ, Goate AM, Blacker D, Tsuang DW, Beekly D, Cupples LA, Hakonarson H, Kukull W, Foroud TM, Haines J, Mayeux R, Farrer LA, Pericak-Vance MA, Schellenberg GD",2010,full_text,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": ""Jun G, Naj AC, Beecham GW, Wang LS, Buros J, Gallins PJ, Buxbaum JD, Ertekin-Taner N, Fallin MD, Friedland R, Inzelberg R, Kramer P, Rogaeva E, St George-Hyslop P, Arnold SE, Baldwin CT, Barber R, Beach T, Bigio EH, Bird TD, Boxer A, Burke JR, Cairns N, Carroll SL, Chui HC, Clark DG, Cotman CW, Cummings JL, Decarli C, Diaz-Arrastia R, Dick M, Dickson DW, Ellis WG, Fallon KB, Farlow MR, Ferris S, Frosch MP, Galasko DR, Gearing M, Geschwind DH, Ghetti B, Gilman S, Giordani B, Glass J, Graff-Radford NR, Green RC, Growdon JH, Hamilton RL, Harrell LE, Head E, Honig LS, Hulette CM, Hyman BT, Jicha GA, Jin LW, Johnson N, Karlawish J, Karydas A, Kaye JA, Kim R, Koo EH, Kowall NW, Lah JJ, Levey AI, Lieberman A, Lopez OL, Mack WJ, Markesbery W, Marson DC, Martiniuk F, Masliah E, McKee AC, Mesulam M, Miller JW, Miller BL, Miller CA, Parisi JE, Perl DP, Peskind E, Petersen RC, Poon W, Quinn JF, Raskind M, Reisberg B, Ringman JM, Roberson ED, Rosenberg RN, Sano M, Schneider JA, Schneider LS, Seeley W, Shelanski ML, Smith CD, Spina S, Stern RA, Tanzi RE, Trojanowski JQ, Troncoso JC, Van Deerlin VM, Vinters HV, Vonsattel JP, Weintraub S, Welsh-Bohmer KA, Woltjer RL, Younkin SG, Cantwell LB, Dombroski BA, Saykin AJ, Reiman EM, Bennett DA, Morris JC, Lunetta KL, Martin ER, Montine TJ, Goate AM, Blacker D, Tsuang DW, Beekly D, Cupples LA, Hakonarson H, Kukull W, Foroud TM, Haines J, Mayeux R, Farrer LA, Pericak-Vance MA, Schellenberg GD"", ""year"": ""2010"", ""title"": ""Meta-analysis Confirms CR1, CLU, and PICALM as Alzheimer Disease Risk Loci and Reveals Interactions With APOE Genotypes"", ""doi"": ""10.1001/archneurol.2010.201"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""In a recently published article an interaction between PICALM (rs3851179) and APOEε4 was reported (interaction OR=0.84, unadjusted p=0.0068), but not between CR1 (rs3818361) and APOEε4 (interaction OR=1.01, unadjusted p=0.28) [16]."", ""outcome"": ""failed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""The only nominally significant epistatic interactions reported to date are with the powerful APOE ε4 allele; the CR1 * APOEε4 interaction reported here and the PICALM * APOEε4 interaction reported previously. These two interactions were both weak and neither replicated."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true}"
10.1109/esem.2011.30,530fcf3dacadf57b,0,,,Zimmermann and Nagappan,,abstract,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": ""Zimmermann and Nagappan"", ""year"": """", ""title"": """", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""This paper presents a replication of one such study conducted by Zimmermann and Nagappan on Windows Server 2003 where the authors leveraged dependency relationships between software entities captured using social network metrics to predict whether they are likely to have defects."", ""outcome"": ""mixed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""Our results are in agreement with the original study by Zimmermann and Nagappan when using a similar experimental setup as them (random sampling). However, when we evaluated the metrics using setups more suited for industrial use -- forward-release and cross-project prediction -- we found network metrics to offer no vantage over code metrics."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""abstract"", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The same experimental setup agrees with the original; forward-release and cross-project tests do not retain the original advantage of network metrics. Abstract lacks full target citation.""}"
10.18148/srm/2012.v6i1.5092,b77773abf87cb45c,0,,Do we have to combine values in the Schwartz’ human values scale? A comment on the Davidov studies.,"Knoppen, D., & Saris, W.",2009,full_text,same_author_year_siblings,correct,argon,"{""target_index"": 0, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""verdict"": ""shipped_right"", ""corrected_value"": null, ""quote"": ""Finally, we compared the outcomes of our analysis with those reported by Knoppen and Saris (2009b)."", ""location"": ""methods"", ""reason"": ""Although the paper cites Knoppen and Saris (2009a,b) jointly in headings and general discussion when referring to their student-sample research, the title frames the study as a replication of a single work ('Knoppen and Saris (2009)') and Section 3.3 explicitly states that Study 1 followed the three-step procedure of and compared its analysis outcomes with those reported by Knoppen and Saris (2009b). Every specific empirical comparison in Results (Section 3.4) and Discussion (Section 3.5) is cited exclusively to 2009b, with no separate findings from 2009a independently tested or compared. Thus target 0 (2009a) is a companion citation rather than a separate omitted replication target.""}","{""year"": ""2009"", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": ""1"", ""evidence_quote"": ""Study 1 examines whether the Knoppen and Saris (2009a,b) findings generalize to a representative sample of the German population."", ""outcome"": ""mixed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""Study 1 followed the procedures of Knoppen and Saris (2009a,b) to investigate the measurement models of the ten values as measured by the 40-item PVQ. Our findings in a representative German national sample largely support those that Knoppen and Saris reported for homogeneous samples of German students. But there were notable exceptions, too."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Authors explicitly characterize support with notable exceptions; detailed results include replicated sub-dimensions and cross-loadings alongside contradictory conformity and tradition results."", ""authors"": ""Knoppen, D., & Saris, W."", ""title"": ""Do we have to combine values in the Schwartz’ human values scale? A comment on the Davidov studies.""}"
10.1111/j.1365-2265.2011.04175.x,f38bdcc69a2f8ac6,0,,,,,abstract,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": """", ""year"": """", ""title"": """", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": """", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""A recent genome-wide association study for type 2 diabetes in Han Chinese identified several novel genetic variants. We investigated their associations with quantitative measures to explore the mechanism by which these variants influence glucose homoeostasis."", ""outcome"": ""cannot_be_determined"", ""outcome_quote"": """", ""outcome_quote_section"": """", ""outcome_confident"": false, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The abstract reports associations but does not give the original study citation or a verdict comparing all retested signals to its findings.""}"
10.1016/j.rasd.2014.01.006,f05d51a3482f3343,0,,,,,abstract,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": """", ""year"": """", ""title"": """", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": """", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""We investigated visual scanning behaviour in children with ASD during an EFT in an attempt replicating a previous study examining differences in visual search behaviour."", ""outcome"": ""cannot_be_determined"", ""outcome_quote"": """", ""outcome_quote_section"": """", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": false, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Abstract gives the present results but does not state the original result or identify its citation; comparison cannot be established.""}"
10.1080/22243534.2015.11828344,1c44b19bafd0d2c9,0,,,Hofstede,,abstract,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": ""Hofstede"", ""year"": """", ""title"": """", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": """", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""In the present study the cultural dimensions of the well known Dutch researcher Geert Hofstede were tested on a sample of international business students."", ""outcome"": ""mixed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""The findings of the present research contest many of Hofstede's original findings. The dimensions power distance and long-term orientation show significant differences with Hofstede's original country values. The dimensions individualism, masculinity and uncertainty avoidance show significant differences for half of the countries."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""abstract"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": false, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Abstract contests many original findings, with discrepancies on two dimensions and only half the countries on three dimensions; coded as partial agreement/disagreement with uncertainty because no full results are available. No reference list supplied, so cannot resolve whether the underlying original findings belong to one or multiple Hofstede publications.""}"
10.1186/s13195-015-0154-x,1c4983e63889f830,1,10.1038/ng.2802,"Meta-analysis of 74,046 individuals identifies 11 new susceptibility loci for Alzheimer's disease.","Lambert JC, Ibrahim-Verbaas CA, Harold D, Naj AC, Sims R, Bellenguez C, et al.",2013,full_text,,correct,argon,"{""target_index"": 1, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""verdict"": ""shipped_right"", ""corrected_value"": null, ""quote"": ""Additionally, none of these loss-of-function variants were in linkage disequilibrium (LD) with the ABCA7 common variant identified by GWAS [ 2 ], suggesting that there are multiple and independent mechanisms throughout the ABCA7 region that increase risk for AD. Based on these results, we tried to replicate the association of the ABCA7 loss-of-function variants in a large cohort of European–Americans."", ""location"": ""Introduction"", ""reason"": ""Lambert et al. (2013) [2] is cited only once in the Introduction as background identifying the previously reported GWAS common variant (rs4147929). The paper explicitly identifies Steinberg et al. [1] as 'the original study' and genotypes rs4147929 as a power check and to confirm Steinberg et al.'s finding that the ABCA7 loss-of-function variants are independent of (not in LD with) the common GWAS signal, rather than as an independent target replication of Lambert et al. [2].""}","{""authors"": ""Lambert JC, Ibrahim-Verbaas CA, Harold D, Naj AC, Sims R, Bellenguez C, et al."", ""year"": ""2013"", ""title"": ""Meta-analysis of 74,046 individuals identifies 11 new susceptibility loci for Alzheimer's disease."", ""doi"": ""10.1038/ng.2802"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""The ABCA7 GWAS common variant (rs4147929) as well as the loss-of-function variants (p.Glu709Alafs*86, p.Leu1403Argfs*7, rs200538373, and rs113809142) reported by Steinberg et al. [ 1 ] were genotyped in our dataset using competitive allele-specific polymerase chain reaction KASPar genotyping technologies as described previously [ 3 , 4 ]."", ""outcome"": ""successful"", ""outcome_quote"": ""In our dataset, the common variant rs4147929[A] was significantly associated with AD risk (Table 1 ; OR = 1.162, P = 0.022), suggesting that we have enough power to replicate known associations for common variants."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""results"", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The GWAS ABCA7 common-variant association is deliberately genotyped and independently replicated; citation 2 identifies the publication that reported that signal.""}"
10.1111/jcal.12211,73bcc3e73f111e2c,0,10.1016/j.compedu.2017.05.013,Seeing the instructor's face and gaze in demonstration video examples affects attention allocation but not learning.,"Van Wermeskerken, M., & Van Gog, T.",2017,full_text,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": ""Van Wermeskerken, M., & Van Gog, T."", ""year"": ""2017"", ""title"": ""Seeing the instructor's face and gaze in demonstration video examples affects attention allocation but not learning."", ""doi"": ""10.1016/j.compedu.2017.05.013"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""This study replicated the face visible and face not visible conditions from our prior study (Van Wermeskerken & Van Gog, 2017) with adolescents in prevocational education, using the same molecule building task, to investigate whether or not seeing the model's face would affect learning outcomes in a younger population with a lower level of education."", ""outcome"": ""successful"", ""outcome_quote"": ""With respect to the first aim, we replicated our previous findings in this adolescent population, by showing that their learning was not affected by the presence or absence of the model's face, even though they spent proportionally less time looking at the task area when the model's face was visible."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Authors explicitly state replication of their prior findings; the earlier Van Gog et al. paper is background in the replication chain, not the closest target.""}"
10.1002/jocb.350,0f58697d4f41df92,0,,,,,abstract,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": """", ""year"": """", ""title"": """", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": """", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""We could also replicate a positive relationship of mental speed with speeded divergent thinking (r = .21) and mental speed with unspeeded divergent thinking (r = .25)."", ""outcome"": ""successful"", ""outcome_quote"": ""We could also replicate a positive relationship of mental speed with speeded divergent thinking (r = .21) and mental speed with unspeeded divergent thinking (r = .25)."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""abstract"", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The abstract explicitly states the positive relationships replicated, but does not identify the earlier paper(s); target identity and number remain uncertain.""}"
10.1002/per.2176,04117bda0ee9a4bf,1,10.1016/j.jrp.2011.12.003,From madness to genius: The Openness/Intellect trait domain as a paradoxical simplex,"DeYoung, C. G., Grazioplene, R. G., & Peterson, J. B.",2012,full_text,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": ""DeYoung, C. G., Grazioplene, R. G., & Peterson, J. B."", ""year"": ""2012"", ""title"": ""From madness to genius: The Openness/Intellect trait domain as a paradoxical simplex"", ""doi"": ""10.1016/j.jrp.2011.12.003"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": ""1, 2"", ""evidence_quote"": ""The O/I simplex reported in Study 1 and by DeYoung and colleagues (2012) was approximately replicated, albeit imperfectly, and a simplex model was adequate for describing relations among the O/I constructs."", ""outcome"": ""successful"", ""outcome_quote"": ""The results were also consistent with the DeYoung and colleagues (2012) ﬁnding that the O/I domain is well described by a simplex. In both studies, a simplex model ﬁt the data well, with intelligence at one end, apophenia at the other end, and the remaining constructs falling in between."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_confident"": true}"
10.1016/j.system.2020.102377,f133a7e9cc8706dc,0,,,Henry and Cliffordson,2017,abstract,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": ""Henry and Cliffordson"", ""year"": ""2017"", ""title"": """", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""Results replicated those of Henry and Cliffordson (2017) for initial hypotheses related to motivational selves; current and ideal selves were statistically significant predictors of effort, in line with prior studies in the Chinese context."", ""outcome"": ""mixed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""Results replicated those of Henry and Cliffordson (2017) for initial hypotheses related to motivational selves; current and ideal selves were statistically significant predictors of effort, in line with prior studies in the Chinese context. However, results among this sample supported discrete constructs for ideal L2 and current L2 self and provide evidence of a separable sub-component of self-authenticity, digital self-authenticity."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""abstract"", ""outcome_confident"": false, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Initial hypotheses replicated but the abstract frames construct results as contrasting with them. Publisher conclusion preview also explicitly refers to discrepancies with Henry and Cliffordson. Full results unavailable; mixed is uncertain.""}"
10.1017/s0033291720001919,7252b65650fbd13d,0,,,,,abstract,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": """", ""year"": """", ""title"": """", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": """", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""This study aimed to replicate research behind Climate Schools: Alcohol and Cannabis course among a large cohort of adolescents."", ""outcome"": ""cannot_be_determined"", ""outcome_quote"": """", ""outcome_quote_section"": """", ""outcome_confident"": false, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Abstract explicitly reports replicating alcohol effects but names no original paper or uniquely bounded set of papers. It does not establish which findings from which original were re-tested, so a per-original verdict cannot be assigned. An unresolved target placeholder represents missing identification, not a claim that only one original exists.""}"
10.3390/jcm10010052,0e4704e600fb03ca,0,10.1192/bjo.2020.109,"Anxiety, depression, traumatic stress and COVID-19-related anxiety in the UK general population during the COVID-19 pandemic.","Shevlin, M.; McBride, O.; Murphy, J.; Miller, J.G.; Hartman, T.K.; Levita, L.; Mason, L.; Martinez, A.P.; McKay, R.; Stocks, T.V.A.; et al.",2020,full_text,,error,argon,"{""target_index"": 0, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""verdict"": ""blind_right"", ""corrected_value"": {""doi"": ""10.1192/bjo.2020.109"", ""title"": ""Anxiety, depression, traumatic stress and COVID-19-related anxiety in the UK general population during the COVID-19 pandemic."", ""authors"": ""Shevlin, M.; McBride, O.; Murphy, J.; Miller, J.G.; Hartman, T.K.; Levita, L.; Mason, L.; Martinez, A.P.; McKay, R.; Stocks, T.V.A.; et al."", ""year"": ""2020"", ""type"": ""replication"", ""outcome"": ""mixed""}, ""quote"": ""Summarizing, these results are in line with those of the UK study about anxiety, depression, and traumatic stress [19]. The UK and Italian data identify factors associated with worst psychological health: being a woman, younger age, having children, preexisting health issues of self or someone close, and the moderate to high perceived risk of contracting COVID-19 within one month. Unlike Italy, in the UK lower household incomes and having (had) the COVID-19 were associated with worst psychological health outcomes."", ""location"": ""Discussion, line 49 (see also Aims, line 14; Analytic Methods, line 34; Discussion, line 44; References, line 288)"", ""reason"": ""Omitted target. The paper explicitly re-tests two distinct UK C19PRC empirical papers [19,20]. In Section 3.2 (line 34, 41) and the Discussion (line 44: 'In a second part of the analysis, we replicated the approach by Shevlin et al. [19]'), the authors replicate the three binary logistic regression models from Shevlin et al. (2020) [19] (10.1192/bjo.2020.109) in the Italian sample and report mixed results (line 49: several risk factors matched the UK study, whereas lower household income and having had COVID-19 were associated with worse outcomes in the UK unlike Italy). Because [19] is an independent published paper whose findings are directly re-tested, it must be coded as a separate replication target.""}","{""authors"": ""Shevlin, M.; McBride, O.; Murphy, J.; Miller, J.G.; Hartman, T.K.; Levita, L.; Mason, L.; Martinez, A.P.; McKay, R.; Stocks, T.V.A.; et al."", ""year"": ""2020"", ""title"": ""Anxiety, depression, traumatic stress and COVID-19-related anxiety in the UK general population during the COVID-19 pandemic."", ""doi"": ""10.1192/bjo.2020.109"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""In a second part of the analysis, we replicated the approach by Shevlin et al. [19]."", ""outcome"": ""mixed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""Summarizing, these results are in line with those of the UK study about anxiety, depression, and traumatic stress [19]. The UK and Italian data identify factors associated with worst psychological health: being a woman, younger age, having children, preexisting health issues of self or someone close, and the moderate to high perceived risk of contracting COVID-19 within one month. Unlike Italy, in the UK lower household incomes and having (had) the COVID-19 were associated with worst psychological health outcomes."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The authors explicitly identify matching risk factors and contrasting findings; the first sentence concerns these results rather than an overall verdict on the whole replication.""}"
10.1108/ijpdlm-04-2021-0121,a15282356e0c998b,1,,Power and relationship commitment: Their impact on marketing channel member performance.,"Brown, J., Lusch, R. & Nicholson, C. Y.",1995,full_text,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": ""Brown, J., Lusch, R. & Nicholson, C. Y."", ""year"": ""1995"", ""title"": ""Power and relationship commitment: Their impact on marketing channel member performance."", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""We find that the impact of non-mediated power  on normative relationship commitment and \n\nthat  of  mediated  power  on  instrumental  relationship  commitment  in  the  United  States  are \n\nconsistent with the findings of Brown et al. (1995). Moreover, the results show that expert and \n\nlegitimate power are negatively associated with whereas referent power is positively associated \n\nwith  instrumental  relationship  commitment,  which  provides  a  possible  explanation  of  the \n\nfinding  of  the  insignificant  effect  of  non-mediated  power  on  instrumental  relationship \n\ncommitment  (Brown  et  al.,  1995).  The  result  reveals  that  different  bases  of  non-mediated \n\npower  may  have  conflicting  effects  on  instrumental  relationship  commitment  and  hence \n\nresearchers need to consider the individual effects of different bases of non-mediated power. \n\nMoreover, we find that reward, coercive and legal legitimate power do not significantly affect \n\nnormative relationship  commitment,  whereas Brown et  al. (1995) find that mediated power \n\nsignificantly reduces normative relationship commitment. Brown et al. (1995) also investigate \n\nthe moderating effect of symmetry of power and find that a supplier’s use of mediated power \n\ndoes  not  significantly  affect  normative  relational  commitment  when  the  supplier  is  more \n\npowerful. We focus on a manufacturer’s major customers and hence the customers usually are \n\nmore powerful. Thus, the result supports Brown et al.’s (1995) finding on the moderating effect \n\nof symmetry of power. The finding suggests that researchers should consider the moderating \n\neffect of symmetry of power when investigating the impacts of mediated power."", ""outcome"": ""mixed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""We find that the impact of non-mediated power  on normative relationship commitment and \n\nthat  of  mediated  power  on  instrumental  relationship  commitment  in  the  United  States  are \n\nconsistent with the findings of Brown et al. (1995). Moreover, the results show that expert and \n\nlegitimate power are negatively associated with whereas referent power is positively associated \n\nwith  instrumental  relationship  commitment,  which  provides  a  possible  explanation  of  the \n\nfinding  of  the  insignificant  effect  of  non-mediated  power  on  instrumental  relationship \n\ncommitment  (Brown  et  al.,  1995).  The  result  reveals  that  different  bases  of  non-mediated \n\npower  may  have  conflicting  effects  on  instrumental  relationship  commitment  and  hence \n\nresearchers need to consider the individual effects of different bases of non-mediated power. \n\nMoreover, we find that reward, coercive and legal legitimate power do not significantly affect \n\nnormative relationship  commitment,  whereas Brown et  al. (1995) find that mediated power \n\nsignificantly reduces normative relationship commitment."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The US sample directly tests the power-commitment findings of Brown et al.; the dedicated comparison states both agreements and a contrast for mediated power and normative commitment. The later moderation comparison supports one part, not an overall verdict.""}"
10.1177/25152459241267902,5f752faa16b30af3,1,10.1016/j.concog.2016.01.004,"Vision, knowledge, and assertion.","Turri, J.",2016,full_text,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": ""Turri, J."", ""year"": ""2016"", ""title"": ""Vision, knowledge, and assertion."", ""doi"": ""10.1016/j.concog.2016.01.004"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": ""Experiment 7"", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""Next, we explored whether attributions of outcomes to luck versus ability influence knowledge attributions, as suggested by prior research (Turri, 2016, 2017)."", ""outcome"": ""failed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""Thus, unlike in previous research (Turri, 2016, 2017), luck attributions did not decrease the likelihood of participants demonstrating Gettier intuitions."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""results"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Explicit comparisons to the stated original finding determine the verdict.""}"
