10.1080/01973533.2020.1806845,da281f858e509152,0,10.1006/jesp.2001.1491,Reducing the effects of stereotype threat on African American college students by shaping theories of intelligence.,"Aronson, J., Fried, C. B., & Good, C.",2002,full_text,,correct,argon,"{""target_index"": 0, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""verdict"": ""shipped_right"", ""corrected_value"": null, ""quote"": ""Growth mindset interventions have been effective at improving students’grades and/or GPA (Aronson et al., 2002; Yeager, Walton, et al., 2016), increasing student retention (Yeager, Walton, et al., 2016), and enjoyment and/or engagement in academics (Aronson et al., 2002)."", ""location"": ""introduction (p. 2)"", ""reason"": ""Wrong original by blind coder (no omitted target): Aronson et al. (2002) is cited only in multi-work literature review sentences in the Introduction alongside six other studies and is never mentioned in the Methods, Results, or Discussion. Under the codebook, a work cited for background or motivation without the paper re-testing its specific finding is not a replication target.""}","{""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""outcome"": ""failed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""The proposition that a one-time intervention at the postsecondary level will result in long-term measurable student outcomes was not supported in the present study."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The authors conclude the college intervention does not improve academic outcomes; their new university sample re-tests prior college effects."", ""authors"": ""Aronson, J., Fried, C. B., & Good, C."", ""year"": ""2002"", ""title"": ""Reducing the effects of stereotype threat on African American college students by shaping theories of intelligence."", ""doi"": ""10.1006/jesp.2001.1491"", ""evidence_quote"": ""Growth mindset interventions have been effective at improving students’grades and/or GPA (Aronson et al., 2002; Yeager, Walton, et al., 2016), increasing student retention (Yeager, Walton, et al., 2016), and enjoyment and/or engagement in academics (Aronson et al., 2002).""}"
10.1080/01973533.2020.1806845,da281f858e509152,1,10.1073/pnas.1524360113,Teaching a lay theory before college narrows achievement gaps at scale.,"Yeager, D. S., Walton, G. M., Brady, S. T., Akcinar, E. N., Paunesku, D., Keane, L., Kamentz, D., Ritter, G., Duckworth, A. L., Urstein, R., Gomez, E. M., Markus, H. R., Cohen, G. L., & Dweck, C. S.",2016,full_text,,correct,argon,"{""target_index"": 1, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""verdict"": ""shipped_right"", ""corrected_value"": null, ""quote"": ""The research design followed protocols used in earlier mindset intervention research; however, the student population involved in the present study had not been addressed in the previous studies. Additionally, we included first-time college students, a time of transition when interventions are purported to be especially applicable (Yeager, Walton, et al., 2016). Despite previous findings showing the effectiveness of a one-time intervention for this transition time (Yeager & Walton, 2011), our data did not support this conclusion."", ""location"": ""discussion (p. 5)"", ""reason"": ""Wrong original by blind coder (no omitted target): Yeager, Walton, et al. (2016) is not cited in Materials and Methods (where only Blackwell et al., 2007 is cited for the intervention), and three of the five academic terms of data collection (Spring 2015, Fall 2015, and Spring 2016, p. 3) took place before Yeager, Walton, et al. (2016) was published. In the Introduction and Discussion it is cited only as broader literature context on college transition and disadvantaged student populations.""}","{""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""outcome"": ""failed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""The proposition that a one-time intervention at the postsecondary level will result in long-term measurable student outcomes was not supported in the present study."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The authors conclude the college intervention does not improve academic outcomes; their new university sample re-tests prior college effects."", ""authors"": ""Yeager, D. S., Walton, G. M., Brady, S. T., Akcinar, E. N., Paunesku, D., Keane, L., Kamentz, D., Ritter, G., Duckworth, A. L., Urstein, R., Gomez, E. M., Markus, H. R., Cohen, G. L., & Dweck, C. S."", ""year"": ""2016"", ""title"": ""Teaching a lay theory before college narrows achievement gaps at scale."", ""doi"": ""10.1073/pnas.1524360113"", ""evidence_quote"": ""Additionally, we included first-time college students, a time of transition when interventions are purported to be especially applicable (Yeager, Walton, et al., 2016).""}"
10.32872/cpe.v2i4.2593,8ac348d62cc5f33c,1,10.1080/02699931.2016.1154817,The beneficial and detrimental effects of major depression on intuitive decision-making.,"Remmers C., Topolinski S., Buxton A., Dietrich D. E., Michalak J.",2017,full_text,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": ""Remmers C., Topolinski S., Buxton A., Dietrich D. E., Michalak J."", ""year"": ""2017"", ""title"": ""The beneficial and detrimental effects of major depression on intuitive decision-making."", ""doi"": ""10.1080/02699931.2016.1154817"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""The first hypothesis (H1) refers to the replicability of recently found intuition deficits in depression ( Remmers et al., 2015 ; Remmers et al., 2017 )."", ""outcome"": ""failed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""Overall the current study did not replicate findings from previous research regarding intuitive semantic coherence detection deficits in depression."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""abstract"", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The overall deficit replication verdict covers the two originals explicitly named for H1.""}"
10.32872/cpe.v2i4.2593,8ac348d62cc5f33c,2,10.1037/a0014678,The architecture of intuition: Fluency and affect determine intuitive judgments of semantic and visual coherence and judgments of grammaticality in artificial grammar learning.,"Topolinski S., Strack F.",2009,full_text,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": ""Topolinski S., Strack F."", ""year"": ""2009"", ""title"": ""The architecture of intuition: Fluency and affect determine intuitive judgments of semantic and visual coherence and judgments of grammaticality in artificial grammar learning."", ""doi"": ""10.1037/a0014678"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": ""Experiment 8 (valence); fluency experiment unspecified"", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""Building up on basic research ( Topolinski & Strack, 2009a ; Experiment 8) showing that healthy subjects are more likely to judge triads to be coherent that consist of positive as compared to negative words, we expected that this effect would also emerge for depressed patients."", ""outcome"": ""mixed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""Results did not reveal any effect of our fluency manipulation, and thus our hypotheses regarding fluency (H2) were not supported. As such, the current study could not replicate previous results that bolstered the fluency model proposed by Topolinski and Strack (2009a , 2009b )."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Fluency comparison fails, while positive-valence comparison agrees. The fluency passage explicitly contrasts prior results. Additional agreement passage: The results further showed that positive (vs. negative) valence triggered coherence judgments (H3a) and that this effect was – in contrast to our hypothesis (H3b) – not moderated by depression.""}"
10.32872/cpe.v2i4.2593,8ac348d62cc5f33c,3,10.1080/02699930802420745,The analysis of intuition: Processing fluency and affect in judgements of semantic coherence.,"Topolinski S., Strack F.",2009,full_text,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": ""Topolinski S., Strack F."", ""year"": ""2009"", ""title"": ""The analysis of intuition: Processing fluency and affect in judgements of semantic coherence."", ""doi"": ""10.1080/02699930802420745"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""Results did not reveal any effect of our fluency manipulation, and thus our hypotheses regarding fluency (H2) were not supported. As such, the current study could not replicate previous results that bolstered the fluency model proposed by Topolinski and Strack (2009a , 2009b )."", ""outcome"": ""failed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""Results did not reveal any effect of our fluency manipulation, and thus our hypotheses regarding fluency (H2) were not supported. As such, the current study could not replicate previous results that bolstered the fluency model proposed by Topolinski and Strack (2009a , 2009b )."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Authors explicitly include this paper in the previous fluency results they could not replicate. No separately attributable positive-valence retest is stated for this original.""}"
10.1017/rdc.2024.52,2cb7ebb539e144f1,1,,A signature of cosmic-ray increase in AD 774–775 from tree rings in Japan.,"Miyake F, Nagaya K, Masuda K and Nakamura T",2012,full_text,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": ""Miyake F, Nagaya K, Masuda K and Nakamura T"", ""year"": ""2012"", ""title"": ""A signature of cosmic-ray increase in AD 774–775 from tree rings in Japan."", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""Firstly, we present new 14C measurements over the period from 771–779 CE, spanning the years of the 775 CE event. Our results show the same sharp rise shown by Miyake et al. (2012), even though the Δ14C values are on average higher than the ones reported from the original study."", ""outcome"": ""successful"", ""outcome_quote"": ""Firstly, we present new 14C measurements over the period from 771–779 CE, spanning the years of the 775 CE event. Our results show the same sharp rise shown by Miyake et al. (2012), even though the Δ14C values are on average higher than the ones reported from the original study."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_confident"": true}"
10.1515/iral-2012-0001,7399924f3ac7dc01,1,,The role of pronouns in young children’s acquisition of the English transitive construction.,"Childers, Jane B & Tomasello, Michael.",2001,full_text,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": ""Childers, Jane B & Tomasello, Michael."", ""year"": ""2001"", ""title"": ""The role of pronouns in young children’s acquisition of the English transitive construction."", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""The present study differs from Childers and Tomasello (2001) because only proforms such as he were provided in the noun phrases during the training session."", ""outcome"": ""failed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""Hypothesis 2 was also disproved. The results showed that input consistency played a negative role for adult L2 construction learning."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""This paper explicitly retests the input-consistency benefit; the reported effect does not agree.""}"
10.1515/iral-2012-0001,7399924f3ac7dc01,2,,Constructions as categories of language: The role of order on construction learning.,"Goldberg, Adele E., Casenhiser, Devin & White, Tiffani.",2007,full_text,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": ""Goldberg, Adele E., Casenhiser, Devin & White, Tiffani."", ""year"": ""2007"", ""title"": ""Constructions as categories of language: The role of order on construction learning."", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""Goldberg et al. (2007) showed that initially providing skewed input facilitated rapid construction generalization. The present study investigates this effect for the position of verbs with relative high token frequency."", ""outcome"": ""mixed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""There was a significant main effect for order of frequent verbs in the comprehension of the APPEARANCE construction. Figure 1 shows that the (+forward order) group outperformed the (−forward skewing) group. No significant effects were found in the guided production of the APPEARANCE construction and the comprehension of the Samoan ergative construction."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The authors present an agreeing comprehension effect and explicitly absent effects in the other tasks; no overall verdict supersedes this comparison.""}"
10.1037/edu0000141,5a644ad030ba92a5,1,10.3102/0162373715581709,An Effort to Close Achievement Gaps at Scale through Self-Affirmation.,"Borman, G. D., Grigg, J., & Hanselman, P.",2016,full_text,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": ""Borman, G. D., Grigg, J., & Hanselman, P."", ""year"": ""2016"", ""title"": ""An Effort to Close Achievement Gaps at Scale through Self-Affirmation."", ""doi"": ""10.3102/0162373715581709"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""The current study was designed to replicate both the original self-affirmation study\n(Cohen et al., 2006) and the previous successful independent replication (Borman et al., 2016)."", ""outcome"": ""failed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""We found no evidence of\neffects in this replication study and estimates were precise enough to reject benefits larger than\nan effect size of 0.10. These null effects were significantly different from persistent benefits in\nthe prior study in the same setting, and extensive testing revealed that currently theorized\nmoderators of self-affirmation effects could not explain the difference."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""abstract"", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The new cohort produced no benefits; the paper explicitly describes its failure to replicate. Previously reported cohort results are the comparison, not new successful replications.""}"
10.1080/00221546.2019.1650582,47a8be34f8916560,0,,,,,abstract,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": """", ""year"": """", ""title"": """", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": """", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""Though low-cost value affirmation interventions have been shown to reduce achievement gaps in some classroom settings, these findings have not been consistently replicated. In this study, we test the efficacy of this intervention among a new sample of students enrolled in a community college in the Midwest."", ""outcome"": ""failed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""Overall, we find little evidence of a positive effect of this one-time affirmation of social identity."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""abstract"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Overall verdict indicates little evidence for the intervention effect. Abstract does not identify the original paper; no citation guessed.""}"
10.1007/s11205-005-5074-x,b62c747678016333,0,,Feeding the illusion of growth and happiness: A reply to Hagerty and Veenhoven,"Easterlin, R.A",2005,full_text,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": ""Easterlin, R.A"", ""year"": ""2005"", ""title"": ""Feeding the illusion of growth and happiness: A reply to Hagerty and Veenhoven"", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""Richard Easterlin elegantly disputed these findings in a paper: Feeding the\n      Illusion of Growth and Happiness: A Reply to Hagerty and Veenhoven, published in this\n      journal (Easterlin 2005)."", ""outcome"": ""failed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""All in all, it seems that people have become happier in the second half of the 20th century\n    and that the gains in happiness have been greater in poor nations than in rich nations."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The extended national survey series contradict the closest target in the debate: Easterlin’s 2005 no-growth interpretation. The overall judgment is rising happiness despite measurement uncertainty.""}"
10.1371/journal.pone.0220419,d54c6bf1f5190f00,0,,"Interfering with theories of sleep and memory: sleep, declarative memory, and associative interference.","Ellenbogen JM, Hulbert JC, Stickgold R, et al.",2006,full_text,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": ""Ellenbogen JM, Hulbert JC, Stickgold R, et al."", ""year"": ""2006"", ""title"": ""Interfering with theories of sleep and memory: sleep, declarative memory, and associative interference."", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""However, two studies by Ellenbogen, et al. [\n8\n,\n9\n] have been by far the most cited and most influential evidence for sleep’s proposed role in protecting against interference. These studies form the basis for the present replication."", ""outcome"": ""failed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""However, we did not replicate Ellenbogen et al.’s observation of a large effect of sleep in protecting memories against interference learning. Instead, we observed that the Sleep and Wake groups both experienced a significant and very similar retroactive interference effect."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The central protective effect failed; title and abstract give the overall failure verdict.""}"
10.1371/journal.pone.0220419,d54c6bf1f5190f00,2,,Scopolamine does not impact declarative and motor memory consolidation across a night of sleep or a day of wakefulness.,"Tucker MA, Taylor K, Merchant R, et al.",2018,full_text,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": ""Tucker MA, Taylor K, Merchant R, et al."", ""year"": ""2018"", ""title"": ""Scopolamine does not impact declarative and motor memory consolidation across a night of sleep or a day of wakefulness."", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""We have also shown that increased intrinsic motivation (the desire to do well even in the absence of extrinsic rewards), measured by responses to items from the Intrinsic Motivation Inventory [\n27\n] used in the present study, correlated with better acquisition of visual paired associates (picture-object pairs) at training, and also with change in performance from training to retest (retention) [\n26\n]."", ""outcome"": ""mixed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""In the present study, we observed a similar positive correlation between intrinsic motivation and training and retest performance. However, motivation did not correlate with memory consolidation (change in performance from training to retest), nor did intrinsic motivation favor performance in the sleep (v. wake) participants."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The separately hypothesized re-test of a published intrinsic-motivation finding agrees on acquisition but explicitly differs on consolidation.""}"
10.31219/osf.io/x394e_v3,78fb9fef3070ea73,1,10.1037/a0021971,Hindsight bias from 3 to 95 years of age.,"Bernstein, D. M., Erdfelder, E., Meltzoff, A. N., Peria, W., & Loftus, G. R.",2011,full_text,,correct,argon,"{""target_index"": 1, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""verdict"": ""shipped_right"", ""corrected_value"": null, ""quote"": ""We conducted an open data and open material pre-registered replication of Experiment 2 from Fischhoff (1975) with extensions investigating the associations between the hindsight bias with sociodemographic variables and depressive symptoms, and the moderation role of depressive symptoms on hindsight bias."", ""location"": ""introduction"", ""reason"": ""Wrong original: the paper only replicates Experiment 2 of Fischhoff (1975), while Bernstein et al. (2011) is cited only as background literature motivating the novel extension hypothesis on age.""}","{""authors"": ""Bernstein, D. M., Erdfelder, E., Meltzoff, A. N., Peria, W., & Loftus, G. R."", ""year"": ""2011"", ""title"": ""Hindsight bias from 3 to 95 years of age."", ""doi"": ""10.1037/a0021971"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""For the extensions, we predict that age will positively correlate with hindsight bias, whereas other socio-demographic variables (such as socioeconomic status, gender, occupation, and education) will not (Hypothesis 2). This investigation will provide further evidence for the claim that populations of older ages are more susceptible to hindsight bias (Bernstein et al., 2011; Groß & Bayen, 2021; Pohl et al., 2018), whereas evidence for gender, occupation, and education is not consolidated yet."", ""outcome"": ""failed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""We expected to find a positive correlation between age and hindsight bias, and no correlation with other socio-demographic variables. Also, we expected to find a positive correlation between depressive symptoms and hindsight bias. Contrary to our expectations, no correlation was found between hindsight bias and sociodemographic or depressive symptoms. These results contrast with previous findings showing a higher magnitude of hindsight bias for older adults (Groß, 2013) and a positive correlation between depressive symptoms and hindsight bias (Groß et al., 2017; Groß & Bayen, 2017)."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Specific earlier finding is explicitly tested and the authors report its absence. Differences in design do not remove the comparison.""}"
10.31219/osf.io/x394e_v3,78fb9fef3070ea73,2,10.3758/s13421-021-01195-w,Older and younger adults’ hindsight bias after positive and negative outcomes.,"Groß, J., & Bayen, U. J.",2021,full_text,,correct,argon,"{""target_index"": 2, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""verdict"": ""shipped_right"", ""corrected_value"": null, ""quote"": ""We conducted an open data and open material pre-registered replication of Experiment 2 from Fischhoff (1975) with extensions investigating the associations between the hindsight bias with sociodemographic variables and depressive symptoms, and the moderation role of depressive symptoms on hindsight bias."", ""location"": ""introduction"", ""reason"": ""Wrong original: the paper only replicates Experiment 2 of Fischhoff (1975), while Groß and Bayen (2021) is cited only as background literature motivating the novel extension hypotheses on age and depressive symptoms.""}","{""authors"": ""Groß, J., & Bayen, U. J."", ""year"": ""2021"", ""title"": ""Older and younger adults’ hindsight bias after positive and negative outcomes."", ""doi"": ""10.3758/s13421-021-01195-w"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""For the extensions, we predict that age will positively correlate with hindsight bias, whereas other socio-demographic variables (such as socioeconomic status, gender, occupation, and education) will not (Hypothesis 2). This investigation will provide further evidence for the claim that populations of older ages are more susceptible to hindsight bias (Bernstein et al., 2011; Groß & Bayen, 2021; Pohl et al., 2018), whereas evidence for gender, occupation, and education is not consolidated yet. Also, we predict that depressive symptoms will be positively correlated with hindsight bias (Hypothesis 3), thus suggesting an interaction between the cognitive mechanisms in depression and the magnitude of the hindsight bias. Finally, considering the previous evidence of higher levels of hindsight bias associated with higher levels of depressive symptoms (Groß et al., 2017; Groß & Bayen, 2021; Haslam & Jayasinghe, 1995), we expect that depressive symptoms will act as a moderator variable of the hindsight bias effect (Hypothesis 4)."", ""outcome"": ""failed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""We expected to find a positive correlation between age and hindsight bias, and no correlation with other socio-demographic variables. Also, we expected to find a positive correlation between depressive symptoms and hindsight bias. Contrary to our expectations, no correlation was found between hindsight bias and sociodemographic or depressive symptoms. These results contrast with previous findings showing a higher magnitude of hindsight bias for older adults (Groß, 2013) and a positive correlation between depressive symptoms and hindsight bias (Groß et al., 2017; Groß & Bayen, 2017)."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Specific earlier finding is explicitly tested and the authors report its absence. Differences in design do not remove the comparison.""}"
