10.1037/com0000223,c700fc592b908d09,0,10.1371/journal.pbio.0060202,Mirror-induced behavior in the magpie (Pica pica): Evidence of self-recognition.,"Prior, H., Schwarz, A., & Güntürkün, O.",2008,full_text,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": ""Prior, H., Schwarz, A., & Güntürkün, O."", ""year"": ""2008"", ""title"": ""Mirror-induced behavior in the magpie (Pica pica): Evidence of self-recognition."", ""doi"": ""10.1371/journal.pbio.0060202"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""Here,\nwe present a close replication of the study of Prior et al. (2008) by\nusing eight magpies captured in the wild and following almost\nexactly the five successive experimental stages and performing the\nsame statistical analyses as in Prior et al. (2008)."", ""outcome"": ""failed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""Thus,\nour replication fails to confirm the original results by Prior et al.\n(2008)."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Explicit overall verdict that this replication fails to confirm the original results. Agreement in preliminary behavior comparisons does not outweigh that overall verdict.""}"
10.1016/s0163-6383(83)80012-5,592d372d41a77ec8,0,,,,,abstract,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": """", ""year"": """", ""title"": """", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": """", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": ""1, 2"", ""evidence_quote"": ""The purpose of the present research was to replicate two experiments in which neonatal imitation was reported."", ""outcome"": ""failed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""The results of both replications failed to yield evidence of neonatal imitation and thus raise questions about the neonate’s capacity to imitate."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""abstract"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The abstract gives an explicit verdict covering both attempts, but names neither original. This unnamed entry records the unresolved target relationship; the number of distinct original papers cannot be established from abstract evidence.""}"
10.2307/2586310,b91a64486d8bd684,1,,,"MacKuen, Erikson, and Stimson",1992,abstract,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": ""MacKuen, Erikson, and Stimson"", ""year"": ""1992"", ""title"": """", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""MacKuen, Erikson, and Stimson (1989, 1992) argue that the aggregate distribution of party identification, or macropartisanship, shifts significantly over short periods in response to changes in presidential popularity and consumer sentiment. We replicate this analysis using a more extensive inventory of Gallup and CBS/New York Timesdata and find considerably less evidence of partisan fluctuation."", ""outcome"": ""successful"", ""outcome_quote"": ""The amount of partisan change caused by short-term movements in consumer sentiment and presidential popularity is found to be two to three times smaller than initially reported by MacKuen et al. (1989). Our results indicate that macropartisanship adjusts to short-term shocks in a limited and gradual fashion, consistent with traditional views of partisan realignment."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""abstract"", ""outcome_confident"": false, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The abstract describes both cited originals as reporting responsiveness to these inputs and reports responsiveness in the same direction but smaller. Reduced magnitude alone does not imply mixed. Full-text verdict and reference entries unavailable.""}"
10.1080/0003684042000229532,236d200a82c3a67f,1,,"Career mobility, job match, and overeducation",Robst J.,1995,full_text,,error,argon,"{""target_index"": 1, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""verdict"": ""blind_right"", ""corrected_value"": {""authors"": ""Robst J."", ""year"": ""1995"", ""title"": ""Career mobility, job match, and overeducation"", ""doi"": """", ""type"": ""replication"", ""outcome"": ""successful""}, ""quote"": ""In Section 4.3, we extend Robst’s (1995) test for robustness by using an objective, as well as a subjective, measure of training participation. Both measures confirm Robst’s findings that overeducated (undereducated) workers indeed receive less (more) training than correctly allocated ones."", ""location"": ""Section 1 (Introduction), p. 3, lines 190-198; Section 4.3, pp. 16-17, lines 864-920"", ""reason"": ""Omitted target in shipped data. Beyond citing Robst (1995) as background, the paper explicitly re-tests Robst's (1995) empirical finding on training participation in Section 4.3 (Tables 5 and 6) using the same subjective survey question in the German GSOEP sample as well as an objective formal training indicator, concluding that both measures confirm Robst's findings.""}","{""authors"": ""Robst J."", ""year"": ""1995"", ""title"": ""Career mobility, job match, and overeducation"", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""In Section 4.3, we extend Robst’s (1995) test for robustness by using an objective, as well as a subjective, measure of training participation."", ""outcome"": ""successful"", ""outcome_quote"": ""Both measures confirm Robst’s findings that overeducated (undereducated) workers indeed receive less (more) training than correctly allocated ones."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""introduction"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true}"
10.1016/j.rasd.2013.02.015,6c43f57b16329dce,0,,,Reichow and Wolery,,abstract,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": ""Reichow and Wolery"", ""year"": """", ""title"": """", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": ""1, 2"", ""evidence_quote"": ""The current pair of studies seeks to replicate and extend the findings of Reichow and Wolery."", ""outcome"": ""mixed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""Results were mixed and do not match previously published robust findings."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""fulltext"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Publisher highlight explicitly states mixed results relative to earlier robust findings. Abstract describes efficient acquisition for only one of four participants and no extension to dyad instruction. Bibliographic fields unavailable in abstract are left blank; in-text author form retained.""}"
10.1080/09362835.2016.1238379,4a65f0bbd549e374,0,,,"Hux, Marquardt, Skinner, and Bond",1999,abstract,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": ""Hux, Marquardt, Skinner, and Bond"", ""year"": ""1999"", ""title"": """", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""The purpose of this study is to replicate a study conducted by Hux, Marquardt, Skinner, and Bond (1999) in which the researchers explored the frequency of traumatic brain injuries (TBI) in the special education population as compared to the frequency of TBI among students in general education. "", ""outcome"": ""failed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""Results indicated that the sample in the current study reported a lower incidence rate of TBI than the general population as well as that in the 1999 study by Hux and colleagues. Additionally, only one student in the current sample (5% of TBI group) reported a history of TBI along with receiving special education services. Discrepancies between the current study and the Hux and colleagues (1999) study are explored, and possible reasons for the discrepancies are discussed."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""abstract"", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Explicit discrepancies from original incidence and special-education findings. Full reference unavailable.""}"
10.1016/j.jml.2018.07.004,a58db421709e9509,1,,Contrasting intrusion profiles for agreement and anaphora: Experimental and modeling evidence.,"Dillon, B., Mishler, A., Sloggett, S., & Phillips, C.",2013,full_text,,correct,argon,"{""target_index"": 1, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""verdict"": ""shipped_right"", ""corrected_value"": null, ""quote"": ""In the number agreement experiments, study 1 is the ungrammatical agreement data from Experiment 1 of Dillon et al. (2013); studies 2-5 are the experiments reported in Lago et al. (2015), and 6-10 are from Wagers et al. (2009). Studies 11 and 12 are from Cunnings and Sturt (2018). The estimates shown in the figure were computed by fitting a linear mixed model (with full covariance matrices for random effects) in Stan using log-transformed reading times, and then by back-transforming the estimate of the facilitation effect to milliseconds. Our estimates may be slightly different from the original published estimates in some cases because we did not remove any data."", ""location"": ""Appendix C, p. 72"", ""reason"": ""Wrong original / insufficient evidence from source: Dillon et al. (2013) is one of 12 datasets (10 from the prior meta-analysis of Jäger et al., 2017) plotted in the General Discussion and Appendix C merely as an incidental background illustration that wide credible intervals are common in psycholinguistics, rather than a central target the paper set out to test. Moreover, even in the blind coding, computational reproducibility was 'not checked' and substantive robustness for this specific study is 'cannot_be_determined' (the text reports only pooled summary ranges and an unattributed count across the 10 studies). Under the audit rules, incidental re-estimations with 'not checked' and 'cannot_be_determined' outcomes establish no omitted-target error.""}","{""authors"": ""Dillon, B., Mishler, A., Sloggett, S., & Phillips, C."", ""year"": ""2013"", ""title"": ""Contrasting intrusion profiles for agreement and anaphora: Experimental and modeling evidence."", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": ""Experiment 1"", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""In the number agreement experiments, study 1 is the ungrammatical agreement data from Experiment 1 of Dillon et al. (2013); studies 2-5 are the experiments reported in Lago et al. (2015), and 6-10 are from Wagers et al. (2009)."", ""outcome"": """", ""outcome_quote"": """", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""fulltext"", ""outcome_computation"": ""not checked"", ""outcome_computational_quote"": ""Our estimates may be slightly different from the original published estimates in some cases because we did not remove any data."", ""outcome_robustness"": ""cannot_be_determined"", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": false, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Original data were reanalysed with alternative models/inclusion rules. No target-specific substantive robustness verdict can be assigned from the pooled presentation; numerical reproduction was not the analysis goal.""}"
10.1016/j.jml.2018.07.004,a58db421709e9509,2,,Agreement attraction in Spanish comprehension.,"Lago, S., Shalom, D. E., Sigman, M., Lau, E. F., & Phillips, C.",2015,full_text,,correct,argon,"{""target_index"": 2, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""verdict"": ""shipped_right"", ""corrected_value"": null, ""quote"": ""In the number agreement experiments, study 1 is the ungrammatical agreement data from Experiment 1 of Dillon et al. (2013); studies 2-5 are the experiments reported in Lago et al. (2015), and 6-10 are from Wagers et al. (2009). Studies 11 and 12 are from Cunnings and Sturt (2018). The estimates shown in the figure were computed by fitting a linear mixed model (with full covariance matrices for random effects) in Stan using log-transformed reading times, and then by back-transforming the estimate of the facilitation effect to milliseconds. Our estimates may be slightly different from the original published estimates in some cases because we did not remove any data."", ""location"": ""Appendix C, p. 72"", ""reason"": ""Wrong original / insufficient evidence from source: Lago et al. (2015) is one of 12 datasets (from the prior meta-analysis of Jäger et al., 2017) plotted in the General Discussion and Appendix C as an incidental illustration of estimate uncertainty rather than a focal replication/reproduction target of this study. Even if treated as a reanalysis, computational reproducibility was 'not checked' and study-specific robustness is 'cannot_be_determined' because results are only discussed as pooled ranges and an unattributed significance count (1 of 10), which establishes no omitted-target error.""}","{""authors"": ""Lago, S., Shalom, D. E., Sigman, M., Lau, E. F., & Phillips, C."", ""year"": ""2015"", ""title"": ""Agreement attraction in Spanish comprehension."", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""In the number agreement experiments, 