10.2307/2586120,dc8b28ebe5aac8f8,0,,,,,abstract,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": """", ""year"": """", ""title"": """", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": """", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""Aggregate and survey data offer two ways of providing external validation for experiments. These aggregate data confirm our original findings. Experiments, surveys, and aggregate data all point to the same conclusion: Negative advertising demobilizes voters."", ""outcome"": ""successful"", ""outcome_quote"": ""These aggregate data confirm our original findings. Experiments, surveys, and aggregate data all point to the same conclusion: Negative advertising demobilizes voters."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""abstract"", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The abstract expressly says independent aggregate data confirm the original findings; identification uncertainty does not reduce confidence in this verdict.""}"
10.1002/hbm.25386,542be9dcc424519c,0,10.1001/jamapsychiatry.2018.2467,Mapping the heterogeneous phenotype of schizophrenia and bipolar disorder using normative models.,"Wolfers, T., Doan, N. T., Kaufmann, T., Alnæs, D., Moberget, T., Agartz, I., … Marquand, A. F.",2018,full_text,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": ""Wolfers, T., Doan, N. T., Kaufmann, T., Alnæs, D., Moberget, T., Agartz, I., … Marquand, A. F."", ""year"": ""2018"", ""title"": ""Mapping the heterogeneous phenotype of schizophrenia and bipolar disorder using normative models."", ""doi"": ""10.1001/jamapsychiatry.2018.2467"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": ""1, 2"", ""evidence_quote"": ""Due to these important implications, we here report an attempt to replicate and extend our initial findings in two independent samples acquired on different scanners following an identical analytical procedure as in our previous discovery study."", ""outcome"": ""mixed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""Our replications support that group level‐differences in brain structure disguise considerable individual differences in brain aberrations. While we find additional similarity across discovery and replication study (Figures S2 and S3), such as extreme negative deviations are on average 3.91, 4.00, and 4.95 times more prevalent in individuals with schizophrenia than in healthy controls, we also find differences. Especially with respect to extreme positive deviations the pattern of overlap is as similar as it is different across studies (Figure S4)."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Authors affirm the heterogeneity finding but explicitly describe both similarities and differences, including failure of the bipolar cerebellar group effect. The conclusion supporting the main heterogeneity claim concerns one effect, rather than all tested comparisons. Additional contrast: Further, we could not replicate a main group effect of bipolar disorder in comparison with healthy individuals in the cerebellum while this effect was present in the discovery sample (Figure 2).""}"
10.1007/s11002-025-09794-2,9e5e8fb1308ca417,1,,Misery is not miserly: Sad and self-focused individuals spend more,"Cryder, C. E., Lerner, J. S., Gross, J. J., & Dahl, R. E.",2008,full_text,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": ""Cryder, C. E., Lerner, J. S., Gross, J. J., & Dahl, R. E."", ""year"": ""2008"", ""title"": ""Misery is not miserly: Sad and self-focused individuals spend more"", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""These results offer a cohesive perspective on how both happiness and sadness relate to increased spending. Specifically, we provide support for a novel contingency that demonstrates\nwhen\nhappy people spend more. In this vein, our study replicates both Babin and Darden’s findings and Lerner et al.’s and Cryder et al.’s findings."", ""outcome"": ""successful"", ""outcome_quote"": ""These results offer a cohesive perspective on how both happiness and sadness relate to increased spending. Specifically, we provide support for a novel contingency that demonstrates\nwhen\nhappy people spend more. In this vein, our study replicates both Babin and Darden’s findings and Lerner et al.’s and Cryder et al.’s findings."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""results"", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Explicit set-level verdict says this study replicates all three originals. Product-contingency comparisons connect the tested happiness/spending findings with the specific original results. Bibliography contains no printed target DOI.""}"
