10.1037/xlm0001529,f915a8798237e4f0,1,10.1080/00221309.1992.9921156,The reversed eyewitness testimony design: More evidence for source monitoring.,"Rantzen, A., & Markham, R.",1992,full_text,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": ""Rantzen, A., & Markham, R."", ""year"": ""1992"", ""title"": ""The reversed eyewitness testimony design: More evidence for source monitoring."", ""doi"": ""10.1080/00221309.1992.9921156"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": ""3"", ""evidence_quote"": ""Although previous studies using this method support the persistence of the effect, supporting activation-based theories (e.g., Rantzen & Markham, 1992), these findings are based on a limited number of older studies. Therefore, in the current project, we replicated the reversed order procedure to distinguish between competing memory-based explanations of the misinformation effect."", ""outcome"": ""failed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""Importantly, we found no misinformation effect in the reversed order procedure, contrasting previous studies using the methodology (e.g., Lindsay & Johnson, 1989; Rantzen & Markham, 1992)."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true}"
