10.1017/s0261444825100992,c93427e18d97d673,1,10.1177/1362168820938814,Commitment to the profession of ELT and an organization: A profile of expat faculty in South Korea.,"Moodie, I.",2023,full_text,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": ""Moodie, I."", ""year"": ""2023"", ""title"": ""Commitment to the profession of ELT and an organization: A profile of expat faculty in South Korea."", ""doi"": ""10.1177/1362168820938814"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""Similarly, based on a prior study (Moodie, 2023), Hypothesis 6 expected higher affective and normative commitment among NESTs with formal teacher qualifications. These results did not replicate, indicating that teacher qualifications do not necessarily translate into stronger commitment profiles among expatriate NESTs."", ""outcome"": ""failed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""Similarly, based on a prior study (Moodie, 2023), Hypothesis 6 expected higher affective and normative commitment among NESTs with formal teacher qualifications. These results did not replicate, indicating that teacher qualifications do not necessarily translate into stronger commitment profiles among expatriate NESTs."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The extension explicitly re-tests the published qualification–commitment result and reports it did not replicate.""}"
10.1108/scm-06-2023-0297,9de69025f12b33a8,0,,,Ambulkar et al.,2015,abstract,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": ""Ambulkar et al."", ""year"": ""2015"", ""title"": """", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""The purpose is to replicate and extend Ambulkar et al.’s (2015) work testing resource reconfiguration as a mediator of the supply chain disruption/firm resilience relationship and testing risk management infrastructure as a moderator."", ""outcome"": ""mixed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""Two of four of Ambulkars et al.’s (2015) hypotheses were confirmed as relevant to firm resilience during the pandemic while two were not confirmed."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""abstract"", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Explicit partial confirmation of the four hypotheses from a single original paper. Original reference citation is unavailable in abstract.""}"
10.1177/0022022110381114,3531eb735322f77d,1,,"Self-awareness and other-awareness II: Mirror self-recognition, social contingency awareness, and synchronic imitation.","Asendorpf, J. B., Warkentin, V., & Baudonnière, P.- M.",1996,full_text,,error,argon,"{""target_index"": 1, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""verdict"": ""blind_right"", ""corrected_value"": {""authors"": ""Asendorpf, J. B., Warkentin, V., & Baudonnière, P.- M."", ""year"": ""1996"", ""title"": ""Self-awareness and other-awareness II: Mirror self-recognition, social contingency awareness, and synchronic imitation."", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": ""1, 2"", ""type"": ""replication"", ""record_type"": ""replication"", ""outcome"": ""mixed""}, ""quote"": ""Numerous developmental studies of predominantly urban Western middle-class children suggest that children reach this milestone by 18 to 24 months of age. By 18 months, approximately 50% pass and by 24 months a significant majority of children (more than 70%) are reported to self- refer by touching or removing a mark from their own body (face or other body region) while exploring their mirror image (Amsterdam, 1972; Asendorpf, Warkentin, & Baudonnière, 1996; Bard, Todd, Bernier, Love, & Leavens, 2006; Bertenthal & Fisher, 1978; Lewis & Brooks-Gunn, 1979; Lewis & Ramsay, 2004; Nielsen, Dissanayake, & Kashima, 2003; Schulman & Kaplowitz, 1976). Here we report cross-cultural observations on the emergence of mirror self-recognition (MSR), using a simplified mark test, raising questions regarding the meaning and universal validity of this test as an ontogenetic benchmark of self-concept."", ""location"": ""introduction, p. 2"", ""reason"": ""The abstract states that the study attempted to replicate across cultures the empirical finding that Western children show mirror self-recognition at 18 to 24 months, and the introduction explicitly attributes that specific quantitative benchmark (~50% passing by 18 months and >70% by 24 months) to this eight-study set rather than citing it as generic background. Whereas Amsterdam (1972) is singled out in the Method section only as the source of the original linguistic mark-test paradigm, Experiments 1 and 2 re-test the developmental benchmark finding reported by Asendorpf et al. (1996) regardless of its single in-text citation.""}","{""authors"": ""Asendorpf, J. B., Warkentin, V., & Baudonnière, P.- M."", ""year"": ""1996"", ""title"": ""Self-awareness and other-awareness II: Mirror self-recognition, social contingency awareness, and synchronic imitation."", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": ""1, 2"", ""evidence_quote"": ""Numerous developmental studies of predominantly urban Western middle-class children suggest that children reach this milestone by 18 to 24 months of age. By 18 months, approximately 50% pass and by 24 months a significant majority of children (more than 70%) are reported to self- refer by touching or removing a mark from their own body (face or other body region) while exploring their mirror image (Amsterdam, 1972; Asendorpf, Warkentin, & Baudonnière, 1996; Bard, Todd, Bernier, Love, & Leavens, 2006; Bertenthal & Fisher, 1978; Lewis & Brooks-Gunn, 1979; Lewis & Ramsay, 2004; Nielsen, Dissanayake, & Kashima, 2003; Schulman & Kaplowitz, 1976). Here we report cross-cultural observations on the emergence of mirror self-recognition (MSR), using a simplified mark test, raising questions regarding the meaning and universal validity of this test as an ontogenetic benchmark of self-concept. The basic rationale behind the mark test is that when a child is marked surreptitiously and attempts to touch the mark on the body, as opposed to doing nothing or trying to reach for it on the mirror surface, it indicates that the specular image stands for the child’s own body. Self-oriented gestures are taken as the behavioral index of self-recognition, indicating that the embodied self is the referent of what is seen in the mirror (Rochat, 2003). In a different account, Nielsen, Suddendorf, and Slaughter (2006) claim that the mark test is passed by individuals who have a rapidly updatable image of themselves that they recognize in the mirror. Regardless of the rela- tive validity of these accounts, the general consensus is that passing the mark test indicates that children recognize their image and that those who fail the test do not. From a comparative perspective, chimpanzees, orangutans, dolphins, and Asian elephants are among the few nonhuman species reported to pass the mark test (Gallup, 1982; Plotnik & de Waal, 2006; Povinelli, 1995; Reiss & Marino, 1998). Nonhuman individuals passing the test typically have extensive prior experience with reflective surfaces. In contrast, research with humans suggests that children’s relative familiarity with mirrors, which greatly varies across contexts, does not correlate with the age at which the mark test is passed (Priel & deSchonen, 1986). Priel and deShonen (1986) tested Bedouin nomadic children with no previous mirror experience and compared them to same-age Israeli children familiar with mirrors. They found no significant difference in the developmental onset of MSR between mirror familiar and unfa- miliar children. However, more recent cross-cultural studies point to significant cultural variations in the onset of MSR. Keller and collaborators compared 18- to 20-month-olds from urban Greece, Costa Rica, Germany, as well as from a rural community in Cameroon, and they report a greater proportion of German, Greek, and Costa Rican children passing the test (more than 50%), compared to Cameroonian children (less than 4%) (Keller, Kartner, Borke, Yovsi, & Kleis, 2005; Keller et al., 2004). These authors correlate such variations to variations in par- enting strategies that exist across these cultures, fostering more or less autonomy in the young child. If such variability is validated with other cultures, testing children older than 18 to 20 months would allow us to determine whether this variability pertains only to a developmental delay, pos- sibly resulting from variations in parenting styles (see Keller et al., 2005; Keller et al., 2004) or whether such differences persist throughout development. If the latter is the case, this would sug- gest that there may be culture-specific responses to the passing of the mirror mark test (i.e., greater occurrence of potential false negatives). To probe this alternative explanation, we per- formed two experiments testing (a) whether cross-cultural variability in the passing of the mirror mark test could be verified across more cultures and (b) whether such variability would persist beyond the age at which children typically are reported to pass the mirror mark test. In two experiments, we tested children 18 to 72 months old living in seven distinct cultural groups. Because of the exploratory nature of this work, aside from probing these questions, no specific hypotheses were made."", ""outcome"": ""mixed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""The second experiment demonstrates that a significant amount of variability exists across cultural contexts in children’s behavioral response to this version of the MSR test that measures spontaneous self-recognition (see also Lewis & Ramsey, 2004). Consistent with past research, 77% to 88% of Western children (Canada and United States) pass this version of the MSR test by spontaneously displaying self-oriented behaviors. In contrast, only half of the children from Saint Lucia, Grenada, or Peru and none of the Fijian children of the same age showed any sign of such behaviors."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Western samples agree with the cited developmental benchmark; non-Western samples explicitly contrast with it. No overall replication verdict supersedes these comparisons.""}"
10.1177/0022022110381114,3531eb735322f77d,2,,Self-awareness in human and chimpanzee infants: What is measured and what is meant by the mark and mirror test?,"Bard, K. A., Todd, B. K., Bernier, C., Love, J., & Leavens, D. A.",2006,full_text,,error,argon,"{""target_index"": 2, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""verdict"": ""blind_right"", ""corrected_value"": {""authors"": ""Bard, K. A., Todd, B. K., Bernier, C., Love, J., & Leavens, D. A."", ""year"": ""2006"", ""title"": ""Self-awareness in human and chimpanzee infants: What is measured and what is meant by the mark and mirror test?"", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": ""1, 2"", ""type"": ""replication"", ""record_type"": ""replication"", ""outcome"": ""mixed""}, ""quote"": ""Numerous developmental studies of predominantly urban Western middle-class children suggest that children reach this milestone by 18 to 24 months of age. By 18 months, approximately 50% pass and by 24 months a significant majority of children (more than 70%) are reported to self- refer by touching or removing a mark from their own body (face or other body region) while exploring their mirror image (Amsterdam, 1972; Asendorpf, Warkentin, & Baudonnière, 1996; Bard, Todd, Bernier, Love, & Leavens, 2006; Bertenthal & Fisher, 1978; Lewis & Brooks-Gunn, 1979; Lewis & Ramsay, 2004; Nielsen, Dissanayake, & Kashima, 2003; Schulman & Kaplowitz, 1976). Here we report cross-cultural observations on the emergence of mirror self-recognition (MSR), using a simplified mark test, raising questions regarding the meaning and universal validity of this test as an ontogenetic benchmark of self-concept."", ""location"": ""introduction, p. 2"", ""reason"": ""Bard et al. (2006) is explicitly cited as one of the empirical studies establishing the 18-to-24-month Western mirror self-recognition pass-rate benchmark that the abstract and introduction state the present study attempts to replicate across cultures. Treating Amsterdam (1972) alone as the target confuses the source of the classic linguistic mark-test procedure with the attributed developmental benchmark finding re-tested in Experiments 1 and 2.""}","{""authors"": ""Bard, K. A., Todd, B. K., Bernier, C., Love, J., & Leavens, D. A."", ""year"": ""2006"", ""title"": ""Self-awareness in human and chimpanzee infants: What is measured and what is meant by the mark and mirror test?"", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": ""1, 2"", ""evidence_quote"": ""Numerous developmental studies of predominantly urban Western middle-class children suggest that children reach this milestone by 18 to 24 months of age. By 18 months, approximately 50% pass and by 24 months a significant majority of children (more than 70%) are reported to self- refer by touching or removing a mark from their own body (face or other body region) while exploring their mirror image (Amsterdam, 1972; Asendorpf, Warkentin, & Baudonnière, 1996; Bard, Todd, Bernier, Love, & Leavens, 2006; Bertenthal & Fisher, 1978; Lewis & Brooks-Gunn, 1979; Lewis & Ramsay, 2004; Nielsen, Dissanayake, & Kashima, 2003; Schulman & Kaplowitz, 1976). Here we report cross-cultural observations on the emergence of mirror self-recognition (MSR), using a simplified mark test, raising questions regarding the meaning and universal validity of this test as an ontogenetic benchmark of self-concept. The basic rationale behind the mark test is that when a child is marked surreptitiously and attempts to touch the mark on the body, as opposed to doing nothing or trying to reach for it on the mirror surface, it indicates that the specular image stands for the child’s own body. Self-oriented gestures are taken as the behavioral index of self-recognition, indicating that the embodied self is the referent of what is seen in the mirror (Rochat, 2003). In a different account, Nielsen, Suddendorf, and Slaughter (2006) claim that the mark test is passed by individuals who have a rapidly updatable image of themselves that they recognize in the mirror. Regardless of the rela- tive validity of these accounts, the general consensus is that passing the mark test indicates that children recognize their image and that those who fail the test do not. From a comparative perspective, chimpanzees, orangutans, dolphins, and Asian elephants are among the few nonhuman species reported to pass the mark test (Gallup, 1982; Plotnik & de Waal, 2006; Povinelli, 1995; Reiss & Marino, 1998). Nonhuman individuals passing the test typically have extensive prior experience with reflective surfaces. In contrast, research with humans suggests that children’s relative familiarity with mirrors, which greatly varies across contexts, does not correlate with the age at which the mark test is passed (Priel & deSchonen, 1986). Priel and deShonen (1986) tested Bedouin nomadic children with no previous mirror experience and compared them to same-age Israeli children familiar with mirrors. They found no significant difference in the developmental onset of MSR between mirror familiar and unfa- miliar children. However, more recent cross-cultural studies point to significant cultural variations in the onset of MSR. Keller and collaborators compared 18- to 20-month-olds from urban Greece, Costa Rica, Germany, as well as from a rural community in Cameroon, and they report a greater proportion of German, Greek, and Costa Rican children passing the test (more than 50%), compared to Cameroonian children (less than 4%) (Keller, Kartner, Borke, Yovsi, & Kleis, 2005; Keller et al., 2004). These authors correlate such variations to variations in par- enting strategies that exist across these cultures, fostering more or less autonomy in the young child. If such variability is validated with other cultures, testing children older than 18 to 20 months would allow us to determine whether this variability pertains only to a developmental delay, pos- sibly resulting from variations in parenting styles (see Keller et al., 2005; Keller et al., 2004) or whether such differences persist throughout development. If the latter is the case, this would sug- gest that there may be culture-specific responses to the passing of the mirror mark test (i.e., greater occurrence of potential false negatives). To probe this alternative explanation, we per- formed two experiments testing (a) whether cross-cultural variability in the passing of the mirror mark test could be verified across more cultures and (b) whether such variability would persist beyond the age at which children typically are reported to pass the mirror mark test. In two experiments, we tested children 18 to 72 months old living in seven distinct cultural groups. Because of the exploratory nature of this work, aside from probing these questions, no specific hypotheses were made."", ""outcome"": ""mixed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""The second experiment demonstrates that a significant amount of variability exists across cultural contexts in children’s behavioral response to this version of the MSR test that measures spontaneous self-recognition (see also Lewis & Ramsey, 2004). Consistent with past research, 77% to 88% of Western children (Canada and United States) pass this version of the MSR test by spontaneously displaying self-oriented behaviors. In contrast, only half of the children from Saint Lucia, Grenada, or Peru and none of the Fijian children of the same age showed any sign of such behaviors."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Western samples agree with the cited developmental benchmark; non-Western samples explicitly contrast with it. No overall replication verdict supersedes these comparisons.""}"
10.1177/0022022110381114,3531eb735322f77d,3,,Development of self-recognition in the infant.,"Bertenthal, B., & Fisher, K.",1978,full_text,,error,argon,"{""target_index"": 3, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""verdict"": ""blind_right"", ""corrected_value"": {""authors"": ""Bertenthal, B., & Fisher, K."", ""year"": ""1978"", ""title"": ""Development of self-recognition in the infant."", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": ""1, 2"", ""type"": ""replication"", ""record_type"": ""replication"", ""outcome"": ""mixed""}, ""quote"": ""Numerous developmental studies of predominantly urban Western middle-class children suggest that children reach this milestone by 18 to 24 months of age. By 18 months, approximately 50% pass and by 24 months a significant majority of children (more than 70%) are reported to self- refer by touching or removing a mark from their own body (face or other body region) while exploring their mirror image (Amsterdam, 1972; Asendorpf, Warkentin, & Baudonnière, 1996; Bard, Todd, Bernier, Love, & Leavens, 2006; Bertenthal & Fisher, 1978; Lewis & Brooks-Gunn, 1979; Lewis & Ramsay, 2004; Nielsen, Dissanayake, & Kashima, 2003; Schulman & Kaplowitz, 1976). Here we report cross-cultural observations on the emergence of mirror self-recognition (MSR), using a simplified mark test, raising questions regarding the meaning and universal validity of this test as an ontogenetic benchmark of self-concept."", ""location"": ""introduction, p. 2"", ""reason"": ""Bertenthal and Fisher (1978) is attributed not only in the introduction's 18-to-24-month Western benchmark set that the paper attempts to replicate across cultures, but also in the Method and General Discussion sections as a direct source of the >60% (60% to 85%) pass-rate benchmark tested via the binomial analysis and contrasted with the cross-cultural results. Rejecting it in favor of Amsterdam (1972) confuses Amsterdam's role as the original paradigm source with the empirical benchmark findings directly re-tested here.""}","{""authors"": ""Bertenthal, B., & Fisher, K."", ""year"": ""1978"", ""title"": ""Development of self-recognition in the infant."", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": ""1, 2"", ""evidence_quote"": ""Numerous developmental studies of predominantly urban Western middle-class children suggest that children reach this milestone by 18 to 24 months of age. By 18 months, approximately 50% pass and by 24 months a significant majority of children (more than 70%) are reported to self- refer by touching or removing a mark from their own body (face or other body region) while exploring their mirror image (Amsterdam, 1972; Asendorpf, Warkentin, & Baudonnière, 1996; Bard, Todd, Bernier, Love, & Leavens, 2006; Bertenthal & Fisher, 1978; Lewis & Brooks-Gunn, 1979; Lewis & Ramsay, 2004; Nielsen, Dissanayake, & Kashima, 2003; Schulman & Kaplowitz, 1976). Here we report cross-cultural observations on the emergence of mirror self-recognition (MSR), using a simplified mark test, raising questions regarding the meaning and universal validity of this test as an ontogenetic benchmark of self-concept. The basic rationale behind the mark test is that when a child is marked surreptitiously and attempts to touch the mark on the body, as opposed to doing nothing or trying to reach for it on the mirror surface, it indicates that the specular image stands for the child’s own body. Self-oriented gestures are taken as the behavioral index of self-recognition, indicating that the embodied self is the referent of what is seen in the mirror (Rochat, 2003). In a different account, Nielsen, Suddendorf, and Slaughter (2006) claim that the mark test is passed by individuals who have a rapidly updatable image of themselves that they recognize in the mirror. Regardless of the rela- tive validity of these accounts, the general consensus is that passing the mark test indicates that children recognize their image and that those who fail the test do not. From a comparative perspective, chimpanzees, orangutans, dolphins, and Asian elephants are among the few nonhuman species reported to pass the mark test (Gallup, 1982; Plotnik & de Waal, 2006; Povinelli, 1995; Reiss & Marino, 1998). Nonhuman individuals passing the test typically have extensive prior experience with reflective surfaces. In contrast, research with humans suggests that children’s relative familiarity with mirrors, which greatly varies across contexts, does not correlate with the age at which the mark test is passed (Priel & deSchonen, 1986). Priel and deShonen (1986) tested Bedouin nomadic children with no previous mirror experience and compared them to same-age Israeli children familiar with mirrors. They found no significant difference in the developmental onset of MSR between mirror familiar and unfa- miliar children. However, more recent cross-cultural studies point to significant cultural variations in the onset of MSR. Keller and collaborators compared 18- to 20-month-olds from urban Greece, Costa Rica, Germany, as well as from a rural community in Cameroon, and they report a greater proportion of German, Greek, and Costa Rican children passing the test (more than 50%), compared to Cameroonian children (less than 4%) (Keller, Kartner, Borke, Yovsi, & Kleis, 2005; Keller et al., 2004). These authors correlate such variations to variations in par- enting strategies that exist across these cultures, fostering more or less autonomy in the young child. If such variability is validated with other cultures, testing children older than 18 to 20 months would allow us to determine whether this variability pertains only to a developmental delay, pos- sibly resulting from variations in parenting styles (see Keller et al., 2005; Keller et al., 2004) or whether such differences persist throughout development. If the latter is the case, this would sug- gest that there may be culture-specific responses to the passing of the mirror mark test (i.e., greater occurrence of potential false negatives). To probe this alternative explanation, we per- formed two experiments testing (a) whether cross-cultural variability in the passing of the mirror mark test could be verified across more cultures and (b) whether such variability would persist beyond the age at which children typically are reported to pass the mirror mark test. In two experiments, we tested children 18 to 72 months old living in seven distinct cultural groups. Because of the exploratory nature of this work, aside from probing these questions, no specific hypotheses were made."", ""outcome"": ""mixed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""The second experiment demonstrates that a significant amount of variability exists across cultural contexts in children’s behavioral response to this version of the MSR test that measures spontaneous self-recognition (see also Lewis & Ramsey, 2004). Consistent with past research, 77% to 88% of Western children (Canada and United States) pass this version of the MSR test by spontaneously displaying self-oriented behaviors. In contrast, only half of the children from Saint Lucia, Grenada, or Peru and none of the Fijian children of the same age showed any sign of such behaviors."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Western samples agree with the cited developmental benchmark; non-Western samples explicitly contrast with it. No overall replication verdict supersedes these comparisons.""}"
10.1177/0022022110381114,3531eb735322f77d,4,,Social cognition and the acquisition of self.,"Lewis, M., & Brooks-Gunn, J.",1979,full_text,,error,argon,"{""target_index"": 4, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""verdict"": ""blind_right"", ""corrected_value"": {""authors"": ""Lewis, M., & Brooks-Gunn, J."", ""year"": ""1979"", ""title"": ""Social cognition and the acquisition of self."", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": ""1, 2"", ""type"": ""replication"", ""record_type"": ""replication"", ""outcome"": ""mixed""}, ""quote"": ""Numerous developmental studies of predominantly urban Western middle-class children suggest that children reach this milestone by 18 to 24 months of age. By 18 months, approximately 50% pass and by 24 months a significant majority of children (more than 70%) are reported to self- refer by touching or removing a mark from their own body (face or other body region) while exploring their mirror image (Amsterdam, 1972; Asendorpf, Warkentin, & Baudonnière, 1996; Bard, Todd, Bernier, Love, & Leavens, 2006; Bertenthal & Fisher, 1978; Lewis & Brooks-Gunn, 1979; Lewis & Ramsay, 2004; Nielsen, Dissanayake, & Kashima, 2003; Schulman & Kaplowitz, 1976). Here we report cross-cultural observations on the emergence of mirror self-recognition (MSR), using a simplified mark test, raising questions regarding the meaning and universal validity of this test as an ontogenetic benchmark of self-concept."", ""location"": ""introduction, p. 2"", ""reason"": ""Lewis and Brooks-Gunn (1979) is directly cited for the 18-to-24-month Western empirical benchmark in the introduction, for the .60 binomial test threshold in the Method section, and as an explicit comparison in both the Experiment 1 results and General Discussion. Its reported developmental finding is re-tested on the same footing as Amsterdam (1972), which is distinguished only as the source of the original linguistic mark-test procedure.""}","{""authors"": ""Lewis, M., & Brooks-Gunn, J."", ""year"": ""1979"", ""title"": ""Social cognition and the acquisition of self."", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": ""1, 2"", ""evidence_quote"": ""Numerous developmental studies of predominantly urban Western middle-class children suggest that children reach this milestone by 18 to 24 months of age. By 18 months, approximately 50% pass and by 24 months a significant majority of children (more than 70%) are reported to self- refer by touching or removing a mark from their own body (face or other body region) while exploring their mirror image (Amsterdam, 1972; Asendorpf, Warkentin, & Baudonnière, 1996; Bard, Todd, Bernier, Love, & Leavens, 2006; Bertenthal & Fisher, 1978; Lewis & Brooks-Gunn, 1979; Lewis & Ramsay, 2004; Nielsen, Dissanayake, & Kashima, 2003; Schulman & Kaplowitz, 1976). Here we report cross-cultural observations on the emergence of mirror self-recognition (MSR), using a simplified mark test, raising questions regarding the meaning and universal validity of this test as an ontogenetic benchmark of self-concept. The basic rationale behind the mark test is that when a child is marked surreptitiously and attempts to touch the mark on the body, as opposed to doing nothing or trying to reach for it on the mirror surface, it indicates that the specular image stands for the child’s own body. Self-oriented gestures are taken as the behavioral index of self-recognition, indicating that the embodied self is the referent of what is seen in the mirror (Rochat, 2003). In a different account, Nielsen, Suddendorf, and Slaughter (2006) claim that the mark test is passed by individuals who have a rapidly updatable image of themselves that they recognize in the mirror. Regardless of the rela- tive validity of these accounts, the general consensus is that passing the mark test indicates that children recognize their image and that those