guistic mark-test procedure adopted here. It is therefore a directly re-tested empirical benchmark rather than a method-only or generic background citation.""}","{""authors"": ""Nielsen, M., Dissanayake, C., & Kashima, Y."", ""year"": ""2003"", ""title"": ""A longitudinal investigation of self-other discrimination and the emergence of mirror self-recognition."", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": ""1, 2"", ""evidence_quote"": ""Numerous developmental studies of predominantly urban Western middle-class children suggest that children reach this milestone by 18 to 24 months of age. By 18 months, approximately 50% pass and by 24 months a significant majority of children (more than 70%) are reported to self- refer by touching or removing a mark from their own body (face or other body region) while exploring their mirror image (Amsterdam, 1972; Asendorpf, Warkentin, & Baudonnière, 1996; Bard, Todd, Bernier, Love, & Leavens, 2006; Bertenthal & Fisher, 1978; Lewis & Brooks-Gunn, 1979; Lewis & Ramsay, 2004; Nielsen, Dissanayake, & Kashima, 2003; Schulman & Kaplowitz, 1976). Here we report cross-cultural observations on the emergence of mirror self-recognition (MSR), using a simplified mark test, raising questions regarding the meaning and universal validity of this test as an ontogenetic benchmark of self-concept. The basic rationale behind the mark test is that when a child is marked surreptitiously and attempts to touch the mark on the body, as opposed to doing nothing or trying to reach for it on the mirror surface, it indicates that the specular image stands for the child’s own body. Self-oriented gestures are taken as the behavioral index of self-recognition, indicating that the embodied self is the referent of what is seen in the mirror (Rochat, 2003). In a different account, Nielsen, Suddendorf, and Slaughter (2006) claim that the mark test is passed by individuals who have a rapidly updatable image of themselves that they recognize in the mirror. Regardless of the rela- tive validity of these accounts, the general consensus is that passing the mark test indicates that children recognize their image and that those who fail the test do not. From a comparative perspective, chimpanzees, orangutans, dolphins, and Asian elephants are among the few nonhuman species reported to pass the mark test (Gallup, 1982; Plotnik & de Waal, 2006; Povinelli, 1995; Reiss & Marino, 1998). Nonhuman individuals passing the test typically have extensive prior experience with reflective surfaces. In contrast, research with humans suggests that children’s relative familiarity with mirrors, which greatly varies across contexts, does not correlate with the age at which the mark test is passed (Priel & deSchonen, 1986). Priel and deShonen (1986) tested Bedouin nomadic children with no previous mirror experience and compared them to same-age Israeli children familiar with mirrors. They found no significant difference in the developmental onset of MSR between mirror familiar and unfa- miliar children. However, more recent cross-cultural studies point to significant cultural variations in the onset of MSR. Keller and collaborators compared 18- to 20-month-olds from urban Greece, Costa Rica, Germany, as well as from a rural community in Cameroon, and they report a greater proportion of German, Greek, and Costa Rican children passing the test (more than 50%), compared to Cameroonian children (less than 4%) (Keller, Kartner, Borke, Yovsi, & Kleis, 2005; Keller et al., 2004). These authors correlate such variations to variations in par- enting strategies that exist across these cultures, fostering more or less autonomy in the young child. If such variability is validated with other cultures, testing children older than 18 to 20 months would allow us to determine whether this variability pertains only to a developmental delay, pos- sibly resulting from variations in parenting styles (see Keller et al., 2005; Keller et al., 2004) or whether such differences persist throughout development. If the latter is the case, this would sug- gest that there may be culture-specific responses to the passing of the mirror mark test (i.e., greater occurrence of potential false negatives). To probe this alternative explanation, we per- formed two experiments testing (a) whether cross-cultural variability in the passing of the mirror mark test could be verified across more cultures and (b) whether such variability would persist beyond the age at which children typically are reported to pass the mirror mark test. In two experiments, we tested children 18 to 72 months old living in seven distinct cultural groups. Because of the exploratory nature of this work, aside from probing these questions, no specific hypotheses were made."", ""outcome"": ""mixed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""The second experiment demonstrates that a significant amount of variability exists across cultural contexts in children’s behavioral response to this version of the MSR test that measures spontaneous self-recognition (see also Lewis & Ramsey, 2004). Consistent with past research, 77% to 88% of Western children (Canada and United States) pass this version of the MSR test by spontaneously displaying self-oriented behaviors. In contrast, only half of the children from Saint Lucia, Grenada, or Peru and none of the Fijian children of the same age showed any sign of such behaviors."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Western samples agree with the cited developmental benchmark; non-Western samples explicitly contrast with it. No overall replication verdict supersedes these comparisons.""}"
10.1177/0022022110381114,3531eb735322f77d,7,,Mirror-image response during the first two years of life.,"Schulman, A. H., & Kaplowitz, C.",1976,full_text,,error,argon,"{""target_index"": 7, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""verdict"": ""blind_right"", ""corrected_value"": {""authors"": ""Schulman, A. H., & Kaplowitz, C."", ""year"": ""1976"", ""title"": ""Mirror-image response during the first two years of life."", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": ""1, 2"", ""type"": ""replication"", ""record_type"": ""replication"", ""outcome"": ""mixed""}, ""quote"": ""Numerous developmental studies of predominantly urban Western middle-class children suggest that children reach this milestone by 18 to 24 months of age. By 18 months, approximately 50% pass and by 24 months a significant majority of children (more than 70%) are reported to self- refer by touching or removing a mark from their own body (face or other body region) while exploring their mirror image (Amsterdam, 1972; Asendorpf, Warkentin, & Baudonnière, 1996; Bard, Todd, Bernier, Love, & Leavens, 2006; Bertenthal & Fisher, 1978; Lewis & Brooks-Gunn, 1979; Lewis & Ramsay, 2004; Nielsen, Dissanayake, & Kashima, 2003; Schulman & Kaplowitz, 1976). Here we report cross-cultural observations on the emergence of mirror self-recognition (MSR), using a simplified mark test, raising questions regarding the meaning and universal validity of this test as an ontogenetic benchmark of self-concept."", ""location"": ""introduction, p. 2"", ""reason"": ""The abstract and introduction explicitly frame the study as an attempt to replicate across cultures the specific empirical benchmark that Western children pass the mirror mark test by 18 to 24 months (~50% at 18 months and >70% at 24 months), attributing that finding to the eight-study set including Schulman and Kaplowitz (1976). Judging by study relationship rather than citation frequency or Amsterdam's (1972) role as paradigm source, Schulman and Kaplowitz (1976) is one of the attributed benchmark studies re-tested by Experiments 1 and 2.""}","{""authors"": ""Schulman, A. H., & Kaplowitz, C."", ""year"": ""1976"", ""title"": ""Mirror-image response during the first two years of life."", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": ""1, 2"", ""evidence_quote"": ""Numerous developmental studies of predominantly urban Western middle-class children suggest that children reach this milestone by 18 to 24 months of age. By 18 months, approximately 50% pass and by 24 months a significant majority of children (more than 70%) are reported to self- refer by touching or removing a mark from their own body (face or other body region) while exploring their mirror image (Amsterdam, 1972; Asendorpf, Warkentin, & Baudonnière, 1996; Bard, Todd, Bernier, Love, & Leavens, 2006; Bertenthal & Fisher, 1978; Lewis & Brooks-Gunn, 1979; Lewis & Ramsay, 2004; Nielsen, Dissanayake, & Kashima, 2003; Schulman & Kaplowitz, 1976). Here we report cross-cultural observations on the emergence of mirror self-recognition (MSR), using a simplified mark test, raising questions regarding the meaning and universal validity of this test as an ontogenetic benchmark of self-concept. The basic rationale behind the mark test is that when a child is marked surreptitiously and attempts to touch the mark on the body, as opposed to doing nothing or trying to reach for it on the mirror surface, it indicates that the specular image stands for the child’s own body. Self-oriented gestures are taken as the behavioral index of self-recognition, indicating that the embodied self is the referent of what is seen in the mirror (Rochat, 2003). In a different account, Nielsen, Suddendorf, and Slaughter (2006) claim that the mark test is passed by individuals who have a rapidly updatable image of themselves that they recognize in the mirror. Regardless of the rela- tive validity of these accounts, the general consensus is that passing the mark test indicates that children recognize their image and that those who fail the test do not. From a comparative perspective, chimpanzees, orangutans, dolphins, and Asian elephants are among the few nonhuman species reported to pass the mark test (Gallup, 1982; Plotnik & de Waal, 2006; Povinelli, 1995; Reiss & Marino, 1998). Nonhuman individuals passing the test typically have extensive prior experience with reflective surfaces. In contrast, research with humans suggests that children’s relative familiarity with mirrors, which greatly varies across contexts, does not correlate with the age at which the mark test is passed (Priel & deSchonen, 1986). Priel and deShonen (1986) tested Bedouin nomadic children with no previous mirror experience and compared them to same-age Israeli children familiar with mirrors. They found no significant difference in the developmental onset of MSR between mirror familiar and unfa- miliar children. However, more recent cross-cultural studies point to significant cultural variations in the onset of MSR. Keller and collaborators compared 18- to 20-month-olds from urban Greece, Costa Rica, Germany, as well as from a rural community in Cameroon, and they report a greater proportion of German, Greek, and Costa Rican children passing the test (more than 50%), compared to Cameroonian children (less than 4%) (Keller, Kartner, Borke, Yovsi, & Kleis, 2005; Keller et al., 2004). These authors correlate such variations to variations in par- enting strategies that exist across these cultures, fostering more or less autonomy in the young child. If such variability is validated with other cultures, testing children older than 18 to 20 months would allow us to determine whether this variability pertains only to a developmental delay, pos- sibly resulting from variations in parenting styles (see Keller et al., 2005; Keller et al., 2004) or whether such differences persist throughout development. If the latter is the case, this would sug- gest that there may be culture-specific responses to the passing of the mirror mark test (i.e., greater occurrence of potential false negatives). To probe this alternative explanation, we per- formed two experiments testing (a) whether cross-cultural variability in the passing of the mirror mark test could be verified across more cultures and (b) whether such variability would persist beyond the age at which children typically are reported to pass the mirror mark test. In two experiments, we tested children 18 to 72 months old living in seven distinct cultural groups. Because of the exploratory nature of this work, aside from probing these questions, no specific hypotheses were made."", ""outcome"": ""mixed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""The second experiment demonstrates that a significant amount of variability exists across cultural contexts in children’s behavioral response to this version of the MSR test that measures spontaneous self-recognition (see also Lewis & Ramsey, 2004). Consistent with past research, 77% to 88% of Western children (Canada and United States) pass this version of the MSR test by spontaneously displaying self-oriented behaviors. In contrast, only half of the children from Saint Lucia, Grenada, or Peru and none of the Fijian children of the same age showed any sign of such behaviors."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Western samples agree with the cited developmental benchmark; non-Western samples explicitly contrast with it. No overall replication verdict supersedes these comparisons.""}"
10.1177/0022022110381114,3531eb735322f77d,8,,Parenting styles and the development of the categorical self: A longitudinal study on mirror self-recognition in Cameroonian Nso and German families.,"Keller, H., Kartner, J., Borke, J., Yovsi, R., & Kleis, A.",2005,full_text,,correct,argon,"{""target_index"": 8, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""verdict"": ""shipped_right"", ""corrected_value"": null, ""quote"": ""However, more recent cross-cultural studies point to significant cultural variations in the onset of MSR. Keller and collaborators compared 18- to 20-month-olds from urban Greece, Costa Rica, Germany, as well as from a rural community in Cameroon, and they report a greater proportion of German, Greek, and Costa Rican children passing the test (more than 50%), compared to Cameroonian children (less than 4%) (Keller, Kartner, Borke, Yovsi, & Kleis, 2005; Keller et al., 2004)."", ""location"": ""introduction, p. 2"", ""reason"": ""Keller et al. (2005) is cited as prior cross-cultural context and for its contrasting parenting-style developmental-delay explanation, whereas the authors explicitly frame their study as replicating the classic Western 18- to 24-month mark-test benchmark (Amsterdam, 1972) across cultures and older ages.""}","{""authors"": ""Keller, H., Kartner, J., Borke, J., Yovsi, R., & Kleis, A."", ""year"": ""2005"", ""title"": ""Parenting styles and the development of the categorical self: A longitudinal study on mirror self-recognition in Cameroonian Nso and German families."", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": ""1, 2"", ""evidence_quote"": ""Numerous developmental studies of predominantly urban Western middle-class children suggest that children reach this milestone by 18 to 24 months of age. By 18 months, approximately 50% pass and by 24 months a significant majority of children (more than 70%) are reported to self- refer by touching or removing a mark from their own body (face or other body region) while exploring their mirror image (Amsterdam, 1972; Asendorpf, Warkentin, & Baudonnière, 1996; Bard, Todd, Bernier, Love, & Leavens, 2006; Bertenthal & Fisher, 1978; Lewis & Brooks-Gunn, 1979; Lewis & Ramsay, 2004; Nielsen, Dissanayake, & Kashima, 2003; Schulman & Kaplowitz, 1976). Here we report cross-cultural observations on the emergence of mirror self-recognition (MSR), using a simplified mark test, raising questions regarding the meaning and universal validity of this test as an ontogenetic benchmark of self-concept. The basic rationale behind the mark test is that when a child is marked surreptitiously and attempts to touch the mark on the body, as opposed to doing nothing or trying to reach for it on the mirror surface, it indicates that the specular image stands for the child’s own body. Self-oriented gestures are taken as the behavioral index of self-recognition, indicating that the embodied self is the referent of what is seen in the mirror (Rochat, 2003). In a different account, Nielsen, Suddendorf, and Slaughter (2006) claim that the mark test is passed by individuals who have a rapidly updatable image of themselves that they recognize in the mirror. Regardless of the rela- tive validity of these accounts, the general consensus is that passing the mark test indicates that children recognize their image and that those who fail the test do not. From a comparative perspective, chimpanzees, orangutans, dolphins, and Asian elephants are among the few nonhuman species reported to pass the mark test (Gallup, 1982; Plotnik & de Waal, 2006; Povinelli, 1995; Reiss & Marino, 1998). Nonhuman individuals passing the test typically have extensive prior experience with reflective surfaces. In contrast, research with humans suggests that children’s relative familiarity with mirrors, which greatly varies across contexts, does not correlate with the age at which the mark test is passed (Priel & deSchonen, 1986). Priel and deShonen (1986) tested Bedouin nomadic children with no previous mirror experience and compared them to same-age Israeli children familiar with mirrors. They found no significant difference in the developmental onset of MSR between mirror familiar and unfa- miliar children. However, more recent cross-cultural studies point to significant cultural variations in the onset of MSR. Keller and collaborators compared 18- to 20-month-olds from urban Greece, Costa Rica, Germany, as well as from a rural community in Cameroon, and they report a greater proportion of German, Greek, and Costa Rican children passing the test (more than 50%), compared to Cameroonian children (less than 4%) (Keller, Kartner, Borke, Yovsi, & Kleis, 2005; Keller et al., 2004). These authors correlate such variations to variations in par- enting strategies that exist across these cultures, fostering more or less autonomy in the young child. If such variability is validated with other cultures, testing children older than 18 to 20 months would allow us to determine whether this variability pertains only to a developmental delay, pos- sibly resulting from variations in parenting styles (see Keller et al., 2005; Keller et al., 2004) or whether such differences persist throughout development. If the latter is the case, this would sug- gest that there may be culture-specific responses to the passing of the mirror mark test (i.e., greater occurrence of potential false negatives). To probe this alternative explanation, we per- formed two experiments testing (a) whether cross-cultural variability in the passing of the mirror mark test could be verified across more cultures and (b) whether such variability would persist beyond the age at which children typically are reported to pass the mirror mark test. In two experiments, we tested children 18 to 72 months old living in seven distinct cultural groups. Because of the exploratory nature of this work, aside from probing these questions, no specific hypotheses were made."", ""outcome"": ""successful"", ""outcome_quote"": ""The second experiment demonstrates that a significant amount of variability exists across cultural contexts in children’s behavioral response to this version of the MSR test that measures spontaneous self-recognition (see also Lewis & Ramsey, 2004). Consistent with past research, 77% to 88% of Western children (Canada and United States) pass this version of the MSR test by spontaneously displaying self-oriented behaviors. In contrast, only half of the children from Saint Lucia, Grenada, or Peru and none of the Fijian children of the same age showed any sign of such behaviors."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Cross-cultural variability is confirmed.""}"
10.1177/0022022110381114,3531eb735322f77d,9,,Developmental consequences of early parenting experiences: Self-recognition and self-regulation in three cultural communities.,"Keller, H., Yovsi, R., Borke, J., Kartner, J., Henning, J., & Zaira, P.",2004,full_text,,correct,argon,"{""target_index"": 9, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""verdict"": ""shipped_right"", ""corrected_value"": null, ""quote"": ""However, more recent cross-cultural studies point to significant cultural variations in the onset of MSR. Keller and collaborators compared 18- to 20-month-olds from urban Greece, Costa Rica, Germany, as well as from a rural community in Cameroon, and they report a greater proportion of German, Greek, and Costa Rican children passing the test (more than 50%), compared to Cameroonian children (less than 4%) (Keller, Kartner, Borke, Yovsi, & Kleis, 2005; Keller et al., 2004)."", ""location"": ""introduction, p. 2"", ""reason"": ""Keller et al. (2004) is cited as motivating cross-cultural background and for its parenting-style explanation of MSR onset, not as the specific target finding re-tested by this study's mark-test experiments.""}","{""authors"": ""Keller, H., Yovsi, R., Borke, J., Kartner, J., Henning, J., & Zaira, P."", ""year"": ""2004"", ""title"": ""Developmental consequences of early parenting experiences: Self-recognition and self-regulation in three cultural communities."", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": ""1, 2"", ""evidence_quote"": ""Numerous developmental studies of predominantly urban Western middle-class children suggest that children reach this milestone by 18 to 24 months of age. By 18 months, approximately 50% pass and by 24 months a significant majority of children (more than 70%) are reported to self- refer by touching or removing a mark from their own body (face or other body region) while exploring their mirror image (Amsterdam, 1972; Asendorpf, Warkentin, & Baudonnière, 1996; Bard, Todd, Bernier, Love, & Leavens, 2006; Bertenthal & Fisher, 1978; Lewis & Brooks-Gunn, 1979; Lewis & Ramsay, 2004; Nielsen, Dissanayake, & Kashima, 2003; Schulman & Kaplowitz, 1976). Here we report cross-cultural observations on the emergence of mirror self-recognition (MSR), using a simplified mark test, raising questions regarding the meaning and universal validity of this test as an ontogenetic benchmark of self-concept. The basic rationale behind the mark test is that when a child is marked surreptitiously and attempts to touch the mark on the body, as opposed to doing nothing or trying to reach for it on the mirror surface, it indicates that the specular image stands for the child’s own body. Self-oriented gestures are taken as the behavioral index of self-recognition, indicating that the embodied self is the referent of what is seen in the mirror (Rochat, 2003). In a different account, Nielsen, Suddendorf, and Slaughter (2006) claim that the mark test is passed by individuals who have a rapidly updatable image of themselves that they recognize in the mirror. Regardless of the rela- tive validity of these accounts, the general consensus is that passing the mark test indicates that children recognize their image and that those who fail the test do not. From a comparative perspective, chimpanzees, orangutans, dolphins, and Asian elephants are among the few nonhuman species reported to pass the mark test (Gallup, 1982; Plotnik & de Waal, 2006; Povinelli, 1995; Reiss & Marino, 1998). Nonhuman individuals passing the test typically have extensive prior experience with reflective surfaces. In contrast, research with humans suggests that children’s relative familiarity with mirrors, which greatly varies across contexts, does not correlate with the age at which the mark test is passed (Priel & deSchonen, 1986). Priel and deShonen (1986) tested Bedouin nomadic children with no previous mirror experience and compared them to same-age Israeli children familiar with mirrors. They found no significant difference in the developmental onset of MSR between mirror familiar and unfa- miliar children. However, more recent cross-cultural studies point to significant cultural variations in the onset of MSR. Keller and collaborators compared 18- to 20-month-olds from urban Greece, Costa Rica, Germany, as well as from a rural community in Cameroon, and they report a greater proportion of German, Greek, and Costa Rican children passing the test (more than 50%), compared to Cameroonian children (less than 4%) (Keller, Kartner, Borke, Yovsi, & Kleis, 2005; Keller et al., 2004). These authors correlate such variations to variations in par- enting strategies that exist across these cultures, fostering more or less autonomy in the young child. If such variability is validated with other cultures, testing children older than 18 to 20 months would allow us to determine whether this variability pertains only to a developmental delay, pos- sibly resulting from variations in parenting styles (see Keller et al., 2005; Keller et al., 2004) or whether such differences persist throughout development. If the latter is the case, this would sug- gest that there may be culture-specific responses to the passing of the mirror mark test (i.e., greater occurrence of potential false negatives). To probe this alternative explanation, we per- formed two experiments testing (a) whether cross-cultural variability in the passing of the mirror mark test could be verified across more cultures and (b) whether such variability would persist beyond the age at which children typically are reported to pass the mirror mark test. In two experiments, we tested children 18 to 72 months old living in seven distinct cultural groups. Because of the exploratory nature of this work, aside from probing these questions, no specific hypotheses were made."", ""outcome"": ""successful"", ""outcome_quote"": ""The second experiment demonstrates that a significant amount of variability exists across cultural contexts in children’s behavioral response to this version of the MSR test that measures spontaneous self-recognition (see also Lewis & Ramsey, 2004). Consistent with past research, 77% to 88% of Western children (Canada and United States) pass this version of the MSR test by spontaneously displaying self-oriented behaviors. In contrast, only half of the children from Saint Lucia, Grenada, or Peru and none of the Fijian children of the same age showed any sign of such behaviors."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Cross-cultural variability is confirmed.""}"
10.1523/eneuro.0332-25.2025,5c0e372f71ca86f5,1,10.1126/science.7256273,Phenomenological space-time: toward an experiential relativity.,DeLong AJ,1981,full_text,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": ""DeLong AJ"", ""year"": ""1981"", ""title"": ""Phenomenological space-time: toward an experiential relativity."", ""doi"": ""10.1126/science.7256273"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": ""3"", ""evidence_quote"": ""Second, the size of the VR did not significantly impact the retrospective time estimation as would have been expected through other studies ( DeLong, 1981 ; Riemer et al., 2014 )."", ""outcome"": ""failed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""Second, the size of the VR did not significantly impact the retrospective time estimation as would have been expected through other studies ( DeLong, 1981 ; Riemer et al., 2014 ). Two differences can explain this incongruence: our study tested a different time scale and used a retrospective, not a prospective paradigm."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Experiment 3 explicitly tested whether environmental size affects time estimation and contrasts its null effect with this cited earlier result.""}"
10.1523/eneuro.0332-25.2025,5c0e372f71ca86f5,2,10.1007/s00221-014-3848-6,Interrelations between the perception of time and space in large-scale environments.,"Riemer M, Hölzl R, Kleinböhl D",2014,full_text,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": ""Riemer M, Hölzl R, Kleinböhl D"", ""year"": ""2014"", ""title"": ""Interrelations between the perception of time and space in large-scale environments."", ""doi"": ""10.1007/s00221-014-3848-6"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": ""3"", ""evidence_quote"": ""Second, the size of the VR did not significantly impact the retrospective time estimation as would have been expected through other studies ( DeLong, 1981 ; Riemer et al., 2014 )."", ""outcome"": ""failed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""Second, the size of the VR did not significantly impact the retrospective time estimation as would have been expected through other studies ( DeLong, 1981 ; Riemer et al., 2014 ). Two differences can explain this incongruence: our study tested a different time scale and used a retrospective, not a prospective paradigm."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The same explicit contrast identifies this independent earlier paper as a target of the environmental-size test.""}"
10.1111/lang.12403,a58225dce84de1c5,0,10.1016/j.neuropsychologia.2015.10.020,Tracking lexical consolidation with ERPs: Lexical and semantic-priming effects on N400 and LPC responses to newly-learned words.,"Bakker, I., Takashima, A., vanHell, J. G., Janzen, G., & McQueen, J. M.",2015,full_text,same_author_year_siblings,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": ""Bakker, I., Takashima, A., vanHell, J. G., Janzen, G., & McQueen, J. M."", ""year"": ""2015"", ""title"": ""Tracking lexical consolidation with ERPs: Lexical and semantic-priming effects on N400 and LPC responses to newly-learned words."", ""doi"": ""10.1016/j.neuropsychologia.2015.10.020"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""Taken together, the current study sought to conceptually replicate and extend the findings of Bakker et al. (2015), and investigate novel vocabulary learning in monolingual English speakers with minimal foreign language learning experience."", ""outcome"": ""mixed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""Both types of learners showed a LPC semantic priming effect (indexing controlled semantic retrieval), but no N400 effect (indexing at least partial automatic semantic retrieval), after a 24-hour consolidation period. The main difference is that only the experienced learners (Bakker et al., 2015) also showed a frontal LPC priming effect shortly after learning (for words trained and tested on Day 2)."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The authors explicitly compare agreement after 24 hours with absence of the original immediate LPC effect. No overall replication verdict overrides these comparisons.""}"
10.1371/journal.pone.0229161,b74519d198cc668b,1,10.1177/0361684315580125,From sex objects to human beings: Masking sexual body parts and humanization as moderators to women’s objectification,"Bernard, P., Gervais, S. J., Allen, J., Delmée, A., & Klein, O.",2015,full_text,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": ""Bernard, P., Gervais, S. J., Allen, J., Delmée, A., & Klein, O."", ""year"": ""2015"", ""title"": ""From sex objects to human beings: Masking sexual body parts and humanization as moderators to women’s objectification"", ""doi"": ""10.1177/0361684315580125"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": ""1, 2a"", ""evidence_quote"": ""We conducted three studies: a direct replication of Bernard and colleagues’ study [\n4\n], using their original materials, but with counterbalanced stimuli as in [\n5\n] (Study 1); a conceptual replication–to our knowledge the first one–with different sexualized materials to investigate whether the same pattern of results would emerge with different stimuli (Study 2a; indeed, so far most of the evidence in favor of the IBRT has emerged from research using the set of stimuli developed by Bernard and colleagues [\n4\n]); an investigation of the inversion effect with non-sexualized stimuli (Study 2b).\nWe expected (H1) stronger inversion effects for male than female targets and (H1b) that the participant’s gender would not moderate this difference between male and female targets. The confirmation of this hypothesis in Study 1 would be a direct replication of [\n5\n], and its confirmation in Study 2a would be a conceptual replication, enhancing its external validity beyond the specific stimuli. Study 2b tested a boundary condition, namely, whether differences in inversion effects between male and female targets would emerge also for non-sexualized stimuli."", ""outcome"": ""mixed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""Study 1 replicated the original results, showing a stronger inversion effect for male photos. However, no difference between male and female stimuli emerged in either Study 2a or 2b."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""abstract"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Direct replication agrees, conceptual replication fails; authors explicitly contrast these results. The validity critique does not remove the explicit close/conceptual mixed pattern.""}"
10.3389/fnhum.2022.937515,1b5f207ca572ce22,1,10.1113/jphysiol.2009.175885,Modulation of interhemispheric inhibition by volitional motor activity: an ipsilateral silent period study.,"Giovannelli F., Borgheresi A., Balestrieri F., Zaccara G., Viggiano M. P., Cincotta M., et al.",2009,full_text,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": ""Giovannelli F., Borgheresi A., Balestrieri F., Zaccara G., Viggiano M. P., Cincotta M., et al."", ""year"": ""2009"", ""title"": ""Modulation of interhemispheric inhibition by volitional motor activity: an ipsilateral silent period study."", ""doi"": ""10.1113/jphysiol.2009.175885"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""In fact, during the iSP paradigm we manipulated the activity of the contralateral hand, expecting to induce a stronger interhemispheric inhibition in the Contracted compared to the Relaxed condition (Giovannelli et al., 2009 ), which we did not observe."", ""outcome"": ""failed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""In fact, during the iSP paradigm we manipulated the activity of the contralateral hand, expecting to induce a stronger interhemispheric inhibition in the Contracted compared to the Relaxed condition (Giovannelli et al., 2009 ), which we did not observe. The null result on iSP modulation was surprising, considering the consistent effects reported previously throughout several manipulations of contralateral hand activity (Giovannelli et al., 2009 )."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The predicted published contraction effect was directly tested but not observed; the authors do not give a verdict that the attempt as a whole was incapable of adjudication.""}"
10.3102/0162373720948884,6f1653adad3488ee,1,,"Selection into Identification in Fixed Effects Models, with Application to Head Start","Miller, D. L., Shenhav, N. A., & Grosz, M. Z.",2019,full_text,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""outcome_computation"": ""computationally reproducible"", ""outcome_computational_quote"": ""These results closely replicate MSG (2019) findings and can be found in Table S16 of the online appendix."", ""outcome_robustness"": ""not checked"", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": ""We also performed the MSG (2019) reweighting-on-observables procedure for the Deming cohort sample. Similar to MSG (2019), we found reweighting attenuated the FFE estimates of Head Start for high school graduation, idleness, learning disability and poor health, but find little change on other young adult outcomes or ASI."", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""authors"": ""Miller, D. L., Shenhav, N. A., & Grosz, M. Z."", ""year"": ""2019"", ""title"": ""Selection into Identification in Fixed Effects Models, with Application to Head Start"", ""evidence_quote"": ""We also performed the MSG (2019) reweighting-on-observables procedure for the Deming cohort sample."", ""outcome"": """", ""outcome_quote"": """", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""results"", ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Table S16 directly compares MSG weighted estimates with their reproduced values. Reweighting is MSG finding being reproduced; no distinct alternative specifications testing MSG result are reported. New combined cohorts are independent extensions, not original-data robustness.""}"
