10.1080/00224545.2025.2482014,86b1eb2ebf5e0d8f,0,,,"Hall, Schwartz, and Duong",2021,abstract,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": ""Hall, Schwartz, and Duong"", ""year"": ""2021"", ""title"": """", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": ""2"", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""The current paper (N = 549) is a replication of Hall, Schwartz, and Duong (2021, Study 2) using a highly similar study procedure, with a small extension consisting of items from an emotional contagion scale."", ""outcome"": ""mixed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""We consider this to be a successful partial replication of Hall et al.’s (2021) findings."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""abstract"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The overall abstract verdict explicitly calls this a partial replication; the factor structure did not replicate but item rankings did.""}"
10.1080/146324400363491,1f20794bc92538d5,0,,,Cooper et al.,,abstract,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": ""Cooper et al."", ""year"": """", ""title"": """", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""This study tries to adhere to ideals such as Cooper et al's by replicating and extending a theory-driven study of the effects of initial conditions on new venture performance using a very large (700 + cases) and high quality, longitudinal data set."", ""outcome"": ""mixed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""On a conceptual level, the studies results confirm those obtained by Cooper et al . regarding how general human capital, management know-how and industry affect marginal survival probability, as well as concerning the effects of financial and general human capability on the likelihood of becoming a high performance venture. The results sometimes coincide also on a very detailed level, such as the differential effect of gender on marginal survival vs its effect on high performance. Other parts of Cooper et al's results could not be replicated."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""abstract"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Abstract explicitly confirms several findings and says other parts could not be replicated. Full citation not available in saved abstract; no reference inferred.""}"
10.1177/1042258720985478,2e9a4cb6c991a6eb,1,10.1111/etap.12165,Biology and selection into entrepreneurship: The relevance of prenatal testosterone exposure.,"Bönte, W., Procher, V. D., & Urbig, D.",2016,full_text,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": ""Bönte, W., Procher, V. D., & Urbig, D."", ""year"": ""2016"", ""title"": ""Biology and selection into entrepreneurship: The relevance of prenatal testosterone exposure."", ""doi"": ""10.1111/etap.12165"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""In an additional not preregistered exploratory analysis, we test for an association between 2D:4D and entrepreneurial intent in our data using a measure of entrepreneurial intent similar to one of the measures used by Bönte et al. (2016)."", ""outcome"": ""failed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""In an additional not preregistered exploratory analysis, we test for an association between 2D:4D and entrepreneurial intent in our data using a measure of entrepreneurial intent similar to one of the measures used by Bönte et al. (2016). We find no statistically significant or suggestive evidence of an association between 2D:4D and entrepreneurial intent in this analysis."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Exploratory new-sample retest of the reported digit-ratio/entrepreneurial-intent association; original significant negative association is described in the introduction, while this corresponding analysis finds none.""}"
10.1080/23273798.2026.2618581,d2f57b9fdad23ed5,0,,,,,abstract,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": """", ""year"": """", ""title"": """", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": """", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""Here, we aim to replicate this lenient listener effect for second-language speakers, and to determine whether this effect extends to speakers with a language disorder."", ""outcome"": ""successful"", ""outcome_quote"": ""The results replicate the lenient listener effect for second-language speakers and extend it to language disordered speakers."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""abstract"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Abstract explicitly states replication success; original citation cannot be identified from abstract alone.""}"
10.1007/s00426-026-02299-1,9b8d376ac7e0109c,1,,Attention shifts and the simon effect: A failure to replicate Stoffer (1991).,"Weeks, D. J., Chua, R., & Hamblin, K.",1996,full_text,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": ""Weeks, D. J., Chua, R., & Hamblin, K."", ""year"": ""1996"", ""title"": ""Attention shifts and the simon effect: A failure to replicate Stoffer (1991)."", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""Instead, Weeks et al. ( 1996 ) showed that target presentation (simultaneous or sequential) alone influenced the local Simon effect in a similar vein in both precue types (as shown by the three-way interaction between target presentation mode, relative stimulus position and response). Our current study replicated this finding of the dependency of the local Simon effect on target presentation."", ""outcome"": ""mixed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""In sum, Stoffer ( 1991 ) observed a reliable modulation of the local Simon effect under the sequential target presentation only for the two small boxes as precue (i.e., a four-way interaction between precue type, target presentation mode, relative stimulus position, and response), describing both, target presentation and precue type as mutually influencing factors for the local Simon effect. In contrast, the replications by Weeks et al. ( 1996 ) and our current study found no evidence for the reported mutual dependency. Instead, Weeks et al. ( 1996 ) showed that target presentation (simultaneous or sequential) alone influenced the local Simon effect in a similar vein in both precue types (as shown by the three-way interaction between target presentation mode, relative stimulus position and response). Our current study replicated this finding of the dependency of the local Simon effect on target presentation."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Authors explicitly replicate Weeks’s local effect dependency but contrast their concurrent global and local effects with Weeks’s absence of that concurrence."", ""outcome_additional_quote"": ""Substantial global and local Simon effects — reflecting the activation of two distinct spatial reference frames — occurred concurrently across both simultaneous and sequential target presentation. This contrasts with earlier reports by Stoffer ( 1991 ) and Weeks et al. ( 1996 ), where concurrent global and local effects were absent.""}"
10.1111/lang.12451,2193a7ee7fb9cefc,1,10.1017/S0272263197001010,"Accent, intelligibility, and comprehensibility: Evidence from four L1s.","Derwing, T. M., & Munro, M. J.",1997,abstract,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": ""Derwing, T. M., & Munro, M. J."", ""year"": ""1997"", ""title"": ""Accent, intelligibility, and comprehensibility: Evidence from four L1s."", ""doi"": ""10.1017/S0272263197001010"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""It conceptually replicated studies by Munro and Derwing (1995a) and Derwing and Munro (1997), who found partial independence among the three speech dimensions but also evidence that proficiency may mediate the relationship between linguistic features of stimuli (e.g., phonemic and grammatical error rates) and speech dimensions."", ""outcome"": ""cannot_be_determined"", ""outcome_quote"": """", ""outcome_quote_section"": """", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": false, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The abstract identifies the replicated studies but reports no comparison of the present findings to their results.""}"
10.15626/mp.2023.3960,1cdf99852c68cf23,1,10.1177/1948550619900570,Foregone opportunities and choosing not to act: Replications of inaction inertia effect.,"Chen, J., Hui, L. S., Yu, T., Feldman, G., Zeng, S., Ching, T. L., Ng, C. H., Wu, K. W., Yuen, C. M., Lau, T. K., Cheng, B. L., & Ng, K. W.",2021,full_text,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""authors"": ""Chen, J., Hui, L. S., Yu, T., Feldman, G., Zeng, S., Ching, T. L., Ng, C. H., Wu, K. W., Yuen, C. M., Lau, T. K., Cheng, B. L., & Ng, K. W."", ""year"": ""2021"", ""title"": ""Foregone opportunities and choosing not to act: Replications of inaction inertia effect."", ""doi"": ""10.1177/1948550619900570"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""Of note, our study also replicated findings by Chen et al. (2021), which is contemporary to ours, with a different population (mainly Americans assessed via Amazon Mechanical Turk)."", ""outcome"": ""mixed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""Our study shows evidence of the inaction inertia effect in the Brazilian population, partially replicating the original study and replicating very closely the study of Chen et al. (2021): half of the results (6 of 12 possibilities) of the original study were replicated Tykocinski et al. (1995), as shown in Table S10, and almost all results were replicated in comparison with Chen et al. (2021), as shown in Table 4, with the only exception of the frequent flyer scenario (Large-Control condition)."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The authors explicitly say they replicate this earlier replication, compare all scenarios directly in Table 4, and state almost all findings replicated with one specified exception. This is a target, rather than merely a background citation. The qualified overall verdict is mixed despite the close match; the conclusion sentence attesting replication of findings concerns the general presence of the effect, not all comparisons.""}"
