10.1016/j.jvb.2013.06.007,e92a7cc95ddcbf0a,0,,Rule breaking in adolescence and entrepreneurial status: An empirical investigation.,"Zhang, Z. & Arvey, R.D.",2009,abstract,same_author_year_siblings,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": ""Zhang, Z. & Arvey, R.D."", ""year"": ""2009"", ""title"": ""Rule breaking in adolescence and entrepreneurial status: An empirical investigation."", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""Drawing from Zhang and Arvey's retrospective study [Zhang, Z. & Arvey, R.D. (2009). Rule breaking in adolescence and entrepreneurial status: An empirical investigation. Journal of Business Venturing, 24(5), 436–447], which found a link between entrepreneurship status of male adults and their recalled early antisocial rule-breaking behavior in adolescence, the present study utilized prospective longitudinal data from a Swedish cohort study to clarify the connection between antisocial rule-breaking, crime, and entrepreneurship by applying a developmental perspective."", ""outcome"": ""successful"", ""outcome_quote"": ""Drawing from Zhang and Arvey's retrospective study [Zhang, Z. & Arvey, R.D. (2009). Rule breaking in adolescence and entrepreneurial status: An empirical investigation. Journal of Business Venturing, 24(5), 436–447], which found a link between entrepreneurship status of male adults and their recalled early antisocial rule-breaking behavior in adolescence, the present study utilized prospective longitudinal data from a Swedish cohort study to clarify the connection between antisocial rule-breaking, crime, and entrepreneurship by applying a developmental perspective. Regression results, which controlled for early socioeconomic background and intellectual competencies, indeed identified early antisocial rule-breaking behavior in adolescence as a valid positive predictor of a subsequent entrepreneurial career in adulthood in men (but not in women)."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""abstract"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Original male-adult finding and corresponding new longitudinal male-sample result are explicitly stated. Women and registered crime are extensions, not failures of the original male-sample finding.""}"
10.23668/psycharchives.22555,fff5229f57531090,1,10.1016/j.leaqua.2020.101384,"Careless responding in questionnaire measures: Detection, impact, and remedies.","Goldammer, P., Annen, H., Stöckli, P. L., & Klaus, J.",2020,full_text,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""authors"": ""Goldammer, P., Annen, H., Stöckli, P. L., & Klaus, J."", ""year"": ""2020"", ""title"": ""Careless responding in questionnaire measures: Detection, impact, and remedies."", ""doi"": ""10.1016/j.leaqua.2020.101384"", ""evidence_quote"": ""However, indicators targeting restricted or repetitive responding, such as longstring (Johnson, 2005), do not necessarily distinguish careful responders from careless responders equally well (Goldammer et al., 2020). A central challenge in evaluating such indicators is establishing a valid criterion for whether participants actually responded carelessly. In the present study, we addressed this problem by experimentally instructing participants to respond either truthfully or carelessly and separately monitoring their adherence to these instructions in real time."", ""outcome"": ""successful"", ""outcome_quote"": ""Longstring showed comparatively weak discrimination in the present study, particularly for overtly careless responding. This finding is consistent with Goldammer et al. (2020), who likewise reported weak performance of longstring under experimentally induced carelessness."", ""outcome_reasoning"": ""New experimentally induced careless responses test the published indicator discrimination finding, explicitly agreeing on weak longstring performance. General preregistered predictions about all indicators are not imported as contradictions of this specific original finding.""}"
10.1038/s41598-023-48874-z,84057613eb74c2bd,1,,Motivated encoding selectively promotes memory for future inconsequential semantically-related events.,"Oyarzún, J. P., Packard, P. A., de Diego-Balaguer, R. & Fuentemilla, L.",2016,full_text,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": ""Oyarzún, J. P., Packard, P. A., de Diego-Balaguer, R. & Fuentemilla, L."", ""year"": ""2016"", ""title"": ""Motivated encoding selectively promotes memory for future inconsequential semantically-related events."", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": ""1, 2b"", ""evidence_quote"": ""In Experiment 2b, we incorporated a post-conditioning phase to examine prospective memory enhancement effects, building on prior reports of this effect induced by reward-conditioning\n13\nand fear-conditioning\n7\n."", ""outcome"": ""failed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""Experiment 2b provided no evidence for prospective memory effects."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The prospective reward-enhancement finding is retested in the post-conditioning phases, with explicit absence of the effect in Experiments 1 and 2b. The fear-conditioning reference supplies related motivation rather than a retest of fear conditioning.""}"
10.1126/science.aac4716,9fed70371eeb17ff,58,,Social identity contingencies: how diversity cues signal threat or safety for African Americans in mainstream institutions,"Purdie-Vaughns, V., Steele, C. M., Davies, P. G., Ditlmann, R., & Crosby, J. R.",2008,full_text,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": ""Purdie-Vaughns, V., Steele, C. M., Davies, P. G., Ditlmann, R., & Crosby, J. R."", ""year"": ""2008"", ""title"": ""Social identity contingencies: how diversity cues signal threat or safety for African Americans in mainstream institutions"", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": ""2"", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""Replication of “Social identity contingencies: How diversity cues signal threat or safety for African Americans in mainstream institutions” by V Purdie-Vaughns, CM Steele, PG Davies, R Ditlmann, JR Crosby (2008, Journal of Personality and Social Psychology). Kathleen Schmidt kes7z@virginia.edu"", ""outcome"": ""failed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""Confirmatory analyses did not replicate the original finding that fairness cues create more trust for Black but not White participants in an environment with low-minority representation. Exploratory analyses showed a pattern of results in the opposite direction: White participants responded to high fairness cues by feeling more trust to a greater degree than Black participants. The interaction between race and fairness cue did not impact identity contingent evaluations. Data Preparation Data were transposed into short form and cleaned by recoding variables into the appropriate formats."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Individual primary replication report: authors’ verdict and comparisons to this original, prioritizing an overall verdict when stated.""}"
10.1126/science.aac4716,9fed70371eeb17ff,63,,Anchor precision influences the amount of adjustment,"Janiszewski, C., D. Uy.",2008,full_text,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": ""Janiszewski, C., D. Uy."", ""year"": ""2008"", ""title"": ""Anchor precision influences the amount of adjustment"", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": ""4"", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""Replication of Study 4b by Janiszewski & Uy (2008, Psychological Science) Jesse Chandler Princeton University"", ""outcome"": ""mixed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""These findings represent a partial replication of Janiszewski and Uy (2008). The central claim of their paper – that anchor precision influences the degree of adjustment was supported. Additionally, these data replicated the main effect of motivation observed in their study. There was no evidence of the reported interaction."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Individual primary replication report: authors’ verdict and comparisons to this original, prioritizing an overall verdict when stated.""}"
