10.1177/1948550616683017,ff70bf5ad942cddd,1,10.3102/0002831211410683,Implicit social cognitions predict sex differences in math engagement and achievement.,"Nosek, B. A., & Smyth, F. L.",2011,full_text,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": ""Nosek, B. A., & Smyth, F. L."", ""year"": ""2011"", ""title"": ""Implicit social cognitions predict sex differences in math engagement and achievement."", ""doi"": ""10.3102/0002831211410683"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""Nosek and Smyth (2011) compared the gender-science IAT and the explicit stereotype (measured with two self-reported items: Men are better at math than women are and Women can achieve as much as men in math) in predicting math-related outcomes. We re-analyzed that data and found only one variable, the difference between math and verbal SAT scores, that had reliably stronger relation with the IAT (r = .193, p < .001) than the explicit stereotype (r = .090, p = .004), Williams' t(1040) = 2.590, p = .010."", ""outcome"": """", ""outcome_quote"": """", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""results"", ""outcome_computation"": ""not checked"", ""outcome_computational_quote"": ""To refute that possibility, we returned to previous research that found an advantage of the IAT, and tested whether the evidence for this advantage persists even when controlling for self-reported liking."", ""outcome_robustness"": ""robustness challenges"", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": ""Importantly, when we computed partial correlations between each measure and the SAT, partialling out shared variance with attitudes, there was no longer reliable evidence for an IAT advantage: The IAT/SAT relation (r =.049, p = .115) was not significantly better than the explicit stereotype/SAT relation (r = .017, p = .584), Williams' t(1040) < 1, p = .442."", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Analysis directly tests a covariate-adjusted specification; it does not report an audit of recovery of original numerical results. The IAT advantage disappears with attitude adjustment.""}"
10.1016/j.jesp.2015.10.012,2cc1b3af21a3e6fb,3,,,"De Fruyt, Van De Wiele, & Van Heeringen",2000,full_text,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": ""De Fruyt, Van De Wiele, & Van Heeringen"", ""year"": ""2000"", ""title"": """", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""De Fruyt and colleagues (2000) investigated the relation between Cloninger’s Temperament and Character Dimensions (Cloninger, 1987) and the Big Five personality index. The researchers found that Cloninger-assessed persistence correlated with the Big Five trait conscientiousness, r(128) = .46, p < .001, d = 1.04, 95% CI = [.64, 1.43]."", ""outcome"": ""failed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""Across all replication studies (N = 3,193), there was little evidence for a relationship between conscientiousness measured with the TIPI and persistence measured with the unsolvable anagram task, r(3191) = .027, p = .134, 95% CI = [-0.008, 0.061], d = .05, 95% CI = [-.02, .12]."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""fulltext"", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""No De Fruyt entry appears in the supplied reference list; author names and year are supplied by the effect heading, title and DOI unavailable. Behavioral conceptual replication has no reliable predicted correlation.""}"
10.1016/j.jesp.2015.10.012,2cc1b3af21a3e6fb,9,,Moral credentials and the expression of prejudice.,"Monin, B., & Miller, D. T.",2001,full_text,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": ""Monin, B., & Miller, D. T."", ""year"": ""2001"", ""title"": ""Moral credentials and the expression of prejudice."", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": ""1"", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""Monin and Miller (2001) tested whether participants were more willing to express prejudicial attitudes when their prior behavior provided evidence that they were non-prejudiced."", ""outcome"": ""mixed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""In sum, the replications showed a similar main effect of the predicted credentials manipulation on making sexist judgments, but did not replicate the unexpected moderation by gender.14"", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""fulltext"", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": """"}"
10.1016/j.jesp.2015.10.012,2cc1b3af21a3e6fb,10,,Some participants may be better than others: Sustained attention and motivation are higher early in semester.,"Nicholls, M. E., Loveless, K. M., Thomas, N. A., Loetscher, T., & Churches, O.",2015,full_text,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": ""Nicholls, M. E., Loveless, K. M., Thomas, N. A., Loetscher, T., & Churches, O."", ""year"": ""2015"", ""title"": ""Some participants may be better than others: Sustained attention and motivation are higher early in semester."", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""The present study replicated and extended prior observations (Nicholls et al., 2014; Witt et al., 2011). As the semester progressed, participants reported slightly less effort and attention, were slightly more likely to fail an attention check, were slightly less conscientious, had slightly worse mood, had slightly higher stress, and had slightly higher representation of men compared to women."", ""outcome"": ""successful"", ""outcome_quote"": ""The present study replicated and extended prior observations (Nicholls et al., 2014; Witt et al., 2011). As the semester progressed, participants reported slightly less effort and attention, were slightly more likely to fail an attention check, were slightly less conscientious, had slightly worse mood, had slightly higher stress, and had slightly higher representation of men compared to women."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The authors explicitly identify these prior observations as replicated; Nicholls is dated 2014 in text and 2015 in the reference list.""}"
