10.1016/j.jesp.2020.103977,59d194699c6721bf,1,10.1038/scientificamerican0182-160,The psychology of preferences.,"Kahneman, D., & Tversky, A.",1982,full_text,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": ""Kahneman, D., & Tversky, A."", ""year"": ""1982"", ""title"": ""The psychology of preferences."", ""doi"": ""10.1038/scientificamerican0182-160"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""The classic action-effect (Kahneman & Tversky, 1982) findings were that actions leading to a negative outcome are regretted more than inactions leading to the same negative outcomes. We added a regret measure to examine whether the action-effect findings would extend to situations of morality involving intended harmful behavior. Our findings were opposite to the expected action-effect omission-commission asymmetry with participants rating omissions as more regretted than commissions (d = 0.18 to 0.26)."", ""outcome"": ""failed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""The classic action-effect (Kahneman & Tversky, 1982) findings were that actions leading to a negative outcome are regretted more than inactions leading to the same negative outcomes. We added a regret measure to examine whether the action-effect findings would extend to situations of morality involving intended harmful behavior. Our findings were opposite to the expected action-effect omission-commission asymmetry with participants rating omissions as more regretted than commissions (d = 0.18 to 0.26)."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The added regret measure explicitly tests generalization of this published action-effect finding to moral scenarios and finds the opposite pattern.""}"
