10.1016/j.cogdev.2017.10.004,63ba7fc3511ae27e,4,,Early theory of mind competencies: do infants understand others’ beliefs?,"Träuble, B., Marinović, V., & Pauen, S.",2010,full_text,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": ""Träuble, B., Marinović, V., & Pauen, S."", ""year"": ""2010"", ""title"": ""Early theory of mind competencies: do infants understand others’ beliefs?"", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": ""1"", ""evidence_quote"": ""We conducted close replications of studies by Knudsen and Liszkowski (2012) and Buttelmann et al. (2009), as well as two experiments based on published violation of expectation paradigms (Onishi & Baillargeon, 2005; Song, Onishi, Baillargeon & Fisher, 2008; Träuble, Marinović & Pauen, 2010), with procedural changes based on both our novel experimental goals and the original authors’ advice."", ""outcome"": ""failed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""On the other hand, we failed to reproduce evidence that 18-month-old infants’ expectations are violated when an experimenter who ought to have a false belief reaches for an object in its true location (e.g. Onishi & Baillargeon, 2005; Song et al., 2008; Träuble et al., 2010)."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Author verdict explicitly identifies this original; limitations do not replace that verdict.""}"
10.18718/81781.38,67732009dff134e5,1,10.1002/bdm.495,When Efortful Thinking Influences Judgmental Anchoring: Differential Effects of Forewarning and Incentives on Self-generated and Externally Provided Anchors,"Epley, N. & Gilovich, T.",2005,full_text,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": ""Epley, N. & Gilovich, T."", ""year"": ""2005"", ""title"": ""When Efortful Thinking Influences Judgmental Anchoring: Differential Effects of Forewarning and Incentives on Self-generated and Externally Provided Anchors"", ""doi"": ""10.1002/bdm.495"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": ""Study 2"", ""replication_study_numbers"": ""3"", ""evidence_quote"": ""In their Study 2, Epley and Gilovich (2005) found that warning participants that their adjustments\nfrom anchors were insufficient led to increases in adjustments from self-generated anchors but not\nfrom experimenter-provided anchors. We conducted a preregistered (https://osf.io/f5sj8) replica-\ntion of this study."", ""outcome"": ""failed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""Thus, we deem\nthe replication an informative failure: There is evidence against the hypothesis that forewarning\nmoderates adjustment from self-generated anchors."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The moderators forming the central finding did not replicate. General anchoring effects are checks of the paradigm, not a successful replication of the moderator claim.""}"
10.1080/15248372.2018.1544140,bac6178abc69a5fe,1,10.1111/j.2044-835X.2011.02046.x,Where will the triangle look for it? Attributing false beliefs to a geometric shape at 17 months,"Surian, L., & Geraci, A.",2012,full_text,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": ""Surian, L., & Geraci, A."", ""year"": ""2012"", ""title"": ""Where will the triangle look for it? Attributing false beliefs to a geometric shape at 17 months"", ""doi"": ""10.1111/j.2044-835X.2011.02046.x"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""The rationale of the present study was, therefore, to explore whether existing successful\nvs. unsuccessful replications of AL FB tasks may be due to diﬀerences in verbal narration.\nTo this end, we followed up on our previous large-scale replication attempts of the two AL\ntasks by Southgate et al. (2007) and by Surian and Geraci (2012) (Kulke, Reiß, Krist, &\nRakoczy, 2017; Kulke et al., 2018, 2018)."", ""outcome"": ""failed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""Crucially, however, even after adding verbal narration, original ﬁndings could still not\nreliably be replicated, particularly in the Surian and Geraci task—much in line with\nprevious large-scale failed replication attempts of anticipatory looking false belief tasks\n(Kulke et al., 2018)."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Target-specific discussion verdict: only FB1 replicated for Southgate; original belief-based looking failed for Surian and Geraci.""}"
10.1177/0956797617747090,41a25a0b35cfc34c,0,,Eye movements reveal sustained implicit processing of others’ mental states.,"Schneider, D., Bayliss, A. P., Becker, S. I., & Dux, P. E.",2012,full_text,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": ""Schneider, D., Bayliss, A. P., Becker, S. I., & Dux, P. E."", ""year"": ""2012"", ""title"": ""Eye movements reveal sustained implicit processing of others’ mental states."", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": ""1, 2"", ""evidence_quote"": ""In the original studies, 14-month-old children (Surian & Geraci, 2012), adults (Schneider et al., 2012), or both children from the age of 2 years and adults (Low & Watts, 2013; Senju et al., 2009; Southgate et al., 2007) revealed anticipatory-looking patterns suggestive of belief-based anticipation in both their first looks and overall looking time. The current study exactly replicated the methods described in the original articles, using original stimuli and protocols, which were generously shared with us by the authors (for details, see Supplement A in the Supplemental Material available online)."", ""outcome"": ""mixed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""The original results were thus only partially replicated: As in the original study, there was a significant Belief × Location interaction on looking times. But unlike in the original study, this was not due to the crucial difference in looking to the no-object location (participants looked longer to this location in the false-belief than in the truebelief condition)."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""results"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Authors explicitly call these results partially replicated: the interaction agreed but the crucial no-object contrast did not; Study 2 produced the same pattern.""}"
10.1177/0956797617747090,41a25a0b35cfc34c,1,,Where will the triangle look for it? Attributing false beliefs to a geometric shape at 17 months.,"Surian, L., & Geraci, A.",2012,full_text,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": ""Surian, L., & Geraci, A."", ""year"": ""2012"", ""title"": ""Where will the triangle look for it? Attributing false beliefs to a geometric shape at 17 months."", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": ""1"", ""evidence_quote"": ""In the original studies, 14-month-old children (Surian & Geraci, 2012), adults (Schneider et al., 2012), or both children from the age of 2 years and adults (Low & Watts, 2013; Senju et al., 2009; Southgate et al., 2007) revealed anticipatory-looking patterns suggestive of belief-based anticipation in both their first looks and overall looking time. The current study exactly replicated the methods described in the original articles, using original stimuli and protocols, which were generously shared with us by the authors (for details, see Supplement A in the Supplemental Material available online)."", ""outcome"": ""mixed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""The Kulke et al. present study thus replicated only the original negative findings but not the positive ones."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""results"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Authors explicitly distinguish replicated null findings from positive findings that did not replicate.""}"
10.1177/0956797617747090,41a25a0b35cfc34c,2,,Attributing false beliefs about object identity reveals a signature blind spot in humans’ efficient mind-reading system.,"Low, J., & Watts, J.",2013,full_text,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": ""Low, J., & Watts, J."", ""year"": ""2013"", ""title"": ""Attributing false beliefs about object identity reveals a signature blind spot in humans’ efficient mind-reading system."", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": ""1, 3a, 3b"", ""evidence_quote"": ""In the original studies, 14-month-old children (Surian & Geraci, 2012), adults (Schneider et al., 2012), or both children from the age of 2 years and adults (Low & Watts, 2013; Senju et al., 2009; Southgate et al., 2007) revealed anticipatory-looking patterns suggestive of belief-based anticipation in both their first looks and overall looking time. The current study exactly replicated the methods described in the original articles, using original stimuli and protocols, which were generously shared with us by the authors (for details, see Supplement A in the Supplemental Material available online)."", ""outcome"": ""statistically successful but flawed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""In sum, the original pattern of belief-congruent looking could be reproduced only under conditions in which the belief congruency of the locations is confounded with additional factors, and therefore, this pattern might not reflect belief-based anticipation."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""results"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The original materials yielded the effect, but the target-specific main message is that removing confounds eliminated belief-congruent anticipation and invalidated its interpretation as implicit theory of mind.""}"
10.1177/0956797617747090,41a25a0b35cfc34c,4,,Mindblind eyes: An absence of spontaneous theory of mind in Asperger syndrome.,"Senju, A., Southgate, V., White, S., & Frith, U.",2009,full_text,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": ""Senju, A., Southgate, V., White, S., & Frith, U."", ""year"": ""2009"", ""title"": ""Mindblind eyes: An absence of spontaneous theory of mind in Asperger syndrome."", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": ""1"", ""evidence_quote"": ""In the original studies, 14-month-old children (Surian & Geraci, 2012), adults (Schneider et al., 2012), or both children from the age of 2 years and adults (Low & Watts, 2013; Senju et al., 2009; Southgate et al., 2007) revealed anticipatory-looking patterns suggestive of belief-based anticipation in both their first looks and overall looking time. The current study exactly replicated the methods described in the original articles, using original stimuli and protocols, which were generously shared with us by the authors (for details, see Supplement A in the Supplemental Material available online)."", ""outcome"": ""failed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""The overall original findings were not replicated, whereas a differential analysis of the False Belief 1 and False Belief 2 conditions suggests that only the False Belief 1 condition could be replicated."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""results"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Authors explicitly group this original with Southgate 2007 and state an overall nonreplication verdict. The neurotypical anticipatory-looking result is the tested finding, rather than the autism group contrast.""}"
10.17605/osf.io/82bwv,92e046b18547f955,1,,,"Riesel, Weinberg, Moran, & Hajcak",2013,full_text,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": ""Riesel, Weinberg, Moran, & Hajcak"", ""year"": ""2013"", ""title"": """", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""Therefore, we attempted this same analysis strategy and performed a median split based on ERN amplitude and re-assessed the correlation between ERN and EPS magnitude in this subgroup of participants (see Figure 4)."", ""outcome"": ""successful"", ""outcome_quote"": ""Consistent with the Riesel et al. (2013) study, we found a significant correlation between ERN and EPS in this subgroup of participants (r = -.63, p < .01)."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Additional analysis explicitly re-tests the later published subgroup finding and reports agreement. The subsequent removal of an outlier is exploratory. Citation is inline only; title and DOI are absent.""}"
10.3389/fpsyg.2014.01276,3213132881105a19,1,10.1027/a000001,"Does cleanliness influence moral judgments? A direct replication of Schnall, Benton, and Harvey (2008).","Johnson D. J., Cheung F., Donnellan M. B.",2014a,full_text,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": ""Johnson D. J., Cheung F., Donnellan M. B."", ""year"": ""2014a"", ""title"": ""Does cleanliness influence moral judgments? A direct replication of Schnall, Benton, and Harvey (2008)."", ""doi"": ""10.1027/a000001"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": ""1"", ""replication_study_numbers"": ""Pilot Study 1"", ""evidence_quote"": ""First, I explored JCD’s Experiment 1 data to identify a low response effort subsample (\nn\n= 57) based on percentage of correct responses on sentence unscrambling task and found tentative support for the cleanliness hypothesis [\nd\ncomposite\n= -0.41, 95% CI (-0.94, 0.13), see Supplementary Material, Pilot Study 1]."", ""outcome"": """", ""outcome_quote"": """", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""introduction"", ""outcome_computation"": ""not checked"", ""outcome_computational_quote"": ""First, I explored JCD’s Experiment 1 data to identify a low response effort subsample (\nn\n= 57) based on percentage of correct responses on sentence unscrambling task and found tentative support for the cleanliness hypothesis [\nd\ncomposite\n= -0.41, 95% CI (-0.94, 0.13), see Supplementary Material, Pilot Study 1]."", ""outcome_robustness"": ""robustness challenges"", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": ""First, I explored JCD’s Experiment 1 data to identify a low response effort subsample (\nn\n= 57) based on percentage of correct responses on sentence unscrambling task and found tentative support for the cleanliness hypothesis [\nd\ncomposite\n= -0.41, 95% CI (-0.94, 0.13), see Supplementary Material, Pilot Study 1]."", ""outcome_confident"": false, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Exploratory low-effort subgroup reanalysis tentatively qualified the published null; numerical reproduction was outside the stated subgroup analysis.""}"
10.1037/xhp0000331,2bba722926999fc3,1,10.1037/a0024248,Taking a hands-on approach: Apparent grasping ability scales the perception of object size.,"Linkenauger S. A., Witt J. K., & Proffitt D. R.",2011,full_text,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": ""Linkenauger S. A., Witt J. K., & Proffitt D. R."", ""year"": ""2011"", ""title"": ""Taking a hands-on approach: Apparent grasping ability scales the perception of object size."", ""doi"": ""10.1037/a0024248"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": ""2, 3, 4, 5"", ""evidence_quote"": ""We focused on the claim made by Linkenauger et al. (2011) that right-handed observers estimate the size of objects they intend to grasp in their right hand as smaller than objects they intend to grasp in their left hand because they perceive the grasping capacity of their right hand as greater than that of their left hand."", ""outcome"": ""failed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""Thus we did not replicate the results of Linkenauger et al. (2011) because we failed to find the predicted influence of perceived action capacity on spatial perception."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Explicit overall nonreplication verdict for the focal object size finding; successful preliminary hand asymmetry checks do not override it.""}"
10.1371/journal.pone.0172636,1262c4f34ab4cc78,1,,"The retrospective gambler’s fallacy: Unlikely events, constructing the past, and multiple universes.","Oppenheimer DM, Monin B.",2009,full_text,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": ""Oppenheimer DM, Monin B."", ""year"": ""2009"", ""title"": ""The retrospective gambler’s fallacy: Unlikely events, constructing the past, and multiple universes."", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""To help indicate the overall quality of our replication we included as a positive control an additional experiment with a well-defined effect size. Specifically, we used the retrospective gambler’s task [ 11 ]."", ""outcome"": ""successful"", ""outcome_quote"": ""Our inability to detect a substantial decline in religious belief was not due to quality issues as we detected an expected effect in an additional positive control study with the same participants ( F (1,963) = 21.2, p < .001, Table 3 )."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""results"", ""outcome_confident"": true}"
10.1525/collabra.32964,8b1c966efb3f2f97,1,10.1026//0033-3042.52.3.131,Effekte des generischen Maskulinums und alternativer Sprachformen auf den gedanklichen Einbezug von Frauen [Effects of the generic masculinum and of alternative forms of expression on the mental inclusion of women].,"Stahlberg, D., & Sczesny, S.",2001,full_text,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": ""Stahlberg, D., & Sczesny, S."", ""year"": ""2001"", ""title"": ""Effekte des generischen Maskulinums und alternativer Sprachformen auf den gedanklichen Einbezug von Frauen [Effects of the generic masculinum and of alternative forms of expression on the mental inclusion of women]."", ""doi"": ""10.1026//0033-3042.52.3.131"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": ""Study 3"", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""The present high-powered and pre-registered study seeks to contribute to research on cognitive effects of masculine generics by replicating and extending one particular study, namely, a roughly 20-years old study on the effects of masculine generics in German speakers (Stahlberg et al., 2001, Study 2; Stahlberg & Sczesny, 2001, Study 3, is the same study)."", ""outcome"": ""mixed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""That is, for both the experimental condition that produced the lowest inclusion of women (i.e., masculine generics) and the highest inclusion of women (capital-I), the number of women listed by participants in the present study and by participants two decades ago did not differ statistically. Although not a true experimental test, this convergence in absolute numbers indicates that the changes regarding the visibility and increased power of women in society did not lead to an overall increased cognitive accessibility of women. Only for masculine-feminine word pairs, our results differed from the original study. In our replication, significantly more women were listed in this condition than in the masculine generics condition, whereas this contrast was not significant in the original study."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The authors confirm agreement for masculine generics and capital-I but explicitly contrast the word-pair result with the original: a previously nonsignificant contrast becomes significant. The introductory confirmation of the focal effect does not override this named disagreement. The two cited papers report the same original experiment, so the same comparisons apply to both.""}"
10.1016/j.appet.2013.12.020,98ff6e79db78401a,0,,,,,abstract,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": """", ""year"": """", ""title"": """", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": """", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": ""1, 2"", ""evidence_quote"": ""In addition, sugar containing drinks have been demonstrated to counteract this so-called ego-depletion effect, both when being ingested and when merely being sensed in the oral cavity. However, since the underlying evidence is less compelling than suggested, replications are crucially required."", ""outcome"": ""failed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""Despite applying powerful research designs, no effect of sugar sensing or ingestion on ego depletion could be detected."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""abstract"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The abstract states the original glucose restoration finding and reports no corresponding effect in either new experiment. Target identity and number of original papers cannot be recovered from the abstract.""}"
