symmetry is the selected claim tested.""}"
10.3758/s13414-015-0849-x,ae78ae9e5572ca14,3,10.3758/BF03195789,Evaluating the independence of sex and expression in judgments of faces.,"Le Gal, P. M., & Bruce, V.",2002,full_text,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": ""Le Gal, P. M., & Bruce, V."", ""year"": ""2002"", ""title"": ""Evaluating the independence of sex and expression in judgments of faces."", ""doi"": ""10.3758/BF03195789"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": ""1b, 2b"", ""evidence_quote"": ""Experiment\n2b\nwas designed to assess whether the same symmetrical pattern of results would emerge if a smaller set of individuals drawn from the new face set was used. On the basis of the results reported by Le Gal and Bruce (\n2002\n), Atkinson et al. (\n2005\n), Karnadewi and Lipp (\n2011\n), and in Experiment\n1b\n, no symmetrical interaction was expected."", ""outcome"": ""mixed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""Resembling the pattern seen in Experiment 1, Experiment\n2b\nfailed to replicate the symmetrical interaction between face sex and expression on expression and sex categorization seen for the larger picture set used in Experiment\n2a\n. Rather, an asymmetrical interaction was found, in that poser sex affected the categorization of emotional expressions, whereas expression did not affect the categorization of faces by sex. This pattern deviates from that seen in Experiment\n1b\n, in which no interaction was observed in either sex or expression categorization, but is consistent with the pattern of results reported in studies that had employed the Garner paradigm (Atkinson et al.,\n2005\n; Karnadewi & Lipp,\n2011\n)."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": false, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Experiment 1b agrees with the original independence result; Experiment 2b instead has an asymmetrical interaction, explicitly contrasted with 1b. Outcome uncertainty concerns whether the targeted claim is complete independence or just absence of symmetry.""}"
10.1037/xhp0000180,1724e3b3976272af,1,10.1016/j.visres.2008.02.021,Intertrial target-feature changes do not lead to more distraction by singletons: Target uncertainty does.,"Lamy, D., & Yashar, A.",2008,full_text,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": ""Lamy, D., & Yashar, A."", ""year"": ""2008"", ""title"": ""Intertrial target-feature changes do not lead to more distraction by singletons: Target uncertainty does."", ""doi"": ""10.1016/j.visres.2008.02.021"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": ""1"", ""evidence_quote"": ""Furthermore, RTs were shorter when the target shape was repeated than when it changed (881 vs. 915 ms), F(1, 31) ⫽ 21.18, p ⬍ .001, ␩p2 ⫽ 0.406, but there was no interaction with distractor presence (p ⫽ .505), confirming that repetition of the task-relevant shape feature did not affect interference from the distractor (Lamy & Yashar, 2008)."", ""outcome"": ""successful"", ""outcome_quote"": ""Furthermore, RTs were shorter when the target shape was repeated than when it changed (881 vs. 915 ms), F(1, 31) ⫽ 21.18, p ⬍ .001, ␩p2 ⫽ 0.406, but there was no interaction with distractor presence (p ⫽ .505), confirming that repetition of the task-relevant shape feature did not affect interference from the distractor (Lamy & Yashar, 2008)."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""results"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The task-relevant target-shape repetition analysis explicitly confirms the original null moderation with randomly mixed distractor presence.""}"
10.1037/xhp0000180,1724e3b3976272af,2,10.1037/a0027629,"Top-down control of attention: It’s gradual, practice-dependent, and hierarchically organized.","Zehetleitner, M., Goschy, H., & Müller, H. J.",2012,full_text,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": ""Zehetleitner, M., Goschy, H., & Müller, H. J."", ""year"": ""2012"", ""title"": ""Top-down control of attention: It’s gradual, practice-dependent, and hierarchically organized."", ""doi"": ""10.1037/a0027629"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": ""1"", ""evidence_quote"": ""The interaction of practice and distractor, F(1, 60) ⫽ 14.42, p ⬍ .001, ␩p2 ⫽ 0.194, was modulated by the triple interaction of search group, practice and distractor, F(1, 60) ⫽ 9.09, p ⫽ .004, ␩p2 ⫽ 0.132, showing that the distractor effect decreased from the first to the second half in the singleton group (117 vs. 77 ms), but not in the feature group (26 vs. 21 ms), which replicates the work of Zehetleitner et al. (2012)."", ""outcome"": ""successful"", ""outcome_quote"": ""The interaction of practice and distractor, F(1, 60) ⫽ 14.42, p ⬍ .001, ␩p2 ⫽ 0.194, was modulated by the triple interaction of search group, practice and distractor, F(1, 60) ⫽ 9.09, p ⫽ .004, ␩p2 ⫽ 0.132, showing that the distractor effect decreased from the first to the second half in the singleton group (117 vs. 77 ms), but not in the feature group (26 vs. 21 ms), which replicates the work of Zehetleitner et al. (2012)."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""results"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Practice analysis explicitly replicates the original decrease of interference in singleton search but not feature search.""}"
10.1037/xhp0000180,1724e3b3976272af,3,10.3758/s13423-012-0280-4,Experience-dependent attentional tuning of distractor rejection.,"Vatterott, D. B., & Vecera, S. P.",2012,full_text,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": ""Vatterott, D. B., & Vecera, S. P."", ""year"": ""2012"", ""title"": ""Experience-dependent attentional tuning of distractor rejection."", ""doi"": ""10.3758/s13423-012-0280-4"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": ""1"", ""evidence_quote"": ""Vatterott and Vecera (2012) showed that interference occurred in feature search in the initial 24 trials, but not in the following 24 trials. Because participants in the present study started with at least 30 practice trials including both color distractors, it was unlikely that this pattern would replicate in the present study. To be sure, we ran the same ANOVA as in the previous section, but compared Trials 1–24 to Trials 25– 48 instead of the first to the second half of the block (with 240 trials each). A significant interaction of trial number and distractor presence would confirm rapid attentional tuning to the distractor, but this interaction was neither significant during training (p ⫽ .638) nor during test (p ⫽ .229)."", ""outcome"": ""failed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""Vatterott and Vecera (2012) showed that interference occurred in feature search in the initial 24 trials, but not in the following 24 trials. Because participants in the present study started with at least 30 practice trials including both color distractors, it was unlikely that this pattern would replicate in the present study. To be sure, we ran the same ANOVA as in the previous section, but compared Trials 1–24 to Trials 25– 48 instead of the first to the second half of the block (with 240 trials each). A significant interaction of trial number and distractor presence would confirm rapid attentional tuning to the distractor, but this interaction was neither significant during training (p ⫽ .638) nor during test (p ⫽ .229)."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""results"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Authors expressly tested whether the original rapid tuning pattern replicated and found no corresponding interaction. They anticipated nonreplication after practice but did not call the attempt uninformative.""}"
10.1017/s1930297500008299,6f46281e9e4fbbbb,1,10.3758/BF03211332,Another look at reasons for choosing and rejecting.,"Wedell, D. H.",1997,full_text,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": ""Wedell, D. H."", ""year"": ""1997"", ""title"": ""Another look at reasons for choosing and rejecting."", ""doi"": ""10.3758/BF03211332"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""We tested the competing theoretical assertion by Wedell (1997). Our results in support of this account suggest that the stimuli from the 1990s are still of relevance, at least for testing that account."", ""outcome"": ""successful"", ""outcome_quote"": ""We tested the competing theoretical assertion by Wedell (1997). Our results in support of this account suggest that the stimuli from the 1990s are still of relevance, at least for testing that account."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_confident"": true}"
10.1016/j.jecp.2015.12.009,eb5d4130a63b4193,0,,,Borst et al.,2015,abstract,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": ""Borst et al."", ""year"": ""2015"", ""title"": """", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""A previous study demonstrated that preventing mirror errors in reading requires the inhibition of the mirror generalization process in expert adult readers (Borst et al., 2015). Using the same experimental paradigm, the current study aimed at replicating this result in school-aged children."", ""outcome"": ""successful"", ""outcome_quote"": ""Crucially, children required more time to determine that two animals facing opposite directions were identical when preceded by two letters that were lateral mirror images of one another (b/d) than when preceded by letters that were not mirror images of one another (f/t). Importantly, the amplitude of the negative priming effect did not vary with age. Our results suggest that overcoming mirror errors in reading, regardless of the reading proficiency of school-aged children, is rooted in the ability to inhibit the mirror generalization process."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""abstract"", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The abstract states the original inhibitory mechanism and reports the corresponding negative priming result in the new sample. The proposed age effect is an extension rather than a contradicted original result.""}"
