10.31234/osf.io/prfm3,2480054a833f1b61,0,,,Kleiman and Rule,2013,abstract,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": ""Kleiman and Rule"", ""year"": ""2013"", ""title"": """", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""Kleiman and Rule (2013) demonstrated that naive observers could categorize static images at above chance levels according to suicidality and that the faces of individuals who died by suicide were rated as more impulsive than control faces. We attempted to replicate these results with a larger sample and extended the original report by implementing a 2AFC paradigm that minimizes response bias."", ""outcome"": ""mixed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""We found that suicidality categorization was above-chance using the original testing paradigm, but performance dropped to chance in our 2AFC task. We found no evidence supporting differences in perceived impulsivity across categories in either paradigm."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""abstract"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Original paradigm reproduced suicidality classification; the alternative paradigm did not, and the original impulsivity difference was unsupported in both.""}"
https://aisel.aisnet.org/trr/vol7/iss1/3,347aa30b27f119ab,0,,Campus emergency notification systems: An examination of factors affecting compliance with alerts.,"Han, W., Ada, S., Sharman, R., & Rao, H. R.",2015,full_text,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": ""Han, W., Ada, S., Sharman, R., & Rao, H. R."", ""year"": ""2015"", ""title"": ""Campus emergency notification systems: An examination of factors affecting compliance with alerts."", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""This study is a conceptual replication of the study by Han et al. (2015) in which the authors evaluated factors affecting\nstudents’ compliance with emergency instructions during campus emergencies."", ""outcome"": ""mixed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""Results obtained from the current study indicate mixed support for results from the original study. Results\nfrom the unabridged dataset were consistent with the original study, while those from the verify-first dataset\ndiffered."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Explicit overall mixed-support verdict: unabridged results agree while verify-first results differ.""}"
10.1016/j.jecp.2015.11.007,7deb1670745402ab,0,,Online usage of theory of mind continues to develop in late adolescence.,"Dumontheil, I., Apperly, I. A., & Blakemore, S. J.",2010,full_text,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": ""Dumontheil, I., Apperly, I. A., & Blakemore, S. J."", ""year"": ""2010"", ""title"": ""Online usage of theory of mind continues to develop in late adolescence."", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""The current study had three main objectives: (a) to replicate the behavioral findings of Dumontheil and colleagues (2010), (b) to assess the role of inhibitory control in this task across age groups, and (c) to compare the time course of information processing among children, adolescents, and adults through eye-tracking in order to determine in what ways the deployment of ToM differs across age groups."", ""outcome"": ""successful"", ""outcome_quote"": ""To summarize, children and adolescents made more errors than adults only in the Director Experimental trials, which required taking the perspective of the director into account, in contrast to the rule-based control condition (No-Director Experimental trials). This replicated the behavioral results reported in Dumontheil and colleagues (2010)."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""results"", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The stated target is the developmental behavioral finding. Accuracy and reaction-time comparisons agree with the original. Other sources motivate theories, tasks or new inhibitory-control/eye-tracking analyses and are not identified as specific replication targets.""}"
10.3389/fpsyg.2016.01930,1afa9693a775a922,1,10.3758/s13414-013-0430-4,Continuous hand movement induces a far-hand bias in attentional priority.,Festman Y. Adam J. J. Pratt J. Fischer M. H.,2013,full_text,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": ""Festman Y. Adam J. J. Pratt J. Fischer M. H."", ""year"": ""2013"", ""title"": ""Continuous hand movement induces a far-hand bias in attentional priority."", ""doi"": ""10.3758/s13414-013-0430-4"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": ""1, 2"", ""evidence_quote"": ""Furthermore, on the basis of the direction effect of Festman et al. (2013a , b ), we predicted better discrimination when moving toward the probe in both tasks."", ""outcome"": ""mixed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""In addition, we obtained (weak) statistical evidence for a directionality effect, that is, enhanced performance when moving toward compared to away from the probe, but only in spatial gap discrimination."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Directionality was predicted in both tasks, and authors explicitly report it only in spatial discrimination. They call spatial directionality a replication of both Festman papers; aggregate across the two experiments is mixed.""}"
10.3389/fpsyg.2016.01930,1afa9693a775a922,2,10.3389/fpsyg.2013.00657,Both hand position and movement direction modulate visual attention.,Festman Y. Adam J. J. Pratt J. Fischer M. H.,2013,full_text,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": ""Festman Y. Adam J. J. Pratt J. Fischer M. H."", ""year"": ""2013"", ""title"": ""Both hand position and movement direction modulate visual attention."", ""doi"": ""10.3389/fpsyg.2013.00657"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": ""1, 2"", ""evidence_quote"": ""Furthermore, on the basis of the direction effect of Festman et al. (2013a , b ), we predicted better discrimination when moving toward the probe in both tasks."", ""outcome"": ""mixed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""In addition, we obtained (weak) statistical evidence for a directionality effect, that is, enhanced performance when moving toward compared to away from the probe, but only in spatial gap discrimination."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Directionality was predicted in both tasks, and authors explicitly report it only in spatial discrimination. They call spatial directionality a replication of both Festman papers; aggregate across the two experiments is mixed.""}"
10.1353/eca.2016.0000,2050e50097e7846c,0,,How Elastic Are Preferences for Redistribution? Evidence from Randomized Survey Experiments.,"Kuziemko, Ilyana, Michael I. Norton, Emmanuel Saez, and Stefanie Stantcheva.",2013,full_text,,error,argon,"{""target_index"": 0, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""verdict"": ""blind_right"", ""corrected_value"": {""doi"": """", ""title"": ""How Elastic Are Preferences for Redistribution? Evidence from Randomized Survey Experiments."", ""authors"": ""Kuziemko, Ilyana, Michael I. Norton, Emmanuel Saez, and Stefanie Stantcheva."", ""year"": ""2013"", ""type"": ""replication"", ""outcome"": ""successful""}, ""quote"": ""In reality, demand for income redistribution in the United States has remained ﬂat by some measures and decreased according to others (see Kuziemko and others 2013), as we document later in this paper."", ""location"": ""Introduction, p. 368"", ""reason"": ""The paper explicitly cites Kuziemko et al. (2013) for the flat or decreasing U.S. trend in redistribution demand that it documents and replicates in Section I.A, making it a valid omitted target.""}","{""authors"": ""Kuziemko, Ilyana, Michael I. Norton, Emmanuel Saez, and Stefanie Stantcheva."", ""year"": ""2013"", ""title"": ""How Elastic Are Preferences for Redistribution? Evidence from Randomized Survey Experiments."", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""In reality, demand for\nincome redistribution in the United States has remained ﬂat by some mea-\nsures and decreased according to others (see Kuziemko and others 2013),\nas we document later in this paper.\n\nAcross the four measures, we are able\nto replicate the ﬁnding of previous literature showing no increase in sup-\nport for redistribution over this period of increased inequality."", ""outcome"": """", ""outcome_quote"": """", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""results"", ""outcome_computation"": ""not checked"", ""outcome_computational_quote"": ""To ensure that our heterogeneous patterns\nare not driven by data or coding differences between our paper and previ-\nous work, we ﬁrst demonstrate that we can replicate the earlier ﬁnding of\nﬂat aggregate demand using our survey measures."", ""outcome_robustness"": ""robust"", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": ""Across the four measures, we are able\nto replicate the ﬁnding of previous literature showing no increase in sup-\nport for redistribution over this period of increased inequality."", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The earlier flat aggregate trend survives alternative measures and survey datasets. Exact original numerical estimates are not the object of the analysis.""}"
10.23668/psycharchives.4766,3617489aca51758c,0,10.5951/AT.32.5.0051,A study of math anxiety/math avoidance in preservice elementary teachers.,"Kelly, W. P., & Tomhave, W. K.",1985,full_text,,correct,argon,"{""target_index"": 0, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""verdict"": ""shipped_right"", ""corrected_value"": null, ""quote"": ""The current study has two aims: The first aim is to conceptually replicate the finding of the meta-analysis by Hembree (1990) that math anxiety is higher for elementary school teachers than for students from other fields."", ""location"": ""introduction, p. 278"", ""reason"": ""The paper explicitly names Hembree (1990) as the target of its replication aim; Kelly & Tomhave (1985) is cited as supporting literature rather than an independent replication target.""}","{""authors"": ""Kelly, W. P., & Tomhave, W. K."", ""year"": ""1985"", ""title"": ""A study of math anxiety/math avoidance in preservice elementary teachers."", ""doi"": ""10.5951/AT.32.5.0051"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""The current study replicated the finding that female elementary school teachers have a higher level of math anxiety in relation to female students from other fields (Hembree, 1990; Kelly & Tomhave, 1985; Szczygieł & Cipora, 2016)."", ""outcome"": ""successful"", ""outcome_quote"": ""The current study replicated the finding that female elementary school teachers have a higher level of math anxiety in relation to female students from other fields (Hembree, 1990; Kelly & Tomhave, 1985; Szczygieł & Cipora, 2016)."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The authors explicitly compare their own result with this cited original finding. The stated agreement or disagreement determines this target’s outcome.""}"
10.23668/psycharchives.4766,3617489aca51758c,1,10.5604/01.3001.0009.4827,Lęk przed matematyką przyszłych nauczycieli edukacji przedszkolnej i wczesnoszkolnej. Jak uczyć kiedy sama się boję? (Maths anxiety among future pre-service elementary school teachers: How to teach when I am anxious as well?).,"Szczygieł, M., & Cipora, K.",2016,full_text,,correct,argon,"{""target_index"": 1, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""verdict"": ""shipped_right"", ""corrected_value"": null, ""quote"": ""The current study has two aims: The first aim is to conceptually replicate the finding of the meta-analysis by Hembree (1990) that math anxiety is higher for elementary school teachers than for students from other fields."", ""location"": ""introduction, p. 278"", ""reason"": ""The paper explicitly names Hembree (1990) as the target of its replication aim; Szczygieł & Cipora (2016) is cited for background context and discussion comparison rather than as a separate replication target.""}","{""authors"": ""Szczygieł, M., & Cipora, K."", ""year"": ""2016"", ""title"": ""Lęk przed matematyką przyszłych nauczycieli edukacji przedszkolnej i wczesnoszkolnej. Jak uczyć kiedy sama się boję? (Maths anxiety among future pre-service elementary school teachers: How to teach when I am anxious as well?)."", ""doi"": ""10.5604/01.3001.0009.4827"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""The current study replicated the finding that female elementary school teachers have a higher level of math anxiety in relation to female students from other fields (Hembree, 1990; Kelly & Tomhave, 1985; Szczygieł & Cipora, 2016)."", ""outcome"": ""successful"", ""outcome_quote"": ""The current study replicated the finding that female elementary school teachers have a higher level of math anxiety in relation to female students from other fields (Hembree, 1990; Kelly & Tomhave, 1985; Szczygieł & Cipora, 2016)."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The authors explicitly compare their own result with this cited original finding. The stated agreement or disagreement determines this target’s outcome.""}"
10.23668/psycharchives.4766,3617489aca51758c,2,10.1080/1354060042000337084,"Mathematics anxiety, prior experience and confidence to teach mathematics among pre-service education students.","Brady, P., & Bowd, A.",2005,full_text,,correct,argon,"{""target_index"": 2, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""verdict"": ""shipped_right"", ""corrected_value"": null, ""quote"": ""The current study has two aims: The first aim is to conceptually replicate the finding of the meta-analysis by Hembree (1990) that math anxiety is higher for elementary school teachers than for students from other fields. The replication is relevant because the math anxiety of elementary school teachers has consequences for their pupils and it is necessary because previous studies have reported divergent levels of math anxiety, mostly lacked a reference sample, and almost exclusively were based on pre-service teachers. The second aim addresses whether all elementary school teachers experience math anxiety by the consideration of gender, specialization, and experience."", ""location"": ""introduction, p. 278"", ""reason"": ""Brady & Bowd (2005) is cited as background literature and in post-hoc discussion comparisons for the second aim (moderators and teaching attitudes), not as a replication target.""}","{""authors"": ""Brady, P., & Bowd, A."", ""year"": ""2005"", ""title"": ""Mathematics anxiety, prior experience and confidence to teach mathematics among pre-service education students."", ""doi"": ""10.1080/1354060042000337084"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""Among elementary school teachers, female teachers were found to express more math anxiety than male teachers. This corroborates gender effects on math anxiety in general (Hembree, 1990) as well as in particular for elementary school teachers (Brady & Bowd, 2005; Kelly & Tomhave, 1985)."", ""outcome"": ""successful"", ""outcome_quote"": ""Among elementary school teachers, female teachers were found to express more math anxiety than male teachers. This corroborates gender effects on math anxiety in general (Hembree, 1990) as well as in particular for elementary school teachers (Brady & Bowd, 2005; Kelly & Tomhave, 1985)."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The authors explicitly compare their own result with this cited original finding. The stated agreement or disagreement determines this target’s outcome.""}"
10.23668/psycharchives.4766,3617489aca51758c,3,10.1111/j.1949-8594.2006.tb18073.x,Mathematics anxiety and preservice elementary teachers’ confidence to teach mathematics and science.,"Bursal, M., & Paznokas, L.",2006,full_text,,correct,argon,"{""target_index"": 3, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""verdict"": ""shipped_right"", ""corrected_value"": null, ""quote"": ""The current study has two aims: The first aim is to conceptually replicate the finding of the meta-analysis by Hembree (1990) that math anxiety is higher for elementary school teachers than for students from other fields. The replication is relevant because the math anxiety of elementary school teachers has consequences for their pupils and it is necessary because previous studies have reported divergent levels of math anxiety, mostly lacked a reference sample, and almost exclusively were based on pre-service teachers. The second aim addresses whether all elementary school teachers experience math anxiety by the consideration of gender, specialization, and experience."", ""location"": ""introduction, p. 278"", ""reason"": ""Bursal & Paznokas (2006) is cited only as background and literature context regarding confidence in teaching math under the second aim, not as a replicated original study.""}","{""authors"": ""Bursal, M., & Paznokas, L."", ""year"": ""2006"", ""title"": ""Mathematics anxiety and preservice elementary teachers’ confidence to teach mathematics and science."", ""doi"": ""10.1111/j.1949-8594.2006.tb18073.x"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""Nevertheless, our results were consistent with previous studies showing the relation of math anxiety to confidence (Brady & Bowd, 2005; Bursal & Paznokas, 2006), efficacy (Gresham, 2008; Jaggernauth & Jameson-Charles, 2015; Swars et al., 2006) and anxiety of teaching math (Ganley et al., 2019; Olson & Stoehr, 2019)."", ""outcome"": ""successful"", ""outcome_quote"": ""Nevertheless, our results were consistent with previous studies showing the relation of math anxiety to confidence (Brady & Bowd, 2005; Bursal & Paznokas, 2006), efficacy (Gresham, 2008; Jaggernauth & Jameson-Charles, 2015; Swars et al., 2006) and anxiety of teaching math (Ganley et al., 2019; Olson & Stoehr, 2019)."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The authors explicitly compare their own result with this cited original finding. The stated agreement or disagreement determines this target’s outcome.""}"
10.23668/psycharchives.4766,3617489aca51758c,4,10.1177/2332858419839702,The construct validation of the math anxiety scale for teachers.,"Ganley, C. M., Schoen, R. C., LaVenia, M., & Tazaz, A. M.",2019,full_text,,correct,argon,"{""target_index"": 4, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""verdict"": ""shipped_right"", ""corrected_value"": null, ""quote"": ""The current study has two aims: The first aim is to conceptually replicate the finding of the meta-analysis by Hembree (1990) that math anxiety is higher for elementary school teachers than for students from other fields. The replication is relevant because the math anxiety of elementary school teachers has consequences for their pupils and it is necessary because previous studies have reported divergent levels of math anxiety, mostly lacked a reference sample, and almost exclusively were based on pre-service teachers. The second aim addresses whether all elementary school teachers experience math anxiety by the consideration of gender, specialization, and experience."", ""location"": ""introduction, p. 278"", ""reason"": ""Ganley et al. (2019) is cited for background and literature comparison regarding specialization and teaching attitudes under the paper's second aim, not as a replication target.""}","{""authors"": ""Ganley, C. M., Schoen, R. C., LaVenia, M., & Tazaz, A. M."", ""year"": ""2019"", ""title"": ""The construct validation of the math anxiety scale for teachers."", ""doi"": ""10.1177/2332858419839702"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""As expected, math anxiety was lower both in German teachers majoring in math compared to those not majoring in math during their studies of elementary school education (Ganley et al., 2019; Malinsky et al., 2006; Porsch, 2017) and in Belgian teachers who specialized in math during secondary school compared to those who chose an educational trajectory containing less math (see the Appendix, Table A4)."", ""outcome"": ""successful"", ""outcome_quote"": ""As expected, math anxiety was lower both in German teachers majoring in math compared to those not majoring in math during their studies of elementary school education (Ganley et al., 2019; Malinsky et al., 2006; Porsch, 2017) and in Belgian teachers who specialized in math during secondary school compared to those who chose an educational trajectory containing less math (see the Appendix, Table A4)."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The authors explicitly compare their own result with this cited original finding. The stated agreement or disagreement determines this target’s outcome.""}"
10.23668/psycharchives.4766,3617489aca51758c,5,,Math anxiety in pre-service elementary school teachers.,"Malinsky, M., Ross, A., Pannells, T., & McJunkin, M.",2006,full_text,,correct,argon,"{""target_index"": 5, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""verdict"": ""shipped_right"", ""corrected_value"": null, ""quote"": ""The current study has two aims: The first aim is to conceptually replicate the finding of the meta-analysis by Hembree (1990) that math anxiety is higher for elementary school teachers than for students from other fields. The replication is relevant because the math anxiety of elementary school teachers has consequences for their pupils and it is necessary because previous studies have reported divergent levels of math anxiety, mostly lacked a reference sample, and almost exclusively were based on pre-service teachers. The second aim addresses whether all elementary school teachers experience math anxiety by the consideration of gender, specialization, and experience."", ""location"": ""introduction, p. 278"", ""reason"": ""Malinsky et al. (2006) is cited for background context and discussion comparison regarding math specialization under the second aim, not as a replication target.""}","{""authors"": ""Malinsky, M., Ross, A., Pannells, T., & McJunkin, M."", ""year"": ""2006"", ""title"": ""Math anxiety in pre-service elementary school teachers."", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""As expected, math anxiety was lower both in German teachers majoring in math compared to those not majoring in math during their studies of elementary school education (Ganley et al., 2019; Malinsky et al., 2006; Porsch, 2017) and in Belgian teachers who specialized in math during secondary school compared to those who chose an educational trajectory containing less math (see the Appendix, Table A4)."", ""outcome"": ""successful"", ""outcome_quote"": ""As expected, math anxiety was lower both in German teachers majoring in math compared to those not majoring in math during their studies of elementary school education (Ganley et al., 2019; Malinsky et al., 2006; Porsch, 2017) and in Belgian teachers who specialized in math during secondary school compared to those who chose an educational trajectory containing less math (see the Appendix, Table A4)."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The authors explicitly compare their own result with this cited original finding. The stated agreement or disagreement determines this target’s outcome.""}"
10.23668/psycharchives.4766,3617489aca51758c,6,,"Mathematik als Pflichtfach in der Primarstufenlehrerausbildung. Mathematikangst, Enthusiasmus und Gründe der Schwerpunktwahl angehender Grundschullehrkräfte.","Porsch, R.",2017,full_text,,correct,argon,"{""target_index"": 6, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""verdict"": ""shipped_right"", ""corrected_value"": null, ""quote"": ""The current study has two aims: The first aim is to conceptually replicate the finding of the meta-analysis by Hembree (1990) that math anxiety is higher for elementary school teachers than for students from other fields. The replication is relevant because the math anxiety of elementary school teachers has consequences for their pupils and it is necessary because previous studies have reported divergent levels of math anxiety, mostly lacked a reference sample, and almost exclusively were based on pre-service teachers. The second aim addresses whether all elementary school teachers experience math anxiety by the consideration of gender, specialization, and experience."", ""location"": ""introduction, p. 278"", ""reason"": ""Porsch (2017) is cited for background motivation and literature comparison regarding math specialization under the second aim, not as a replication target.""}","{""authors"": ""Porsch, R."", ""year"": ""2017"", ""title"": ""Mathematik als Pflichtfach in der Primarstufenlehrerausbildung. Mathematikangst, Enthusiasmus und Gründe der Schwerpunktwahl angehender Grundschullehrkräfte."", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""As expected, math anxiety was lower both in German teachers majoring in math compared to those not majoring in math during their studies of elementary school education (Ganley et al., 2019; Malinsky et al., 2006; Porsch, 2017) and in Belgian teachers who specialized in math during secondary school compared to those who chose an educational trajectory containing less math (see the Appendix, Table A4)."", ""outcome"": ""successful"", ""outcome_quote"": ""As expected, math anxiety was lower both in German teachers majoring in math compared to those not majoring in math during their studies of elementary school education (Ganley et al., 2019; Malinsky et al., 2006; Porsch, 2017) and in Belgian teachers who specialized in math during secondary school compared to those who chose an educational trajectory containing less math (see the Appendix, Table A4)."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The authors explicitly compare their own result with this cited original finding. The stated agreement or disagreement determines this target’s outcome.""}"
10.23668/psycharchives.4766,3617489aca51758c,7,10.1177/0022487117702580,Preservice to inservice: Does mathematics anxiety change with teaching experience?,"Gresham, G.",2018,full_text,,correct,argon,"{""target_index"": 7, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""verdict"": ""shipped_right"", ""corrected_value"": null, ""quote"": ""The current study has two aims: The first aim is to conceptually replicate the finding of the meta-analysis by Hembree (1990) that math anxiety is higher for elementary school teachers than for students from other fields. The replication is relevant because the math anxiety of elementary school teachers has consequences for their pupils and it is necessary because previous studies have reported divergent levels of math anxiety, mostly lacked a reference sample, and almost exclusively were based on pre-service teachers. The second aim addresses whether all elementary school teachers experience math anxiety by the consideration of gender, specialization, and experience."", ""location"": ""introduction, p. 278"", ""reason"": ""Gresham (2018) is cited as background literature and in discussion comparison regarding teaching experience under the second aim, not as a replication target.""}","{""authors"": ""Gresham, G."", ""year"": ""2018"", ""title"": ""Preservice to inservice: Does mathematics anxiety change with teaching experience?"", ""doi"": ""10.1177/0022487117702580"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""Consistent with this reported decrease in math anxiety during teacher education, Gresham (2018) found evidence for a further decrease in math anxiety when teachers start teaching. Our study, however, does not support these previously found differences and instead provides moderate evidence for similar levels of math anxiety for female pre-service and in-service elementary school teachers, even when specialization in math was taken into account."", ""outcome"": ""failed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""Consistent with this reported decrease in math anxiety during teacher education, Gresham (2018) found evidence for a further decrease in math anxiety when teachers start teaching. Our study, however, does not support these previously found differences and instead provides moderate evidence for similar levels of math anxiety for female pre-service and in-service elementary school teachers, even when specialization in math was taken into account."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The authors explicitly compare their own result with this cited original finding. The stated agreement or disagreement determines this target’s outcome.""}"
10.23668/psycharchives.4766,3617489aca51758c,8,10.1080/10476210802250133,Mathematics anxiety and mathematics teacher efficacy in elementary pre-service teachers.,"Gresham, G.",2008,full_text,,correct,argon,"{""target_index"": 8, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""verdict"": ""shipped_right"", ""corrected_value"": null, ""quote"": ""The current study has two aims: The first aim is to conceptually replicate the finding of the meta-analysis by Hembree (1990) that math anxiety is higher for elementary school teachers than for students from other fields. The replication is relevant because the math anxiety of elementary school teachers has consequences for their pupils and it is necessary because previous studies have reported divergent levels of math anxiety, mostly lacked a reference sample, and almost exclusively were based on pre-service teachers. The second aim addresses whether all elementary school teachers experience math anxiety by the consideration of gender, specialization, and experience."", ""location"": ""introduction, p. 278"", ""reason"": ""Gresham (2008) is cited only as background and literature comparison regarding teacher efficacy, which was not even directly measured in this study, rather than as a replication target.""}","{""authors"": ""Gresham, G."", ""year"": ""2008"", ""title"": ""Mathematics anxiety and mathematics teacher efficacy in elementary pre-service teachers."", ""doi"": ""10.1080/10476210802250133"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""Nevertheless, our results were consistent with previous studies showing the relation of math anxiety to confidence (Brady & Bowd, 2005; Bursal & Paznokas, 2006), efficacy (Gresham, 2008; Jaggernauth & Jameson-Charles, 2015; Swars et al., 2006) and anxiety of teaching math (Ganley et al., 2019; Olson & Stoehr, 2019)."", ""outcome"": ""successful"", ""outcome_quote"": ""Nevertheless, our results were consistent with previous studies showing the relation of math anxiety to confidence (Brady & Bowd, 2005; Bursal & Paznokas, 2006), efficacy (Gresham, 2008; Jaggernauth & Jameson-Charles, 2015; Swars et al., 2006) and anxiety of teaching math (Ganley et al., 2019; Olson & Stoehr, 2019)."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The authors explicitly compare their own result with this cited original finding. The stated agreement or disagreement determines this target’s outcome.""}"
