sion"", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The authors explicitly compare their own result with this cited original finding. The stated agreement or disagreement determines this target’s outcome.""}"
10.23668/psycharchives.4766,3617489aca51758c,12,10.21449/ijate.615640,An English version of the mathematics teaching anxiety scale.,"Hunt, T. E., & Sari, M. H.",2019,full_text,,correct,argon,"{""target_index"": 12, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""verdict"": ""shipped_right"", ""corrected_value"": null, ""quote"": ""However, in contrast to previous data (Hunt & Sari, 2019), our results provided no evidence of a beneficial effect of teaching math on the teachers’ attitudes, because female in-service and pre-service elementary school teachers did not significantly differ in enjoyment and ease of teaching math, and experience level in female in-service teachers did not significantly alter their teaching attitudes."", ""location"": ""discussion, p. 287"", ""reason"": ""Hunt & Sari (2019) appears only in the discussion as a post-hoc literature contrast for the secondary teaching-attitudes analysis and is not a replication target.""}","{""authors"": ""Hunt, T. E., & Sari, M. H."", ""year"": ""2019"", ""title"": ""An English version of the mathematics teaching anxiety scale."", ""doi"": ""10.21449/ijate.615640"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""However, in contrast to previous data (Hunt & Sari, 2019), our results provided no evidence of a beneficial effect of teaching math on the teachers’ attitudes, because female in-service and pre-service elementary school teachers did not significantly differ in enjoyment and ease of teaching math, and experience level in female in-service teachers did not significantly alter their teaching attitudes."", ""outcome"": ""failed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""However, in contrast to previous data (Hunt & Sari, 2019), our results provided no evidence of a beneficial effect of teaching math on the teachers’ attitudes, because female in-service and pre-service elementary school teachers did not significantly differ in enjoyment and ease of teaching math, and experience level in female in-service teachers did not significantly alter their teaching attitudes."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The authors explicitly compare their own result with this cited original finding. The stated agreement or disagreement determines this target’s outcome.""}"
10.31235/osf.io/zugae,ad7125f74e82a532,1,10.4324/9781315696485,The Effect of Firearms on Suicide.,"Kleck, G.",2018,full_text,,correct,argon,"{""target_index"": 1, ""field"": ""missing_row"", ""method"": ""argon"", ""verdict"": ""shipped_right"", ""corrected_value"": null, ""quote"": ""Using a Poisson\nregression with the data in Kleck (2018, Table 17.3), I compared lethality of firearm (258,386 deaths, 308,623\nattempts, 83.7% lethal) and hanging/suffocation suicide attempts (118,072 deaths, 154,013 attempts, 76.7%\nlethal). Firearm suicide attempts are 8.8% more deadly, or conversely, hanging/suffocation attempts are 36.0%\nmore surivavable (both p < 0.001)."", ""location"": ""Introduction, p. 5, Footnote 2, lines 115-119"", ""reason"": ""Kleck (2018) is discussed only in the Introduction as background critique motivating the study, with an incidental footnote calculation on two summary counts from Table 17.3. The paper's Study Aims ('Given these concerns, I reproduce the study in Kleck (2021)'), Methods, Results, and Conclusion exclusively target Kleck (2021), so Kleck (2018) is not an omitted target.""}","{""authors"": ""Kleck, G."", ""year"": ""2018"", ""title"": ""The Effect of Firearms on Suicide."", ""doi"": ""10.4324/9781315696485"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""Using a Poisson\nregression with the data in Kleck (2018, Table 17.3), I compared lethality of firearm (258,386 deaths, 308,623\nattempts, 83.7% lethal) and hanging/suffocation suicide attempts (118,072 deaths, 154,013 attempts, 76.7%\nlethal). Firearm suicide attempts are 8.8% more deadly, or conversely, hanging/suffocation attempts are 36.0%\nmore surivavable (both p < 0.001)."", ""outcome"": """", ""outcome_quote"": """", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""fulltext"", ""outcome_computation"": ""not checked"", ""outcome_computational_quote"": ""Using a Poisson\nregression with the data in Kleck (2018, Table 17.3), I compared lethality of firearm (258,386 deaths, 308,623\nattempts, 83.7% lethal) and hanging/suffocation suicide attempts (118,072 deaths, 154,013 attempts, 76.7%\nlethal). Firearm suicide attempts are 8.8% more deadly, or conversely, hanging/suffocation attempts are 36.0%\nmore surivavable (both p < 0.001)."", ""outcome_robustness"": ""robustness challenges"", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": ""Using a Poisson\nregression with the data in Kleck (2018, Table 17.3), I compared lethality of firearm (258,386 deaths, 308,623\nattempts, 83.7% lethal) and hanging/suffocation suicide attempts (118,072 deaths, 154,013 attempts, 76.7%\nlethal). Firearm suicide attempts are 8.8% more deadly, or conversely, hanging/suffocation attempts are 36.0%\nmore surivavable (both p < 0.001)."", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Footnote reanalyses the original published counts with Poisson regression, overturning the stated no-difference interpretation; it does not attempt to rerun original numbers.""}"
