10.7287/peerj.preprints.3352v1,c75e48cfc0df56cd,2,10.1016/j.bandl.2009.05.002,Atypical cerebral lateralisation in adults with compensated developmental dyslexia demonstrated using functional transcranial Doppler ultrasound,"Illingworth, S., & Bishop, D. V. M.",2009,full_text,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": ""Illingworth, S., & Bishop, D. V. M."", ""year"": ""2009"", ""title"": ""Atypical cerebral lateralisation in adults with compensated developmental dyslexia demonstrated using functional transcranial Doppler ultrasound"", ""doi"": ""10.1016/j.bandl.2009.05.002"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""We aimed to replicate previous findings of an association between DLD and reduced laterality on a quantitative measure of hand preference (reaching across the midline) and on language laterality assessed using functional transcranial Doppler ultrasound."", ""outcome"": ""failed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""There was no difference between the DLD and TD groups on quantitative measures of handedness or language lateralisation, or on a categorical measure of consistency of left hemisphere dominance."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""abstract"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The corresponding published association is tested in the new twin sample and is not supported.""}"
10.7287/peerj.preprints.3352v1,c75e48cfc0df56cd,4,10.7717/peerj.507,No population bias to left-hemisphere language in 4-year-olds with language impairment,"Bishop, D. V. M., Holt, G., Whitehouse, A. J. O., & Groen, M.",2014,full_text,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": ""Bishop, D. V. M., Holt, G., Whitehouse, A. J. O., & Groen, M."", ""year"": ""2014"", ""title"": ""No population bias to left-hemisphere language in 4-year-olds with language impairment"", ""doi"": ""10.7717/peerj.507"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""We aimed to replicate previous findings of an association between DLD and reduced laterality on a quantitative measure of hand preference (reaching across the midline) and on language laterality assessed using functional transcranial Doppler ultrasound."", ""outcome"": ""failed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""There was no difference between the DLD and TD groups on quantitative measures of handedness or language lateralisation, or on a categorical measure of consistency of left hemisphere dominance."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""abstract"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The corresponding published association is tested in the new twin sample and is not supported.""}"
10.7287/peerj.preprints.3352v1,c75e48cfc0df56cd,5,10.1002/brb3.56,Does cerebral lateralization develop? A study using functional transcranial Doppler ultrasound assessing lateralization for language production and visuospatial memory,"Groen, M., Whitehouse, A. J. O., Badcock, N. A., & Bishop, D. V.",2012,full_text,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": ""Groen, M., Whitehouse, A. J. O., Badcock, N. A., & Bishop, D. V."", ""year"": ""2012"", ""title"": ""Does cerebral lateralization develop? A study using functional transcranial Doppler ultrasound assessing lateralization for language production and visuospatial memory"", ""doi"": ""10.1002/brb3.56"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""A subsidiary aim was to see whether we could replicate associations between language laterality and language tests found by Groen et al. (2012)."", ""outcome"": ""failed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""In the second step of analysis, we tested the relationship between language and laterality from the opposite direction, using language measures as predictors, aiming to replicate the effects reported by Groen et al. (2012). We computed Spearman's correlations between each language measure and the fTCD laterality index in the two replication samples. However, no uncorrected correlation replicated across samples, and no correlation within a sample survived a Holm-Bonferroni correction."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""results"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The corresponding published association is tested in the new twin sample and is not supported.""}"
10.1038/s41598-017-07404-4,4164f1a8792c6dbd,0,10.1016/S1474-4422(15)00338-5,Loci associated with ischaemic stroke and its subtypes (SiGN): a genome-wide association study,"NINDS Stroke Genetics Network (SiGN), I. S. G. C. (ISGC).",2015,full_text,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": ""NINDS Stroke Genetics Network (SiGN), I. S. G. C. (ISGC)."", ""year"": ""2015"", ""title"": ""Loci associated with ischaemic stroke and its subtypes (SiGN): a genome-wide association study"", ""doi"": ""10.1016/S1474-4422(15)00338-5"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""We aimed to replicate the associations of eight loci with IS and/or its subtypes as previously reported in an independent set of patients with IS drawn from a Dutch cohort."", ""outcome"": ""mixed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""In a well-defined cohort of patients with IS, we confirmed the 4q25 (PITX2) and 16q22 (ZFHX3) loci to be significantly associated with the IS subtype CE. We also found locus 16q22 to be significantly associated with overall IS. We were not able to replicate the previously-established associations at the 1p13.2 (TSPAN2), 6p21.1 (SUPT3H/CDC5L), 7p21.1 (HDAC9), 9p21.3 (CDKN2B-AS1), 9q34 (ABO) and 12q24 (ALDH2) with overall IS or its subtypes although, barring locus 6p21.1 (SUPT3H/CDC5L), the effect direction of their associations were consistent with expectation2–11."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""TSPAN2 and ALDH2 associations failed; CE associations supported in discussion references 2–4, with additional subtype-specificity qualification. Identification follows Table 1 numbered reference mapping, corroborated by the discussion.""}"
10.1038/s41598-017-07404-4,4164f1a8792c6dbd,1,10.1002/ana.21480,Risk variants for atrial fibrillation on chromosome 4q25 associate with ischemic stroke,"Gretarsdottir, S. et al.",2008,full_text,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": ""Gretarsdottir, S. et al."", ""year"": ""2008"", ""title"": ""Risk variants for atrial fibrillation on chromosome 4q25 associate with ischemic stroke"", ""doi"": ""10.1002/ana.21480"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""We aimed to replicate the associations of eight loci with IS and/or its subtypes as previously reported in an independent set of patients with IS drawn from a Dutch cohort."", ""outcome"": ""failed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""At the 4q25 (PITX2) locus, previously identified by GWAS, we confirmed the association at rs2634074 with CE stroke (OR = 1.43 for the T allele; 95% CI, 1.13–1.81, p = 0.029), but not at rs2200733 (OR = 1.30 for the T allele; 95% CI, 0.96–1.76; p = 0.60) (Table 1). "", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""results"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Table 1 assigns both rs2200733 associations to reference 3; neither replicated. Successful rs2634074 is assigned to reference 9. Identification follows Table 1 numbered reference mapping, corroborated by the discussion.""}"
10.1038/s41598-017-07404-4,4164f1a8792c6dbd,2,10.1016/S1474-4422(12)70234-X,Genetic risk factors for ischaemic stroke and its subtypes (the METASTROKE Collaboration): A meta-analysis of genome-wide association studies,"Traylor, M. et al.",2012,full_text,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": ""Traylor, M. et al."", ""year"": ""2012"", ""title"": ""Genetic risk factors for ischaemic stroke and its subtypes (the METASTROKE Collaboration): A meta-analysis of genome-wide association studies"", ""doi"": ""10.1016/S1474-4422(12)70234-X"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""We aimed to replicate the associations of eight loci with IS and/or its subtypes as previously reported in an independent set of patients with IS drawn from a Dutch cohort."", ""outcome"": ""mixed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""In a well-defined cohort of patients with IS, we confirmed the 4q25 (PITX2) and 16q22 (ZFHX3) loci to be significantly associated with the IS subtype CE. We also found locus 16q22 to be significantly associated with overall IS. We were not able to replicate the previously-established associations at the 1p13.2 (TSPAN2), 6p21.1 (SUPT3H/CDC5L), 7p21.1 (HDAC9), 9p21.3 (CDKN2B-AS1), 9q34 (ABO) and 12q24 (ALDH2) with overall IS or its subtypes although, barring locus 6p21.1 (SUPT3H/CDC5L), the effect direction of their associations were consistent with expectation2–11."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""HDAC9 association failed; CE associations in discussion references 2–4 were confirmed. Identification follows Table 1 numbered reference mapping, corroborated by the discussion.""}"
