10.1046/j.1365-2702.1999.00307.x,26e3db6b12b038fe,0,,,,,abstract,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": """", ""year"": """", ""title"": """", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": """", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""The findings of a single small‐scale study previously indicated that such patients’ needs for information are not always fully met and pain control is inadequate. This small‐scale study was replicated to produce broadly similar findings in a second hospital 2 years later."", ""outcome"": ""successful"", ""outcome_quote"": ""This small‐scale study was replicated to produce broadly similar findings in a second hospital 2 years later."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""abstract"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The abstract gives an overall verdict of broadly similar findings.""}"
10.1177/147470491100900306,4a9d69d9cd99c0b1,0,,Changes in fat distribution (WHR) and body weight across the menstrual cycle.,"Kirchengast, S., and Gartner, M.",2002,full_text,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": ""Kirchengast, S., and Gartner, M."", ""year"": ""2002"", ""title"": ""Changes in fat distribution (WHR) and body weight across the menstrual cycle."", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""The objective of the current research was to provide an operational replication of the finding that women’s faces are more attractive at high fertility (Roberts et al., 2004) and of the finding that women’s WHRs are lower at high fertility (Kirchengast and Gartner, 2002)."", ""outcome"": ""failed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""We designed the current study to replicate two other potential cues of women’s ovulatory status: lower waist-to-hip ratios at high fertility and enhanced facial attractiveness at high fertility. We failed to replicate either effect."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Explicit overall failure verdict for both designated replication targets.""}"
10.1017/s1352465810000676,9b05053aa65fde14,1,,"The relationship between disgust sensitivity, anxiety and obsessions.","Thorpe, S. J., Patel, S. P. and Simonds, L. M.",2003,full_text,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": ""Thorpe, S. J., Patel, S. P. and Simonds, L. M."", ""year"": ""2003"", ""title"": ""The relationship between disgust sensitivity, anxiety and obsessions."", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""This paper therefore sets out to explore the posited relationship between disgust sensitivity,\nhealth anxiety, danger expectancies and cognitive and behavioural measures of hand washing,\nin vivo, within a non-clinical group. It builds specifically on the work by Jones and Menzies\n(1997) and Thorpe et al. (2003) and will do this by replicating the experimental methodology\ndescribed by Jones and Menzies (1997), by removing the elements that they found to\nbe redundant (responsibility and perfectionism), retaining those factors related to danger\nexpectancies concerning illness beliefs, and adding factors previously identified as being\nimplicated in self-reported levels of obsessional hand washing: namely, measures of self-\nreported disgust sensitivity and health anxiety (Thorpe et al., 2003)."", ""outcome"": ""mixed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""Health anxiety, for example, was not related to washing time but was related to the\nOCI-R washing subscale. However, our analysis showed that disgust sensitivity mediated the\nrelationship between health anxiety and OCI-R washing. This is different to previous findings\nby Thorpe et al. (2003) and may be due to the BAT heightening disgust sensitivity or other\n\f                        Disgust, danger expectancy and hand washing                           187\n\n\nsituational factors."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Named foundational study is retested with questionnaire and behavioural outcomes; health anxiety and disgust associations persist but the authors explicitly identify a different mediation pattern.""}"
10.1080/15248372.2015.1033525,a94c4cdacbb28b3b,0,,Developmental and individual differences in pure numerical estimation.,"Booth, J. L., & Siegler, R. S.",2006,full_text,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": ""Booth, J. L., & Siegler, R. S."", ""year"": ""2006"", ""title"": ""Developmental and individual differences in pure numerical estimation."", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""The primary purpose of this study was the use of a number-line assessment to replicate the logarithmic-to-linear shift pattern in positive number sense explored by Booth and Siegler (2006), as well as to examine the hypothesized parallel logarithmic-to-linear shift using negative numbers."", ""outcome"": ""successful"", ""outcome_quote"": ""Our findings replicate those of Siegler and colleagues (e.g., Booth & Siegler, 2006) for positive numbers; specifically, we found evidence of a shift from logarithmic to linear representations for positive numbers between fourth and sixth grade (0–1,000 scale)."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Authors state an overall replication verdict for the positive-number findings of Siegler and colleagues. Both cited papers are explicitly identified as work this study was designed to replicate.""}"
10.1080/15248372.2015.1033525,a94c4cdacbb28b3b,1,,Representational change and children’s numerical estimation.,"Opfer, J. E., & Siegler, R. S.",2007,full_text,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": ""Opfer, J. E., & Siegler, R. S."", ""year"": ""2007"", ""title"": ""Representational change and children’s numerical estimation."", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""First, we wanted to directly replicate Siegler’s work (Opfer & Siegler, 2007)as closely as possible and thus chose to use the original positive number line and values used in those studies."", ""outcome"": ""successful"", ""outcome_quote"": ""Our findings replicate those of Siegler and colleagues (e.g., Booth & Siegler, 2006) for positive numbers; specifically, we found evidence of a shift from logarithmic to linear representations for positive numbers between fourth and sixth grade (0–1,000 scale)."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Authors state an overall replication verdict for the positive-number findings of Siegler and colleagues. Both cited papers are explicitly identified as work this study was designed to replicate.""}"
10.17705/1atrr.00039,2b3e33af5f30e0a2,0,,,,2015,abstract,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": """", ""year"": ""2015"", ""title"": """", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": """", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""In this study, we carry out a methodological replication of the research done by Choi et al. (2015) published in Information System Research."", ""outcome"": ""mixed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""Our replication results show that whereas most of the hypotheses supported by the original results on behavioral responses replicate with high consistency (8 out of 12 hypotheses), the results that deal with the effects of network commonality on perceived privacy invasion and perceived relationship bonding did not replicate (4 out of 12 hypotheses)."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""abstract"", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Authors explicitly report behavioral-response agreement and network-commonality disagreement. A failed manipulation does not override their mixed comparisons. Full reference unavailable.""}"
10.31234/osf.io/nwg7p,2fd219f965eadcec,1,10.1027/1618-3169.52.3.195,"Two-digit comparison - Decomposed, holistic, or hybrid?","Verguts, T., & De Moor, W.",2005,full_text,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": ""Verguts, T., & De Moor, W."", ""year"": ""2005"", ""title"": ""Two-digit comparison - Decomposed, holistic, or hybrid?"", ""doi"": ""10.1027/1618-3169.52.3.195"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""Regarding modulations of the numerical effects due to linguistic markedness of the task instruction, we expected to replicate the findings by Verguts and De Moor (2005) showing comparable distance effects for marked and unmarked task instructions."", ""outcome"": ""mixed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""However, in contrast to previous findings in single- and two-digit number comparison (Arend & Henik 2015; Verguts and De Moor, 2005), we did not detect reliable differences in overall response times in frequentist analyses. Although the Bayesian analysis supports the null model, the evidential value is relatively weak."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Distance invariance agrees (abstract and discussion report no numerical effect changes); expected overall latency contrast explicitly fails. No overall verdict for this target overrides the two comparisons.""}"
10.31234/osf.io/nwg7p,2fd219f965eadcec,2,10.1037/xlm0000135,Choosing the larger versus choosing the smaller: Asymmetries in the size congruity effect.,"Arend, I., & Henik, A.",2015,full_text,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": ""Arend, I., & Henik, A."", ""year"": ""2015"", ""title"": ""Choosing the larger versus choosing the smaller: Asymmetries in the size congruity effect."", ""doi"": ""10.1037/xlm0000135"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""In line with previous reports (Arend & Henik, 2015; Verguts & De Moor, 2005), we expected prolonged reaction times when instructed to pick the smaller as compared to picking the larger number."", ""outcome"": ""failed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""However, in contrast to previous findings in single- and two-digit number comparison (Arend & Henik 2015; Verguts and De Moor, 2005), we did not detect reliable differences in overall response times in frequentist analyses."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Explicitly tests the prior overall reaction-time difference in a new number range; no difference found. Exploratory bin comparison also differs.""}"
10.1037/xhp0000483,6c17994fbe674096,1,10.3758/APP.72.2.326,What is top-down about contingent capture?,"Belopolsky, A. V., Schreij, D., & Theeuwes, J.",2010,full_text,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": ""Belopolsky, A. V., Schreij, D., & Theeuwes, J."", ""year"": ""2010"", ""title"": ""What is top-down about contingent capture?"", ""doi"": ""10.3758/APP.72.2.326"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": ""3"", ""evidence_quote"": ""Further, even the frequent green cues elicited validity effects in combination with red color singleton targets. This suggests that the bottom-up salience signal overrode the task-set for red color singletons. The finding replicates previous studies, in which targetnonmatching cues captured attention (Belopolsky et al., 2010; Noesen et al., 2014)."", ""outcome"": ""successful"", ""outcome_quote"": ""Further, even the frequent green cues elicited validity effects in combination with red color singleton targets. This suggests that the bottom-up salience signal overrode the task-set for red color singletons. The finding replicates previous studies, in which targetnonmatching cues captured attention (Belopolsky et al., 2010; Noesen et al., 2014)."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Authors explicitly identify their nonmatching-cue capture result as replicating both named papers; these are targets in addition to the principal 2015 target.""}"
10.1037/xhp0000483,6c17994fbe674096,2,10.1080/20445911.2014.892112,An electrophysiological study of attention capture by salience: Does rarity enable capture?,"Noesen, B., Lien, M. C., & Ruthruff, E.",2014,full_text,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": ""Noesen, B., Lien, M. C., & Ruthruff, E."", ""year"": ""2014"", ""title"": ""An electrophysiological study of attention capture by salience: Does rarity enable capture?"", ""doi"": ""10.1080/20445911.2014.892112"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": ""3"", ""evidence_quote"": ""Further, even the frequent green cues elicited validity effects in combination with red color singleton targets. This suggests that the bottom-up salience signal overrode the task-set for red color singletons. The finding replicates previous studies, in which targetnonmatching cues captured attention (Belopolsky et al., 2010; Noesen et al., 2014)."", ""outcome"": ""successful"", ""outcome_quote"": ""Further, even the frequent green cues elicited validity effects in combination with red color singleton targets. This suggests that the bottom-up salience signal overrode the task-set for red color singletons. The finding replicates previous studies, in which targetnonmatching cues captured attention (Belopolsky et al., 2010; Noesen et al., 2014)."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The explicit replication statement covers this target as well as Belopolsky et al.; later manipulations do not expressly contradict this target-specific finding.""}"
10.1002/1520-6629(199110)19:4<373::aid-jcop2290190409>3.0.co;2-j,6d95c6ae74f8dc7b,0,,,,,abstract,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": """", ""year"": """", ""title"": """", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": """", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""The present study tested the following hypotheses: (1) Stress has more of a negative impact on the life satisfaction of those under age 75 than those over age 75; (2) social support has more of a positive effect on life satisfaction in those under age 75 than those over age 75; (3) the buffering effect of social support is stronger in the under age 75 group than the over age 75 group. None of the three hypotheses was supported, contrary to prior research."", ""outcome"": ""failed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""The present study tested the following hypotheses: (1) Stress has more of a negative impact on the life satisfaction of those under age 75 than those over age 75; (2) social support has more of a positive effect on life satisfaction in those under age 75 than those over age 75; (3) the buffering effect of social support is stronger in the under age 75 group than the over age 75 group. None of the three hypotheses was supported, contrary to prior research."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""abstract"", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The abstract explicitly says none of the three tested age-effect hypotheses was supported, contrary to prior research; title explicitly frames failure to replicate.""}"
10.1007/s40750-018-0101-8,6ea46c9c39367ac5,1,10.1177/0146167208321484,Genetic correlates of adult attachment style,"Gillath O, Shaver PR, Baek J-M, Chun DS",2008,full_text,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": ""Gillath O, Shaver PR, Baek J-M, Chun DS"", ""year"": ""2008"", ""title"": ""Genetic correlates of adult attachment style"", ""doi"": ""10.1177/0146167208321484"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""Although we replicate previous null findings for attachment style and one of the most widely studied SNPs, OXTR rs53576 (Gillath et al. 2008 ; Rodrigues et al. 2009 ), this corroborative result for rs237897 illustrates the importance of parallel testing of a number of different SNPs for each gene, rather than focusing on a few popular ones."", ""outcome"": ""successful"", ""outcome_quote"": ""Although we replicate previous null findings for attachment style and one of the most widely studied SNPs, OXTR rs53576 (Gillath et al. 2008 ; Rodrigues et al. 2009 ), this corroborative result for rs237897 illustrates the importance of parallel testing of a number of different SNPs for each gene, rather than focusing on a few popular ones."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true}"
