10.31234/osf.io/akz6b,834b55f482c6e8a1,12,10.1093/ijnp/pyu026,PTSD-like memory generated through enhanced noradrenergic activity is mitigated by a dual step pharmacological intervention targeting its reconsolidation.,"Gazarini, L., Stern, C. A. J., Piornedo, R. R., Takahashi, R. N., & Bertoglio, L. J.",2015,full_text,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": ""Gazarini, L., Stern, C. A. J., Piornedo, R. R., Takahashi, R. N., & Bertoglio, L. J."", ""year"": ""2015"", ""title"": ""PTSD-like memory generated through enhanced noradrenergic activity is mitigated by a dual step pharmacological intervention targeting its reconsolidation."", ""doi"": ""10.1093/ijnp/pyu026"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": ""NS12, NS20"", ""evidence_quote"": ""In two of our experiments, we attempted to pharmacologically enhance memory destabilization. NMDA receptor activation plays a crucial role in memory destabilization: pre-reactivation administration of the NMDA receptor antagonist Ifenprodil can prevent the impairing effect of post-reactivation anisomycin (Mamou, Gamache, & Nader, 2006); conversely, administration of the NMDA partial agonist D-cycloserine (DCS) before 16 the reactivation session allows destabilization of otherwise resistant contextual fear memories (Bustos et al., 2010; Espejo et al., 2016, 2017; Gazarini et al., 2015; Ortiz et al., 2015). We investigated whether DCS could boost destabilization and thereby enable memory interference with post-reactivation MDZ, but experiments NS12 and NS20 provided no evidence for an amnestic effect of MDZ when DCS was administered before the reactivation session."", ""outcome"": ""failed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""We investigated whether DCS could boost destabilization and thereby enable memory interference with post-reactivation MDZ, but experiments NS12 and NS20 provided no evidence for an amnestic effect of MDZ when DCS was administered before the reactivation session."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The authors explicitly contrast their own results with this original or the target set containing it.""}"
10.31234/osf.io/akz6b,834b55f482c6e8a1,13,10.1038/npp.2014.174,Reconsolidation allows fear memory to be updated to a less aversive level through the incorporation of appetitive information.,"Haubrich, J., Crestani, A. P., Cassini, L. F., Santana, F., Sierra, R. O., Alvares, L. de O., & Quillfeldt, J. A.",2015,full_text,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": ""Haubrich, J., Crestani, A. P., Cassini, L. F., Santana, F., Sierra, R. O., Alvares, L. de O., & Quillfeldt, J. A."", ""year"": ""2015"", ""title"": ""Reconsolidation allows fear memory to be updated to a less aversive level through the incorporation of appetitive information."", ""doi"": ""10.1038/npp.2014.174"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""To this aim, we set out to conceptually or directly replicate previous studies in which systemic, post-reactivation administration of midazolam (MDZ, see Table 1), propranolol (PROP, see Table 2), or cycloheximide (CYCLO; Haubrich et al., 2015) resulted in amnesia for contextual fear memories."", ""outcome"": ""failed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""We can conclude that our findings are in stark contrast with the existing literature on post-reactivation amnesia in rats, in which systemic administration of MDZ (Table 1), PROP (Table 2), or CYCLO (Haubrich et al., 2015) has been shown to induce amnesia for contextual fear memories or other types of memories (Briggs & Olson, 2013; Dębiec & Ledoux, 2004; Flint & Marino, 2007; Milton, Lee, & Everitt, 2008; Przybyslawski et al., 1999; Robinson & Franklin, 2010; Taubenfeld et al., 2009)."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The authors explicitly contrast their own results with this original or the target set containing it.""}"
