https://openalex.org/w96171599,85d1ad39a05d633c,1,10.1038/ng.664,A genome-wide association study for myopia and refractive error identifies a susceptibility locus at 15q25.,"Hysi PG, Young TL, Mackey DA, Andrew T, Fernandez-Medarde A, Solouki AM, Hewitt AW, Macgregor S, Vingerling JR, Li YJ, Ikram MK, Fai LY, Sham PC, Manyes L, Porteros A, Lopes MC, Carbonaro F, Fahy SJ, Martin NG, van Duijn CM, Spector TD, Rahi JS, Santos E, Klaver CC, Hammond CJ",2010,full_text,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": ""Hysi PG, Young TL, Mackey DA, Andrew T, Fernandez-Medarde A, Solouki AM, Hewitt AW, Macgregor S, Vingerling JR, Li YJ, Ikram MK, Fai LY, Sham PC, Manyes L, Porteros A, Lopes MC, Carbonaro F, Fahy SJ, Martin NG, van Duijn CM, Spector TD, Rahi JS, Santos E, Klaver CC, Hammond CJ"", ""year"": ""2010"", ""title"": ""A genome-wide association study for myopia and refractive error identifies a susceptibility locus at 15q25."", ""doi"": ""10.1038/ng.664"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""We used the Age-Related Eye Disease Study (AREDS) cohort to replicate published significant associations at two loci on chromosome 15 from two genome-wide association studies (GWASs)."", ""outcome"": ""mixed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""Although use of the same SNPs used in the Consortium for Refractive Error and Myopia study did not show any evidence of association with refractive error in this AREDS sample, other SNPs within the candidate regions demonstrated an association with refractive error. Significant evidence of association was found using the hyperopia categorical trait, with the most significant SNPs rs1357179 on 15q14 (p=1.69×10 −3 ) and rs7164400 on 15q25 (p=8.39×10 −4 ), which passed the replication thresholds."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""abstract"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The authors contrast non-replication of the original SNPs with regional replication for hyperopia at this locus.""}"
https://openalex.org/w2484584964,1439ac7adb504246,1,,Cues of being watched enhance cooperation in a real-world setting.,"Bateson M, Nettle D, Roberts G.",2006,full_text,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": ""Bateson M, Nettle D, Roberts G."", ""year"": ""2006"", ""title"": ""Cues of being watched enhance cooperation in a real-world setting."", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""Based on [21] and [22] we have chosen eyes as prosocial primes, and flowers as a neutral control prime."", ""outcome"": ""failed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""What we may conclude, therefore, is that the effect of eye primes on prosocial behavior does not generalize from the settings used by Haley and Fessler [21] and Bateson, Nettles, and Roberts [22]. Our failure to find an effect is informative about the actual overall effect size, and the conditions in which it occurs [32]."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The authors explicitly judge both studies’ eye-prime effect not to generalize to this new sample. They call the null result informative; their inability to rule out the phenomenon in all contexts is not a verdict that this particular test is uninformative.""}"
10.1111/jpr.12599,1515a66179d34cf7,1,10.1177/0022022103034004005,Boundaries of cultural influence: Construct activation as a mechanism for cultural differences in social perception.,"Hong, Y., Benet-Martinez, V., Chiu, C.-Y., & Morris, M. W.",2003,full_text,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": ""Hong, Y., Benet-Martinez, V., Chiu, C.-Y., & Morris, M. W."", ""year"": ""2003"", ""title"": ""Boundaries of cultural influence: Construct activation as a mechanism for cultural differences in social perception."", ""doi"": ""10.1177/0022022103034004005"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": ""4, 5, 6"", ""evidence_quote"": ""Therefore, in Experiment 4, we attempted a replication using the high salience image used by Hong et al. (2003) (see Online Appendix 2)."", ""outcome"": ""failed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""The six experiments and the mini meta-analysis consistently failed to replicate the cultural priming effect."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""abstract"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The overall verdict covers all six cultural priming attempts, including the attempts testing this original.""}"
10.36948/ijfmr.2025.v07i06.61995,c2ce02faa87b6213,0,,,,2013,full_text,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": """", ""year"": ""2013"", ""title"": """", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": """", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""This study conducted a direct replication of Dzulkifli and Mustafar (2013) in an Indian university population."", ""outcome"": ""failed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""This direct replication of Dzulkifli and Mustafar (2013) failed to detect significant effects of colour priming on memory span or sustained attention in a sample of 60 Indian university undergraduates. Warm colour (red) background did not enhance cognitive performance relative to cool colour (blue) or white control conditions."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Authors explicitly report failure to detect the two predicted effects. Low power is discussed, but authors explicitly say their null findings are informative. Target citation is unresolved because the sole Dzulkifli/Mustafar entry is a review, not the empirical primary study attributed in the prose.""}"
https://blogs.lse.ac.uk/impactofsocialsciences/2013/09/10/bias-at-the-bbc-replicating-the-cps-analysis/,ed4df17dff750947,0,,Bias at the Beeb: A quantitative study of slant in BBC online reporting,,,full_text,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": """", ""year"": """", ""title"": ""Bias at the Beeb: A quantitative study of slant in BBC online reporting"", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""In order to dig deeper into the results obtained by the CPS, I have tried to replicate this part of the\n   research. Of course, without access to the CPS dataset there will be some differences from their\n   results, but the methodology I have applied is designed to be transparent and robust in its own\n   right."", ""outcome"": ""statistically successful but flawed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""These numbers are all indicative, prior to any reliability testing, and, of course, they do not\n   realistically provide evidence of the existence or absence of bias in the BBC any more than the\n   CPS figures do. What they do represent is a fairly simple way to debunk the main conclusions\n   drawn by the CPS from their own analysis and then spread by an overly­credulous press."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The contextualisation effects were recovered, but the main argument is that those measures do not establish ideological bias; the report’s overall interpretation is undermined.""}"
https://osf.io/wgznk,ddeb7a5f2b2f89e8,0,,,,2017,full_text,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": """", ""year"": ""2017"", ""title"": """", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": """", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""Claim for Review – The claim selected was that strongly tied customers who complain (vs. withhold feedback) after a service failure will increase their loyalty. In contrast, for weakly tied customers, complaining has no effect on loyalty."", ""outcome"": ""failed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""Stage 2: H* was not supported as the interaction term on the overall ANOVA was not significant. In addition, the pattern of means in the strong ties group was not consistent but the pattern of means in the weak ties group was consistent."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""results"", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Both completed stages explicitly reject support for the focal interaction. A consistent weak-ties mean pattern is ancillary to the central failed test. The final report identifies the target by project shorthand, but supplies no reference entry.""}"
https://openalex.org/w1703767599,adb4867dd3755f27,1,,,Rothermund & Wentura,2004,abstract,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": ""Rothermund & Wentura"", ""year"": ""2004"", ""title"": """", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": ""1, 2"", ""evidence_quote"": ""However, the construct validity of this type of IAT has been called into question by recent findings showing that the IAT score implying in-group preference is rather driven by the salience asymmetry within each categorical dyad of the procedure than by evaluative associations between concepts (Rothermund & Wentura, 2001, 2004)."", ""outcome"": ""successful"", ""outcome_quote"": ""Consistent with our predictions, results showed that in both cases salience asymmetries reliably predict the IAT effect."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""abstract"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The abstract identifies both earlier salience-asymmetry findings and reports the corresponding predicted relationship in both new experiments. Bibliographic titles and original study numbers are absent from the abstract.""}"
https://openalex.org/w185729583,a4ea79c751bc0ee1,0,,,Eagle et. al.,1966,abstract,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": ""Eagle et. al."", ""year"": ""1966"", ""title"": """", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": ""1, 2, 3"", ""evidence_quote"": ""The goal of the following study is to replicate the crucial findings of Eagle et. al. (1966)."", ""outcome"": ""failed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""Three experiments were conducted attempting to replicate the original findings but were unable to do so."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""abstract"", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Abstract explicitly states all three experiments could not replicate the original findings.""}"
10.1525/collabra.163080,7bdaa130567dec72,1,10.1177/1948550611418535,Can uniform color color aggression? Quasi-experimental evidence from professional ice hockey,"Webster, G. D., Urland, G. R., & Correll, J.",2012,full_text,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": ""Webster, G. D., Urland, G. R., & Correll, J."", ""year"": ""2012"", ""title"": ""Can uniform color color aggression? Quasi-experimental evidence from professional ice hockey"", ""doi"": ""10.1177/1948550611418535"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": ""3"", ""evidence_quote"": ""We now extend our investigation to the modern NHL while attempting to address deficiencies in past research which supported the black uniforms-heightened aggression connection."", ""outcome"": ""failed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""Also, we failed to find support for reduced aggression when wearing white uniforms (Webster et al., 2012) as we conducted comparisons within teams across eight seasons while ensuring the overall aggression in the league remained consistent (thus avoiding a history threat)."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Study III directly addresses the modern NHL black-uniform and white-uniform findings, discusses methodological deficiencies in Webster, and reports failure for the white effect as well as the nested black-uniform analysis.""}"
https://hdl.handle.net/20.500.14721/40318,71aff154dfbcab0e,1,10.1016/j.jad.2021.05.047,How long do mood induction procedure (MIP) primes really last? Implications for cognitive vulnerability research.,"Gillies, J. C. P., & Dozois, D. J. A.",2021,full_text,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": ""Gillies, J. C. P., & Dozois, D. J. A."", ""year"": ""2021"", ""title"": ""How long do mood induction procedure (MIP) primes really last? Implications for cognitive vulnerability research."", ""doi"": ""10.1016/j.jad.2021.05.047"", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": ""1, 2"", ""evidence_quote"": ""The two dissertation studies aimed to test the reproducibility of findings from the original study (Gillies, 2018; Gillies & Dozois, 2021) by conducting an exact replication and extending the study design to address the three additional questions listed above."", ""outcome"": ""successful"", ""outcome_quote"": ""Statistical analyses of data from these studies revealed results that largely support findings from the original study. That is, the MIPs were often ineffective for a substantial percentage of participants and induced sadness diminished quickly, often within 6 min."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""fulltext"", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The lay summary gives an overall supportive verdict across both studies. The thesis and subsequent article report the same original sample, but the thesis uniquely reports the latent growth analyses; both are explicitly retested.""}"
https://hdl.handle.net/10419/280434,a00f5635a03e4c69,0,,The origin of the state: Land productivity or appropriability?,"Mayshar, J., Moav, O., and Pascali, L.",2022,full_text,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": ""Mayshar, J., Moav, O., and Pascali, L."", ""year"": ""2022"", ""title"": ""The origin of the state: Land productivity or appropriability?"", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""This is a replication of Mayshar et al. (2022) (henceforth MMP)."", ""outcome"": """", ""outcome_quote"": """", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""fulltext"", ""outcome_computation"": ""computationally reproducible"", ""outcome_computational_quote"": ""The computational reproduction of Mayshar et al. (2022) confirms that their data and code closely reproduce the main results presented in their Table 1."", ""outcome_robustness"": ""robustness challenges"", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": ""Our robustness replication with a range of plausible alternative specifications points to additional nuance and sometimes eroded support for the main results."", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Authors confirm close numerical reproduction, treating small discrepancies as not changing qualitative interpretation. Alternative specifications erode support and sometimes contradict central claims. Both axes concern original data; added climate controls do not constitute a fresh participant sample.""}"
https://datacolada.org/90,ad1a93f0ecabac93,0,,Product Entitativity: How the Presence of Product Replicates Increases Perceived and Actual Product Efficacy,,,full_text,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": """", ""year"": """", ""title"": ""Product Entitativity: How the Presence of Product Replicates Increases Perceived and Actual Product Efficacy"", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": ""Study 1"", ""replication_study_numbers"": ""1, 2, 3"", ""evidence_quote"": ""This JCR paper contains five studies. We chose to replicate Study 1 (N = 87) because it was the only study run on MTurk."", ""outcome"": ""failed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""As you can see, the original finding did not replicate. Participants did not judge Dettol to be significantly more effective after seeing an add that displayed five (vs. one) bottles of it."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""results"", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Explicit overall verdict covers the three replications of Study 1.""}"
https://datacolada.org/97,973b80ad76cf383f,0,,Goal Conflict Encourages Work and Discourages Leisure,,,full_text,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": """", ""year"": """", ""title"": ""Goal Conflict Encourages Work and Discourages Leisure"", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": ""1a"", ""replication_study_numbers"": ""1, 2"", ""evidence_quote"": ""We chose to replicate Study 1a (N = 210) because it represented a simple test of both of the authors’ hypotheses."", ""outcome"": ""failed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""On balance, then, these replications were not successful."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""results"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The authors give an explicit overall unsuccessful verdict, which governs despite directional evidence for the leisure effect and the original authors’ more favorable feedback.""}"
https://datacolada.org/83,961e6cb7f45dd3fb,0,,"Wine for the Table: Self-Construal, Group Size, and Choice for Self and Others",,,full_text,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": """", ""year"": """", ""title"": ""Wine for the Table: Self-Construal, Group Size, and Choice for Self and Others"", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": ""3"", ""replication_study_numbers"": ""1, 2"", ""evidence_quote"": ""The JCR paper contains six studies, all of which were conducted on MTurk. We chose to try to replicate Study 3 because it seemed to have the best statistical evidence, as it was supported by the smallest p-value in the paper: t(239) = 2.20, p = .03"", ""outcome"": ""mixed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""Let us first consider whether the original result was significant in the more highly powered replication attempts. In this case, the results are mixed. In the first replication attempt (with the typo), the three-way interaction among prime, group size, and preference for nonfiction was significant and in the same direction as in the original study: b = .162, SE = .070, p = .022. But in the second replication attempt (with no typo), that same interaction did not approach significance, though it was still in the same direction: b = .050, SE = .064, p = .432"", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""results"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The authors explicitly call the two attempts mixed and describe significant agreement in the first and failure in the second; their summary also notes inconsistency with the hypothesized pattern. The original authors’ feedback is not the replicators’ verdict.""}"
https://datacolada.org/87,a391e316831961a9,0,,The Influence of Product Anthropomorphism on Comparative Choice,,,full_text,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": """", ""year"": """", ""title"": ""The Influence of Product Anthropomorphism on Comparative Choice"", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": ""2"", ""replication_study_numbers"": ""1, 2"", ""evidence_quote"": ""In the fifth installment of\nData Replicada\n, we report our attempt to replicate a recently published\nJournal of Consumer Research\n(JCR) article entitled, “The Influence of Product Anthropomorphism on Comparative Choice” (\n.html\n)."", ""outcome"": ""failed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""In sum, using 5.5 times the sample size as the original study, we do not find significant evidence that adorning laptops with human-like features makes consumers more likely to choose the one that has more total stars (instead of the one that is better on more dimensions)."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The replication authors explicitly conclude that their two larger replications do not find significant evidence for the original central effect. The original authors’ feedback is not the replication authors’ verdict. Target author names and year are not printed in the post and are left blank.""}"
https://datacolada.org/89,e0029361b6b9eefc,0,,The Impact of Resource Scarcity on Price-Quality Judgments,,,full_text,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": """", ""year"": """", ""title"": ""The Impact of Resource Scarcity on Price-Quality Judgments"", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": ""Study 2A"", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""In this sixth installment of Data Replicada, we report our attempt to replicate a recently published Journal of Consumer Research (JCR) article entitled, “The Impact of Resource Scarcity on Price-Quality Judgments” ( .html )."", ""outcome"": ""uninformative"", ""outcome_quote"": ""On the one hand, we were successful at dramatically reducing attrition. On the other hand, we were unsuccessful at reducing differential attrition, as once again many more participants dropped out of the Scarcity condition than the Control condition. So then we gave up, and decided to call the whole thing “inconclusive.”"", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""fulltext"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Authors explicitly call the entire attempt inconclusive because differential attrition compromised random assignment and valid comparison, despite initial significant effects. Aggregate includes the additional attempts discussed in footnote 7.""}"
https://datacolada.org/82,047b58e00efe7e6c,0,,Having Control Over and Above Situations: The Influence of Elevated Viewpoints on Risk Taking,,,full_text,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": """", ""year"": """", ""title"": ""Having Control Over and Above Situations: The Influence of Elevated Viewpoints on Risk Taking"", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": ""Study 1b"", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""In this post, we report our attempt to replicate a study in a recently published\nJournal of Marketing Research\n(\nJMR\n) article entitled, “Having Control Over and Above Situations: The Influence of Elevated Viewpoints on Risk Taking” (\n.htm\n)."", ""outcome"": ""failed"", ""outcome_quote"": ""In sum, using three times the sample size as the original study, we find no evidence that being exposed to an elevated vertical position increases risk taking, at least when risk is operationalized as the likelihood of purchasing new products."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Explicit overall conclusion of no evidence for target effect. Blog links the original but gives no reference-list author/year or printed DOI; these fields remain empty.""}"
https://datacolada.org/94,cd37df8161b7704f,0,,Advertising a Desired Change: When Process Simulation Fosters (vs. Hinders) Credibility and Persuasion,,,full_text,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": """", ""year"": """", ""title"": ""Advertising a Desired Change: When Process Simulation Fosters (vs. Hinders) Credibility and Persuasion"", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": ""Study 2"", ""replication_study_numbers"": ""1, 2"", ""evidence_quote"": ""We chose to replicate Study 2 (N = 213) because (1) it, along with Study 3, represented the simplest test of the authors’ hypothesis, and (2) it contained stronger statistical evidence (ps  < .01) than Study 3 (p = .02)."", ""outcome"": ""successful"", ""outcome_quote"": ""In sum, this was an unambiguous success. We of course can’t speak to all of the studies in this paper, but we can confidently conclude that people judge this progression weight loss ad to be more credible than these Before/After ads."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""Explicit overall verdict covers both new-sample replications of Study 2.""}"
https://datacolada.org/92,fc5081ab3a9c538f,0,,The Left-Digit Bias: When and Why Are Consumers Penny Wise and Pound Foolish?,,,full_text,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": """", ""year"": """", ""title"": ""The Left-Digit Bias: When and Why Are Consumers Penny Wise and Pound Foolish?"", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": ""1"", ""replication_study_numbers"": ""1, 2"", ""evidence_quote"": ""We chose to replicate Study 1 (N = 145) because it represented the simplest test of the authors’ hypothesis, and because its effect size was among the largest in the paper"", ""outcome"": ""successful"", ""outcome_quote"": ""In sum, we think the totality of the evidence suggests that the left-digit bias is larger when the products/prices are presented on the same screen than when they are presented sequentially. At the same time, our evidence suggests that the effect is much smaller than the original authors reported, and that thousands of participants may be required to reliably produce it."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""discussion"", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The overall conclusion supports the original interaction despite attenuation. The article names the target title but supplies no bibliographic reference with its authors or year.""}"
https://osf.io/9hjxc,49d19f8bda738911,0,,,,2014,full_text,,unverified,unresolved,{},"{""authors"": """", ""year"": ""2014"", ""title"": """", ""doi"": """", ""identified_by"": ""citation"", ""study_numbers"": """", ""replication_study_numbers"": """", ""evidence_quote"": ""Key Inference - For the purposes of SCORE, to test H* and attempt to replicate the H* statistical evidence (if relevant), we will run t test with condition as the independent variable and support as the dependent variable."", ""outcome"": ""successful"", ""outcome_quote"": ""Stage 2: H* was supported. The pattern of means was directionally consistent with the pattern in the paper. The results are as follows [t(356)=3.434, p < .001, Cohen’s d = .363]. There were no deviations from the preregistration."", ""outcome_quote_section"": ""results"", ""outcome_computation"": """", ""outcome_computational_quote"": """", ""outcome_robustness"": """", ""outcome_robustness_quote"": """", ""outcome_confident"": true, ""outcome_reasoning"": ""The registered two-stage sampling rule pools additional participants when Stage 1 fails; Stage 2 is the final supported focal test. These are sequential sampling stages of one test, not conflicting independent replication studies.""}"
